AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0026"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0028"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0029"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0030"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0031"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0032"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0033"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">AVAILABLE_AFTER_EXACT_HYPOTHESIS_AND_STATEMENT_MATCH</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0035"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0036"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0037"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0038"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0041"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0042"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0043"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0044"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="BR-LAW-0045"><data key="k0">typed_bridge</data><data key="k1">VALID_AFTER_ADDED_HYPOTHESES</data></node><node id="CG01"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Domain founding and status ladder</data></node><node id="CG02"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Admission kinds and lawful domain transition</data></node><node id="CG03"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Bootstrap, seeding, and no-free access</data></node><node id="CG04"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Prospective commitment and temporal admissibility</data></node><node id="CG05"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Partial access and non-transfer from common origin</data></node><node id="CG06"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Join entry and record normal form</data></node><node id="CG07"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Join existence, obstruction, and certified non-interaction</data></node><node id="CG08"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Strict joint novelty and anti-manufacture</data></node><node id="CG09"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Source independence and native parent evidence</data></node><node id="CG10"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Join currency, payment, and resource bounds</data></node><node id="CG11"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Contact modes, peer transport, and interaction without join</data></node><node id="CG12"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Categorical reduction of join</data></node><node id="CG13"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Parent refinement, retention, and descent through join</data></node><node id="CG14"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Enablement definition, attribution, and endogeny</data></node><node id="CG15"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Birth classes, contact-surface birth, and reachability</data></node><node id="CG16"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Enablement-chain composition</data></node><node id="CG17"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Upward/downward transmission and descent fidelity</data></node><node id="CG18"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Confluence, construction routes, and seed dependence</data></node><node id="CG19"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Join order, associativity, and interaction holonomy</data></node><node id="CG20"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Interaction arrow, consistency, irreversibility, and simultaneity</data></node><node id="CG21"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Exposure, recoverability, admissibility, and rigidity</data></node><node id="CG22"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Needles, residuals, and obstruction dissolution</data></node><node id="CG23"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Observer and instrument occupancy</data></node><node id="CG24"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Bridge, transport, and withdrawal discipline</data></node><node id="CG25"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Reachability, non-vacuity, horizon, and guard activity</data></node><node id="CG26"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Negative-result quantifiers</data></node><node id="CG27"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Claim grades, specification discipline, and non-goals</data></node><node id="CG28"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Detector, null, falsifier, and Two-Theory World</data></node><node id="CG29"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Primitive generators and relations</data></node><node id="CG30"><data key="k0">convergence_group</data><data key="k1">Conserved contact degree</data></node><node id="CLD-A1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Give necessary and sufficient join-existence conditions with free/constrained/no-join cases.</data></node><node id="CLD-A2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Prove or refute reduction of the join to a categorical product/pullback under explicit hypotheses.</data></node><node id="CLD-A3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Identify join currency, cross-costs, and possible refunds/economies.</data></node><node id="CLD-A4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine when a join of strict extensions is strict over one or both parents.</data></node><node id="CLD-A5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Analyze associativity and bracketing dependence of joins as interaction holonomy.</data></node><node id="CLD-B1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Characterize confluence of source refinement and target coarsening at whole-construction scale.</data></node><node id="CLD-B2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Find invariants detecting layers reachable only by one construction direction.</data></node><node id="CLD-B3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">State when a closure fixed point is independent of the initial partition and when it is not.</data></node><node id="CLD-C1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Exhibit enablement without factorization or descent.</data></node><node id="CLD-C2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Lift the laboratory enablement-time assay to a scoped layer-agnostic law with no-birth control.</data></node><node id="CLD-C3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Show that an enabler can be necessary while remaining insufficient to determine the enabled layer.</data></node><node id="CLD-C4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine composition conditions and accumulated budget, residual, or holonomy along enablement chains.</data></node><node id="CLD-D1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test whether equal determination with different exposure is behaviorally inert exactly when recoverable from auxiliary structure.</data></node><node id="CLD-D2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define exposure overread when a declared distinction is manufactured by an operation the substrate does not perform.</data></node><node id="CLD-D3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine when a well-formed combination of lawful operations is itself a lawful substrate operation.</data></node><node id="CLD-D4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Classify packages with no nontrivial admissible coarse-graining and distinguish genuine rigidity from overconstraint or wrong interface.</data></node><node id="CLD-DISC"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Use explicit claim grades, countermodel atlas, solved calibrations, nonclaims, mechanization ledger, and no silent universality.</data></node><node id="CLD-E1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">No audited join without a nontrivial payment beyond mere co-presentation.</data></node><node id="CLD-E2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">A join cannot create distinctions absent from parents plus declared bridge.</data></node><node id="CLD-E3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Construct lawful theories with no admissible common joint package.</data></node><node id="CLD-E4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Use order-dependent composition to test absence of a global theory potential.</data></node><node id="CLD-F"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Either develop generators/relations for primitive operations or explicitly defer until categorical apparatus exists.</data></node><node id="CLD-O1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define determination, exposure, and recoverability as separate interface coordinates.</data></node><node id="CLD-O2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define a join relative to an admissibility regime, with compatibility, existence, and cost.</data></node><node id="CLD-O3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define enablement as distinct from descent, containment, and fixed-layer causation.</data></node><node id="CLD-O4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Record top-down refinement versus bottom-up assembly and test whether they reach the same fixed point.</data></node><node id="CM-01"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Same source but no shared access</data></node><node id="CM-02"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Common interface without witnessed contact</data></node><node id="CM-03"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Contact without strict joint information</data></node><node id="CM-04"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Scheduling-only apparent joint novelty</data></node><node id="CM-05"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Join that loses one child</data></node><node id="CM-06"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Enablement without descent</data></node><node id="CM-07"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Enablement necessary but insufficient</data></node><node id="CM-08"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Sound but unreachable admission rule</data></node><node id="CM-09"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Reachable admission that never occurs</data></node><node id="CM-10"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Holonomy with zero arrow</data></node><node id="CM-11"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Observer occupancy omitted from budget</data></node><node id="CM-12"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Total-lens result fails on partial self-owned access</data></node><node id="CM-13"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Product/common refinement without strictness</data></node><node id="CM-14"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Relabeling-only fake join</data></node><node id="CM-15"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Strict extension without certified objecthood</data></node><node id="CM-16"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Strict join without directionality</data></node><node id="CM-17"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Theorist-triggered enablement is not endogenous</data></node><node id="CM-18"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Finite negative search with open family</data></node><node id="CM-19"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Shared carrier without lawful peer transport</data></node><node id="CM-20"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Post-hoc prediction self-certification</data></node><node id="CM-21"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Zero-cost join hides positive observer cost</data></node><node id="CM-22"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Parent refinement destroys an admissible join</data></node><node id="CM-23"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Seed-dependent closure fixed points</data></node><node id="CM-24"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Identical members assembled in different orders</data></node><node id="CM-25"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Join manufactures a cross-term needle</data></node><node id="CM-26"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">One-lineage pseudo-join</data></node><node id="CM-27"><data key="k0">countermodel</data><data key="k1">Lawful parents with no common admissible package</data></node><node id="Ch01"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Scope, inheritance, grades, and bridge contract</data></node><node id="Ch02"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Partial domains and access coordinates</data></node><node id="Ch03"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Admission, bootstrap, and prospective commitment</data></node><node id="Ch04"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Contact surfaces, interaction records, and peer transport</data></node><node id="Ch05"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Join status, strictness, and parent retention</data></node><node id="Ch06"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Source, budget, observer, and no-free-join</data></node><node id="Ch07"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Enablement, birth, transmission, and descent fidelity</data></node><node id="Ch08"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Reachability and negative-result force</data></node><node id="Ch09"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Confluence, order, holonomy, and arrow</data></node><node id="Ch10"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Residuals, needles, and obstruction dissolution</data></node><node id="Ch11"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Two-Theory World and countermodel battery</data></node><node id="Ch12"><data key="k0">chapter</data><data key="k1">Formalization targets and explicit deferrals</data></node><node id="DP01"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Is contact surface primitive or reducible to inherited interface mediation?</data></node><node id="DP02"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Are join statuses complete?</data></node><node id="DP03"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Is source independence required for every join?</data></node><node id="DP04"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Can categorical products or pullbacks represent the join?</data></node><node id="DP05"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Is there one scalar join currency?</data></node><node id="DP06"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Is there a conserved contact degree, including observer/instrument occupancy?</data></node><node id="DP07"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">How is simultaneous contact defined for incommensurable internal times?</data></node><node id="DP08"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">When is enablement endogenous?</data></node><node id="DP09"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Can contact create new participants or only a relation?</data></node><node id="DP10"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Does parent refinement preserve joins?</data></node><node id="DP11"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Does interaction holonomy imply an arrow?</data></node><node id="DP12"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Should primitive generators and relations be developed in VII?</data></node><node id="DP13"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Which P040/P058 member is canonical?</data></node><node id="DP14"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">Can P039 contribute body-level results?</data></node><node id="DP15"><data key="k0">decision</data><data key="k1">How strong may certified non-interaction be?</data></node><node id="E1"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E1</data></node><node id="E10"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E10</data></node><node id="E11"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E11</data></node><node id="E12"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E12</data></node><node id="E13"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E13</data></node><node id="E14"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E14</data></node><node id="E15"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E15</data></node><node id="E16"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E16</data></node><node id="E2"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E2</data></node><node id="E3"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E3</data></node><node id="E4"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E4</data></node><node id="E5"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E5</data></node><node id="E6"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E6</data></node><node id="E8"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E8</data></node><node id="E9"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">E9</data></node><node id="F10"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F10</data></node><node id="F11"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F11</data></node><node id="F12"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F12</data></node><node id="F13a"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F13a</data></node><node id="F13b"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F13b</data></node><node id="F14"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F14</data></node><node id="F15a"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F15a</data></node><node id="F15b"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F15b</data></node><node id="F17"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F17</data></node><node id="F18"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F18</data></node><node id="F19"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F19</data></node><node id="F2"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F2</data></node><node id="F20"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F20</data></node><node id="F21"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F21</data></node><node id="F22"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F22</data></node><node id="F24"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F24</data></node><node id="F25"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F25</data></node><node id="F26"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F26</data></node><node id="F27"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F27</data></node><node id="F29"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F29</data></node><node id="F3"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F3</data></node><node id="F31"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F31</data></node><node id="F33"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F33</data></node><node id="F34"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F34</data></node><node id="F36"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F36</data></node><node id="F37"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F37</data></node><node id="F4"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F4</data></node><node id="F40"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F40</data></node><node id="F42"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F42</data></node><node id="F45"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F45</data></node><node id="F46"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F46</data></node><node id="F47"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F47</data></node><node id="F48"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F48</data></node><node id="F49"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F49</data></node><node id="F50"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F50</data></node><node id="F51"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F51</data></node><node id="F52"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F52</data></node><node id="F6"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F6</data></node><node id="F7"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F7</data></node><node id="F8"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F8</data></node><node id="F9"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">F9</data></node><node id="FT01"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Finite access-status data model</data></node><node id="FT02"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Admission reachability graph and occurrence separation</data></node><node id="FT03"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Bootstrap obstruction</data></node><node id="FT04"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Prospective commitment timestamp/budget record</data></node><node id="FT05"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Source and bridge ledger</data></node><node id="FT06"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Typed contact witness</data></node><node id="FT07"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Join certificate normal form</data></node><node id="FT08"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Anti-product/nonfactorization witness</data></node><node id="FT09"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Join obstruction and budget record</data></node><node id="FT10"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Coverage-qualified non-interaction certificate</data></node><node id="FT11"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Enablement record</data></node><node id="FT12"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Endogenous generator criterion</data></node><node id="FT13"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Transmission/descent fidelity</data></node><node id="FT14"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Confluence and critical-pair finite models</data></node><node id="FT15"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Interaction holonomy/arrow separation</data></node><node id="FT16"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Budget and observer occupancy</data></node><node id="FT17"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Negative quantifier and claim-grade rules</data></node><node id="FT18"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">No-free-access/no-free-join lemmas</data></node><node id="FT19"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Parent retention and refinement transport</data></node><node id="FT20"><data key="k0">formal_target</data><data key="k1">Residual/needle and finite-world semantics</data></node><node id="G1"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G1</data></node><node id="G10"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G10</data></node><node id="G11"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G11</data></node><node id="G12"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G12</data></node><node id="G13"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G13</data></node><node id="G2"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G2</data></node><node id="G3"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G3</data></node><node id="G5"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G5</data></node><node id="G6"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G6</data></node><node id="G7"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G7</data></node><node id="G8"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G8</data></node><node id="G9"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">G9</data></node><node id="HolonomyMemory.LoopAsymmetry"><data key="k0">prior_lean_declaration</data><data key="k1">HolonomyMemory.LoopAsymmetry</data></node><node id="HolonomyMemory.loopAsymmetry_exhibits_movedPredictive_fixedCurrent"><data key="k0">prior_lean_declaration</data><data key="k1">HolonomyMemory.loopAsymmetry_exhibits_movedPredictive_fixedCurrent</data></node><node id="MED-B1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Distinguish undergoing closure from performing closure and test whether birth occurs at contact surfaces.</data></node><node id="MED-B2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">State finite expressivity requirements for a new layer to be reachable, including ability to alter interaction structure where needed.</data></node><node id="MED-B3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Explicitly separate observer-certified extension from endogenous enablement.</data></node><node id="MED-CIRC"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Formulate consistency/fixed-point conditions for mutually constraining layers without self-certification.</data></node><node id="MED-D1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define a typed interaction record with theory packages, contact surface, and witnessed contact.</data></node><node id="MED-D2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Distinguish coincidence, common refinement, strict join, obstructed join, and fake join.</data></node><node id="MED-D3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Classify rendezvous, sequential transport, shared carrier, mediation, and shared-budget contact.</data></node><node id="MED-D4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Type upward and downward transmission rather than using rhetorical arrows.</data></node><node id="MED-D5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Attach host, instrument, source, audit, and nonclaim records to every interaction claim.</data></node><node id="MED-H1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Measure non-associativity/order residue of multi-theory interaction.</data></node><node id="MED-H2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine whether identical members assembled in different orders yield the same theory.</data></node><node id="MED-H3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Require null-versus-driven controls for every inter-theory holonomy or arrow claim.</data></node><node id="MED-H4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test irreversibility of joining beyond entropy production internal to either parent.</data></node><node id="MED-H5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define simultaneous contact for theories with potentially incommensurable internal times.</data></node><node id="MED-I1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Attach detector, null, and falsifier obligations to every interaction law.</data></node><node id="MED-I2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Specify append-only records sufficient to certify a join after the fact.</data></node><node id="MED-I3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Make source independence, visibility, and capacity preconditions first-class detector obligations.</data></node><node id="MED-I4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Require same-source, no-contact, scheduling, relabeling, and coincidence nulls.</data></node><node id="MED-I5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Represent contact and joins that have begun but have not completed.</data></node><node id="MED-M1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Require source-object/target-object maps before transporting a named theorem.</data></node><node id="MED-M2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Maintain first-class records of failed bridges and retracted interpretations.</data></node><node id="MED-M3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Format each design requirement separately from its imported rationale.</data></node><node id="MED-N1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Classify obstruction dissolution by ascent, peer join, refinement, or terminality.</data></node><node id="MED-N2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Characterize obstructions removable by a peer join without ascent.</data></node><node id="MED-N3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test whether joining can manufacture new localized obstructions.</data></node><node id="MED-N4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine additive versus cross-term residual accounting across joins.</data></node><node id="MED-NG1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Relabeling, coarsening, or presentation change cannot manufacture joint predictive content.</data></node><node id="MED-NG2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Common-source re-expression cannot establish an independence-sensitive strict join without an additional independence witness.</data></node><node id="MED-NG3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">A higher layer may select/constrain/reinterpret lower possibilities but cannot create lower facts absent from the lower carrier.</data></node><node id="MED-NG4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Budgeted layers cannot have evidenced contact at zero total cost unless an explicit zero-cost channel exists.</data></node><node id="MED-NG5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Bound simultaneously live joins in terms of finite carrier/interface resources.</data></node><node id="MED-S1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Treat source independence as a necessary gate for independence-sensitive joins, not a sufficient causal-independence proof.</data></node><node id="MED-S2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Use asymmetric error costs and conservative defaults for first-positive join claims.</data></node><node id="MED-S3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Label every interaction condition necessary, sufficient, both, or neither.</data></node><node id="MED-S4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Publish substrate expressivity requirements for each interaction type before interpreting nulls.</data></node><node id="MED-S5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Tie negative-result force to search-family closure and quantifiers.</data></node><node id="MED-S6"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Validate that safety/independence gates are active and not vacuous.</data></node><node id="MED-T1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Construct two theories whose witnessed interaction carries content absent from parents and common refinement.</data></node><node id="MED-T2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Show whether refining a parent can destroy an admissible join.</data></node><node id="MED-T3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Classify vocabulary, budget, record-closure, gluing, and temporal obstructions, with completeness only if proved.</data></node><node id="MED-T4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Separate sequential composition of interactions from interaction of composite theories.</data></node><node id="MED-T5"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Identify conserved quantities or contact degrees across joining, if any.</data></node><node id="MED-T6"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine what laws/records descend through a join and what is necessarily lost.</data></node><node id="MED-T7"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test whether join failure is generically non-attributable to either parent.</data></node><node id="MED-UP"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test an asymmetry: obstructions/invariants rise by necessity while downward influence selects among already afforded options.</data></node><node id="MM-BOOT"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Classify self-bootstrapping capabilities and minimum seed sets for non-self-bootstrapping capabilities.</data></node><node id="MM-BRIDGE"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Turn lawful cross-paper transport into a reflexive rule governing VII's own citations.</data></node><node id="MM-HORIZON"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Specify what finite-horizon non-occurrence implies about reachability and capability.</data></node><node id="MM-INDEP"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Separate ancestry/source independence from behavioral nonfactorization; require join claims to name which one they establish.</data></node><node id="MM-JOINNF"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Require parent tuple, ancestry evidence, licensing control, obstruction semantics, and explicit failure meaning.</data></node><node id="MM-LOCAL"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Require destination ownership and contact-before-connection for local interface growth, with countermodels when dropped.</data></node><node id="MM-NG1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">A join mechanism cannot create the first capability needed to execute itself without an admitted seed.</data></node><node id="MM-NG2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Shared ancestry blocks independence-sensitive join claims regardless of behavioral novelty.</data></node><node id="MM-NG3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">An observer using bounded shared capacity cannot certify native formation while omitting its occupancy.</data></node><node id="MM-NG4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">One negative point cannot refute an existential capability claim; exhaustive closed-family negatives have different force.</data></node><node id="MM-NONGOAL"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Keep reachability/occurrence, interaction/correspondence, observer/world, and reachability/derivation separations explicit.</data></node><node id="MM-OBSERVER"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Account for instrument consumption of bounded shared resources before claiming native formation.</data></node><node id="MM-OP1"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Name the shadow price of confluence and whether it is dual to a resource constraint.</data></node><node id="MM-OP2"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Test whether A-then-B versus B-then-A produces a join obstruction.</data></node><node id="MM-OP3"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine whether rendezvous is minimal or one of several irreducible physical join primitives.</data></node><node id="MM-OP4"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Determine whether join birth can create participants and how it relates to layer birth.</data></node><node id="MM-SEED"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Separate neutral stocking from outcome-rigging using symmetry, task-blindness, preregistration, and carried control coordinates.</data></node><node id="MM-STATUS"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Define a four-stage status ladder: expressible, present/provisioned, reachable by lawful runtime operations, and actually occurrent.</data></node><node id="MM-WITHDRAW"><data key="k0">wishlist_atom</data><data key="k1">Maintain an append-only semantic withdrawal register, including paraphrase sweeps.</data></node><node id="NGVII-01"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No first extension without seed or reachable generator</data></node><node id="NGVII-02"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No resemblance-only independence-sensitive join</data></node><node id="NGVII-03"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No retrospective self-certification</data></node><node id="NGVII-04"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No automatic total-lens transfer</data></node><node id="NGVII-05"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No unpriced observer-native formation credit</data></node><node id="NGVII-06"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No join from contact alone</data></node><node id="NGVII-07"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No product/common-refinement strictness credit</data></node><node id="NGVII-08"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No one-lineage source-independence credit</data></node><node id="NGVII-09"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No positive-cost join credit without payment or certified zero-cost channel</data></node><node id="NGVII-10"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No arrow from holonomy alone</data></node><node id="NGVII-11"><data key="k0">candidate_no_go</data><data key="k1">No occurrence from reachability alone</data></node><node id="NG_ARROW_DPI"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_ARROW_DPI</data></node><node id="NG_FORCE_FOREST"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_FORCE_FOREST</data></node><node id="NG_FORCE_NULL"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_FORCE_NULL</data></node><node id="NG_LADDER_BOUNDED_INTERFACE"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_LADDER_BOUNDED_INTERFACE</data></node><node id="NG_LADDER_IDEM"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_LADDER_IDEM</data></node><node id="NG_MACRO_CLOSURE_DEFICIT"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_MACRO_CLOSURE_DEFICIT</data></node><node id="NG_OBJECT_CONTRACTIVE"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_OBJECT_CONTRACTIVE</data></node><node id="NG_PROTOCOL_TRAP"><data key="k0">prior_law_or_no_go</data><data key="k1">NG_PROTOCOL_TRAP</data></node><node id="P001-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Empirical cosmology infers late-time acceleration by fitting homogeneous FLRW models to data products that are, by construction, coarse-grained summaries of an inhomogeneous universe. This procedure makes it difficult to separate a fundamental component from a closure correction required by the coar</data></node><node id="P001-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope boundary.: This paper does not claim to refute a fundamental cosmological constant, nor does it propose a specific microphysical alternative. Our claim is methodological and testable: given a particular lens and completion, a nonzero effective acceleration term can arise as the closure correct</data></node><node id="P001-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Limitations: We close with explicit limitations, which also identify the highest-value next steps. Background-probe degeneracy: with current SN+BAO geometry alone, the best-fit rewrite family collapses to a constant-like correction ( m 0 ), making it difficult to distinguish a fundamental Lambda fro</data></node><node id="P002-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We present a reproducible computational framework—with Lean 4 mechanized proofs and deterministic Python simulations—that recasts quantum measurement, contextuality, and the measurement problem using operational semantics drawn from Six Birds Theory (SBT), a closure-based emergence calculus. Standar</data></node><node id="P002-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim to derive the Born rule or resolve Bell's theorem</data></node><node id="P002-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim to derive the Born rule or resolve Bell's theorem</data></node><node id="P002-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">To avoid overinterpretation, we emphasize what this paper does not claim.</data></node><node id="P003-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scoped event admissibility on fixed support leaves a specific gap for strict tests: the protocol-indexed flagship-seed bridge family realizes the quadratic-levelset and transition-stability slots, but the protocol-indexed readout-agreement slot remains unrealized, so no accepted strict test family i</data></node><node id="P003-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Inherited nonclaims: Paper 8's scoped event admissibility is bounded by an explicit nonclaim record , in the foundations claim-discipline sense of . It does not assert event-package construction, a proto-effect fragment, an effect algebra, a Born valuation, composition readiness, faithful public com</data></node><node id="P003-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Paper 9 uses the claim and nonclaim discipline of the foundations layer to separate three kinds of statements.</data></node><node id="P003-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Non-factorization certificate: Each exactified carrier carries a carrier-side non-factorization certificate: its native state space, transport law, and readout law do not factor through the bridge-native H2 description. Equivalently, the carrier cannot be reconstructed from old-layer data alone. Thi</data></node><node id="P003-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">New-layer readout slot gate: The readout slot gate consumes the source-backed threshold evidence of together with the PICA-bound source-policy contract fixed in . Its output is a strict-realization decision for the readout-agreement slot at the native H6 carrier level. This is a layer-local decision</data></node><node id="P003-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The companion quadratic-witness and transition-witness slots are treated in , and the all-slot closure test is deferred to .</data></node><node id="P003-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">All-slot gate and strict-domain closure: The all-slot gate consumes the three slot-realization results established in . It returns closure of the H6 strict three-slot domain only when the readout-agreement, quadratic-witness, and transition-witness slots are all strict-realized on the same exactifie</data></node><node id="P003-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">protocol-indexed accepted, conditional on Family on protocol-indexed higher-witness surface readout-agreement, quadratic-witness, transition-witness all five criteria pass new-layer protocol-indexed accepted, conditional on Family on completion-family surface readout-agreement, quadratic-witness, tr</data></node><node id="P003-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bounded scope: no event package, no shared-event identity: Strict-test acceptance certifies that, on each H6 carrier, the family criteria pass and the controls survive. It does not provide a packaging operator over the accepted families, an identification of shared events across families, or any com</data></node><node id="P003-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under the claim/nonclaim discipline of , those are additional structural moves, not consequences of test-family acceptance.</data></node><node id="P003-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Higher structures remain future work: The accepted strict-test families are partitions of the closed three-slot domain at each carrier. Building a proto-effect fragment would require identifying effect-shaped structure in those families that survives the foundations claim discipline; building an eff</data></node><node id="P003-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under , absence of those constructions is a nonclaim boundary, not an implied consequence of the accepted tests.</data></node><node id="P003-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Promotion and carrier exactification as distinct stages: The second schema states that promotion of a candidate object, in the sense of , is distinct from carrier exactification, in the sense of . A promoted candidate is not by itself an exactified carrier: carrier exactification requires already-pr</data></node><node id="P003-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict-domain closure licenses test-family reassembly: The fourth schema states that closure of the H6 strict three-slot domain at a carrier licenses strict-test-family reassembly at that carrier. Closure means that the readout-agreement, quadratic-witness, and transition-witness slots are all stric</data></node><node id="P003-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Old-layer real-provisional status blocks old-layer tests only: The fifth schema is a no-propagation statement. Retaining H2 at real-provisional non-factorization status blocks acceptance of strict test families at H2, but it does not block acceptance at H6 when the evidence is constructed at the new</data></node><node id="P003-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Unrealized required slot blocks new-layer test acceptance: The sixth schema is the corresponding new-layer blocking statement. If any required slot of the H6 strict three-slot domain is not strict-realized at a carrier, then the all-slot gate of fails for that carrier, and no accepted strict test fa</data></node><node id="P003-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Boundary: schema legality, not empirical existence: The schemas of this section establish schema legality and gate constraints: they record the conditions under which Paper 9's claim discipline is internally consistent, not what is empirically the case. They do not prove that the H6 carriers exist, </data></node><node id="P003-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Final verdict statement: Relative to the event-admissibility endpoint inherited from Paper 8 , Paper 9 establishes a strict extension on fixed support. More precisely, the PICA route described in , consisting here of promotion, exactification, and source-policy-admitted carrier construction, yields </data></node><node id="P003-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Decision logic: The final gate is an ordered case analysis over the upstream audits from . The principal positive case is reached when the native H6 strict domain closes, the strict test families on the new-layer carrier survive controls, and descent preserves old-layer compatibility without turning</data></node><node id="P003-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope, limitations, and future work: The main limitations are structural. The accepted strict test families do not constitute an event package, a proto-effect fragment, an effect algebra, a Born valuation, or a composition-ready structure. Those remain explicit nonclaims, for the reasons given in : </data></node><node id="P003-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lean theorem schemas: The schemas of are mirrored on the Lean side as typed entries in the Mathlib-style formalization convention used throughout the fixed-support series . Each manuscript schema has a corresponding typed-record entry, and together these entries give the Lean view of Paper 9's claim</data></node><node id="P003-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Schema legality and empirical content: The Lean development summarized in establishes schema legality and conditional proof structure; it does not establish empirical witness existence. This is the same boundary stated in : the appendix records the Lean-side mirror of the manuscript schemas, not a s</data></node><node id="P003-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete nonclaims ledger: This ledger collects the structures that Paper 9 explicitly does not assert, under the claim/nonclaim discipline of . This would assert a closed strict test-family domain at H2. It remains a nonclaim because the bridge-native readout-agreement slot is not strict-realized.</data></node><node id="P003-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the bridge-native readout-agreement slot is not strict-realized.</data></node><node id="P003-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because committed-state evidence is not admitted for that strict role under the inherited bridge-native policy of Paper 8.</data></node><node id="P003-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because descent records compatibility and forgetting, not identity of events across layers.</data></node><node id="P003-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the paper constructs accepted strict test families only, not an event package.</data></node><node id="P003-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the strict-test acceptance result does not supply the operations, identities, or laws required for an effect algebra.</data></node><node id="P003-C0051"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the paper proves strict-test acceptance and control survival, not a composition interface.</data></node><node id="P003-C0053"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the exactified carriers are protocol-indexed H6 carriers, not lawful public carriers.</data></node><node id="P003-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It remains a nonclaim because the paper's evidence is confined to the fixed-support and scoped carrier construction used in the audit.</data></node><node id="P004-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Given two probe families on the same finite-dimensional carrier, this paper asks what a dissolving family D measures beyond what a native family L can explain under an audit energy C . Under the standing legal-energy quotient and null-mode legality hypotheses, the adequacy residual _C(D L) is the Sc</data></node><node id="P004-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim exact adequacy generically, does not classify all probe-family pairs, and does not claim novelty for the classical operator-theoretic constructions it packages.</data></node><node id="P004-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim that every raw substrate sees every dissolving probe, nor that the residual vanishes generically Finally, the paper does not claim novelty for the classical constructions it uses, such as Schur complements, Moore–Penrose pseudoinverses, projection identities, and Douglas-sty</data></node><node id="P004-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit energy: Let E be a finite-dimensional real or complex Hilbert space. An audit energy on E is a self-adjoint positive-semidefinite operator C=C^* 0 on E . The audit cost of a carrier vector u∈ E is the quadratic form u^*Cu , and the kernel Ker C consists of the zero-audit-cost directions.</data></node><node id="P004-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Legal energy quotient: The legal energy quotient of (E,C) is the orthogonal complement E_C=(Ker C)^ ⊆ E, together with the restricted audit form C_0=C|_ E_C &gt;0 which is strictly positive on E_C . The Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse C^ , following , acts as C_0^ -1 on E_C and as zero on Ker C .</data></node><node id="P004-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Null-legal probe family: A linear map F: E→ Y is a null-legal probe family when it vanishes on the zero-audit-cost directions: F|_ Ker C =0. For such an F , its restriction to the legal subspace is F_0=F|_ E_C : E_C→ Y. The native and dissolving probe families used throughout the paper are taken to </data></node><node id="P004-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Minimum-spend duality: For z∈ Y , define Cost_C(z;F) = ∈f \ u,Cu :\ Fu=z \ . Then Cost_C(z;F) = z^*K_F^ z, z∈ Ran F_0=Ran K_F, +∈fty, z Ran F_0.</data></node><node id="P004-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Native, dissolving, and joint block currencies: Let L: E→ Y and D: E→ Z be null-legal probe families (in the sense of ), with restrictions L_0,D_0 . The native, dissolving, and cross currencies are K_ LL =L_0C_0^ -1 L_0^*, K_ DL =D_0C_0^ -1 L_0^*, K_ LD =L_0C_0^ -1 D_0^*, K_ DD =D_0C_0^ -1 D_0^*. He</data></node><node id="P004-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Adequacy residual: The adequacy residual of D relative to L is Xi_C(D L)=K_ DD -K_ DL K_ LL ^ K_ LD .</data></node><node id="P004-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Projection identity: Xi_C(D L)=T_D(I-P_L)T_D^* 0.</data></node><node id="P004-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Optimal residual identity: For every linear map A: Y→ Z , (D_0-AL_0)C_0^ -1 (D_0-AL_0)^* = Xi_C(D L) +(A-A_*)K_ LL (A-A_*)^*. Consequently, Xi_C(D L) is the Loewner-minimal residual currency among all attempts to factor D through L .</data></node><node id="P004-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Extended native family and conditional currencies: Let M: E→ W be an additional null-legal native probe family. The extended native family is L^+ = L M : E→ Y W, L^+u=(Lu,Mu), which is null-legal because L and M both vanish on Ker C . The conditional native currency of M given L is the Schur complem</data></node><node id="P004-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Chain rule for adequacy residuals: Xi_C(D L^+) = Xi_C(D L) - K_ DM L K_ MM L ^ K_ MD L . In particular, Xi_C(D L^+) Xi_C(D L).</data></node><node id="P004-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Same-family saturation: If M=BL for some linear map B: Y→ W , then Xi_C(D [L;M])=Xi_C(D L).</data></node><node id="P004-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Defective dissolving bridge: Decompose the target dissolving probe as D'_0 = D'_0 + R, D'_0 = BD_0U, and suppose the native bridge is exact and that the exact source probe D'_0 satisfies the hypotheses of : the bridge is reducing on the probe-image subspace, so that the transported-currency identiti</data></node><node id="P004-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Declared residual budget and adequacy defect: A declared residual budget on Z is a positive semidefinite operator Omega 0 . The corresponding adequacy defect of D relative to L and Omega is the Hermitian operator Delta_Xi=Xi_C(D L)-Omega, where Xi_C(D L) is the adequacy residual of . The budget hold</data></node><node id="P004-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Blind-spot witness: Xi_C(D L) Omega if and only if there exists z∈ Z such that z^* (Xi_C(D L)-Omega )z&gt;0. Moreover, the maximum additive violation is lambda_ (Delta_Xi) , the largest eigenvalue of the adequacy defect.</data></node><node id="P004-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim or gating narrows the scope of the claim by excluding the offending response direction instead of proving adequacy for it.</data></node><node id="P004-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim novelty for those operator-theoretic ingredients.</data></node><node id="P004-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not assert that every raw substrate sees every dissolving probe, and it does not assert that Xi_C(D L)=0 generically Finally, it does not claim novelty for Schur complements, pseudoinverses, projection identities, or Douglas-style factorization.</data></node><node id="P004-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not classify all possible probe-family pairs (L,D) , does not claim exact adequacy generically, does not claim novelty for the classical operator-theoretic constructions it uses, and does not depend on any particular application instance.</data></node><node id="P004-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the full identity proof is paper-side. thm:xi:exact-adequacy partial needs-strengthening Lean proves the easy implication chain and records hard directions as hypotheses. thm:xi:adequacy-promotion partial narrowed-surrogate Lean proves promotion conditional on the upstream adequacy-interface algebra</data></node><node id="P005-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">extension beyond the carrier is a conditional schema, not a generic theorem.</data></node><node id="P005-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Problem, result classes, and contribution map: Formal theories of consciousness are difficult to compare, and difficult to trust, partly because three different kinds of claim routinely travel in one sentence: mathematical claims about a formal structure, empirical claims about how that structure me</data></node><node id="P005-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Every scientific sentence in the paper is assigned to one of four claim lanes, defined in Section : structural results [S] (theorems and exact finite constructions), recognition bridges [R] (proposed interpretive mappings, never derivations), ontological implications [O] (conditional on named postul</data></node><node id="P005-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Selected results are additionally mechanized: each of the nineteen law modules has a Lean kernel, and the exact scope boundary of each kernel (what Lean proves versus what it pins as certified constants matching the Python enumeration) is stated where the result is used and cataloged in Appendix . `</data></node><node id="P005-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">no derivation ``proposes,'' ``recognizes,'' ``interprets, conditional on'' treating the mapping as derived or measured [O] conditional ontological implication named postulates plus a mechanized conditional theorem ``implies after assuming,'' ``is conditional on,'' ``purchases'' detaching the conclus</data></node><node id="P005-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Quotient, refinement, factorization defect: A partition Pi' refines Pi when every block of Pi' is contained in a block of Pi . Given two lenses pi_0, pi_1 on the same carrier, the factorization defect is Delta_ fact (pi_0,pi_1) = \ (s,s') : pi_0(s)=pi_0(s') and pi_1(s)≠pi_1(s')\ , the set of split p</data></node><node id="P005-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive and observational equivalence: Fix a carrier with declared dynamics and a lens q . Two states (or histories) are observationally equivalent when all admissible current observables agree on them; the induced quotient is the current public quotient Q . They are predictively equivalent when </data></node><node id="P005-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure operator and audit: A closure operator on a finite structured set is an extensive, monotone, idempotent endomap; its fixed points are the descriptions the structure recognizes as complete. When a closure-like operator E is only approximately idempotent, its idempotence defect delta(E)= 12 _z</data></node><node id="P005-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Full C-package target: The full C-package C is the eight-clause full-design and audit contract C1 – C8 , together with cross-clause coherence data and a full promotion-gate panel. A candidate consists of a carrier X , declared transition/action families, a public lens and exposure channels, a predic</data></node><node id="P005-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[t] p 0.06 p 0.2 p 0.24 p 0.22 p 0.18 →prule Clause Standard object Strongest landed evidence Simplified (T17-S) proxy Blocking condition / nonclaim C1 closure operators E_tau with fixed-point, defect, and perturbation audits T1: exact E_tau audit on the ten-state kernel</data></node><node id="P005-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not prove any universal completeness of the six roles.</data></node><node id="P005-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">T1-L1, the self-wheel: The carrier is Z = P × W with |P|=6 public positions and |W|=2 hidden phases, so N = |Z| = 12 . The public self-lens is q(p,w)=p : the system can observe its own position but not its phase. The dynamics contain a live, unobserved phase slip with exact branch weights 3/4 and 1/</data></node><node id="P005-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The theorem: [Exact forcing and minimal restoration; T1] [S] Grade: theorem-grade on the exact T1-L1 carrier; conditional family schema beyond it. Scope: the declared twelve-state deadline carrier, its action family, and its lens. On this carrier: Public exhaustion. Every q -measurable feedback poli</data></node><node id="P005-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact forcing and minimal restoration; T1: [S] Grade: theorem-grade on the exact T1-L1 carrier; conditional family schema beyond it. Scope: the declared twelve-state deadline carrier, its action family, and its lens. On this carrier: Public exhaustion. Every q -measurable feedback policy has an empt</data></node><node id="P005-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact carrier versus family schema: What generalizes, and at what price, is stated once and inherited everywhere. For substrates satisfying the published deadline-family conditions (a live, resettable, non-publicly-definable phase, and a deadline that makes the hidden continuation law viability-rele</data></node><node id="P005-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Interface-relative non-exposure; T2: [S] Grade: theorem-grade direct finite instantiation; the C4 condition is generic and declared, not universal. Scope: the declared channel family Lambda on the finite privacy-wheel carrier. Under non-exposure with predictive witness content, there is a connector </data></node><node id="P005-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">T3: the explanatory gap as a strictness certificate.: Strict promotion means a nonempty factorization defect. The imported nonfactorization dichotomy says Delta_ fact (pi_0, pi_1)≠∅ if and only if pi_1 does not factor through pi_0 . [No-shortcut corollary; T3] [S] Grade: corollary-grade. Scope: decl</data></node><node id="P005-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-shortcut corollary; T3: [S] Grade: corollary-grade. Scope: declared source and target maps on a fixed finite interface. If the promoted content is strict over the public layer, no lower-layer shortcut derivation recovers it: the factor map does not exist, and any closed lower-layer compression fa</data></node><node id="P005-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Report descent and no-overreading; T9: [S] Grade: direct instantiation of imported audit theorems plus finite toy witness. Scope: declared report and no-report interfaces on a three-state hidden support. A valid report licenses only its descended boundary object, never the hidden structure. The fini</data></node><node id="P005-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Leibniz quotient; T4a: [S] Grade: theorem-grade direct quotient instantiation. Scope: the declared admissible public lens family at one layer. Any legitimate layer-level quantity factors through the quotient by empirical indiscernibility; duplicates indiscernible under all admissible lenses are one </data></node><node id="P005-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Certificate demarcation; T4b: [S] Grade: schema-grade certificate contrast. Scope: one public carrier face with an imported certificate battery. A subject/zombie distinction exists relative to a declared audit only if the certificate lens family separates the duplicates. The exact study realizes thi</data></node><node id="P005-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Priced residue and audited authorship; T5: [S] Grade: clauses graded individually below. Scope: one declared deterministic support, bounded observer budgets, declared generator provenance. Nonclaim: no ontic indeterminacy, no metaphysical freedom, no disproof of every libertarian view. Lawfulness (f</data></node><node id="P005-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: introspection is neither impossible nor worthless</data></node><node id="P005-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Same-level audit failure, rotating towers, confabulation; T6: [S] Grade: direct instantiation of imported self-audit theorems plus finite toy extension. Scope: instrument-indexed claims; complete same-level self-certification, not ordinary introspection. No instrument certifies its own complete soun</data></node><node id="P005-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Meta-problem composition; T16: [S] Grade: composition-grade and convention-relative. Scope: one two-state composite carrier whose public interface and report channel coincide; an explicitly declared completion convention. On the composite package: the T2-shape privacy witness and T3-shape strict-fac</data></node><node id="P005-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Binding witness with marking and erasure; T7: [S] Grade: theorem-grade finite instantiation of imported strict-refinement machinery. Scope: the declared branch family and interfaces. Binding is certified by strict refinement R_ rec K_ bw : two assemblages in one branchwise class and distinct recombi</data></node><node id="P005-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Purchased clock and no unified now; T8: [S] Grade: theorem-grade finite instantiation of imported clock and protocol criteria. Scope: the declared three-phase clock carrier and protocol family. The experienced-present proxy is the package's stabilized (order, tick, arrow) triple, with width the clos</data></node><node id="P005-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no smallness threshold adjudicates the audits (only the computed controls do)</data></node><node id="P005-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Induced-kernel similarity geometry; T12: [S] Grade: theorem-grade induced-kernel construction and exact audit; the geometry-birth grade is audit-only. Scope: the corrected 32-state micro-carrier (eight hues, two chroma, two texture levels), packaging lens forgetting texture, staged twice. The lens d</data></node><node id="P005-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no claim that any particular quality space has nontrivial G</data></node><node id="P005-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Descent-fixing gauge and rigidification; T13: [S] Grade: theorem-grade exact finite construction; the broader quality-space rigidification principle is schema-grade. Scope: an eight-state fiber bundle, |Q| = 4 public classes with two fiber points each, declared descents. Define the descent-fixing ga</data></node><node id="P005-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no relativistic proper time, no claim that every subject has this clock. Nonclaim (the repository name is ``subject birth''): no subject is created or detected</data></node><node id="P005-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no claim that actual loss or recovery of consciousness is hysteretic, and any external measure of closure depth would need its own descent audit, and none is given.</data></node><node id="P005-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Existence boundary schema; T10: [S] Grade: schema plus exact correspondences. Scope: a three-state maintenance carrier with a threaded record accumulator (credit accrues 1/8 after a nonempty kernel to a cap of 1/4 , decays 1/8 after an empty one) over the budget grid \ 0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1\ . Existen</data></node><node id="P005-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no complete metaphysics of personal identity.</data></node><node id="P005-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lineage, trauma, fission/fusion, empty target; T14: [S] Grade: theorem-grade transfer of imported route-transport machinery plus finite extensions. Scope: a declared memory-wheel route package with three histories, one current class, two predictive classes. Identity over time is represented by the l</data></node><node id="P005-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim: no general verdict about real collectives, and aggregation of local contents is never sufficient: it is the collapsed regime.</data></node><node id="P005-C0052"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Combination gate; T15: [S] Grade: schema with an exact one-carrier construction. Scope: one joint-package carrier with live states diagonal/crossed and a dead exit. Combination into a joint subject is certified only when the joint family passes both gates on the joint carrier: (i) C7 joint strict re</data></node><node id="P005-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A2: Substrate Indifference: Closure laws apply identically across carriers; the carrier label is not an extra closure law.</data></node><node id="P005-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This is an explicitly simplified approximation of T4b's demarcation target, not the full-certificate relation, which remains open until a full certificate exists.</data></node><node id="P005-C0056"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditional realization bridge; T17-O: [O] Grade: axiom-relative conditional schema, mechanized. Scope: arbitrary carrier type X and package pkg ; ClosureComplete and Realized are caller-supplied predicate parameters. Assume: certificate soundness: every simplified certificate is sound for lawful au</data></node><node id="P005-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">These are obligations for future work, not results, and the rows are illustrative targets: every field must be frozen prospectively before any data contact.</data></node><node id="P005-C0058"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Limitations: Twelve limitations, before any conclusion. Exact finite models only. Every positive result lives on a declared toy carrier. Nothing licenses transfer to large, noisy, or continuous systems beyond the displayed conditional schemas. No empirical content. No dataset, no parameter fit, no v</data></node><node id="P005-C0059"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Imported theorem map: Each import is used under its own hypotheses; analogy is never theorem transport. The table names the source engine, the imported object, and the consuming law. p 0.23 p 0.42 p 0.24 →prule Source (corpus paper) Imported object Used by Foundations I counting lemma ( 2^K of 2^N p</data></node><node id="P005-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">T3 (no-shortcut corollary).: Setup: T9's support; report boundary as lower readout, no-report boundary as target. Result: strictness (nonempty Delta_ fact ) iff no factor map; no closed lower compression recovers strict content. Witness: factor attempt returns none on the strict case. Controls: repo</data></node><node id="P005-C0062"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">T9 (report theorem).: Setup: hidden support \ u_0, u_1, u_2\ ; report channel; separately scoped no-report instrument; vendored claim classifier. Result: reports license descended boundary content only; the report map is non-injective; a no-report channel separates the identified pair. Witness: (u_0</data></node><node id="P005-C0064"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Schema boundaries worth repeating: The simplified schema's own graded honesty, beyond Section : its C3 exit check is one-step only (a genuinely terminal full-domain bisimulation remains an obligation); its C4 is a finite static specialization of the imported history/complexity-theoretic visibility p</data></node><node id="P005-C0066"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Trust base and known limitations: Trusted, inherited: vendored foundations Lean modules and their opacity-guarded axioms (Appendix ); the Lean toolchain; the Python interpreter and pytest. Known limitations, reported rather than resolved: the Lean manifest-level validator currently parses zero inven</data></node><node id="P006-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">full predicate-level non-membership proofs at every level remain framework future work.</data></node><node id="P006-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Two scope disclosures apply.</data></node><node id="P006-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finally, states the status, scope, and nonclaim boundary, and summarizes the audit-closure meta-theory.</data></node><node id="P006-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The isomorphism-invariance statement follows because each audit obligation is a typed field-level condition: transporting a record, route, residual status, nonclaim entry, or meta-audit discharge across an isomorphism changes its presentation, not its existence inside the fixed scope.</data></node><node id="P006-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Equivalently, every typed-record, route, residual-status, nonclaim, and meta-audit obligation demanded by the package has already been supplied.</data></node><node id="P006-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sigma_ stage stratum: The stage defect stratum Sigma_ stage ( C) is the multiset of stage / refinement entries whose stage law, refinement axis, or compatibility with the route registry is not declared. The stratum is empty iff every entry of Stage has a declared refinement kind.</data></node><node id="P006-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sigma_ rewrite stratum: The rewrite defect stratum Sigma_ rewrite ( C) is the multiset of rewrite records whose source and target presentations, admissibility tags, or blocked-rewrite annotations are not declared. The stratum is empty iff every Rewrite entry is either an allowed or an explicitly blo</data></node><node id="P006-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sigma_ meta stratum: The meta defect stratum Sigma_ meta ( C) is the multiset of meta-audit obligations (provenance trail, version pin, audit pass log, dispute resolution) that are not discharged in Meta . The stratum is empty iff every meta obligation has a typed audit record.</data></node><node id="P006-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Residual atom: A residual record a∈Sigma_ residual is an atom if it is minimal under _ res among non-empty records. Equivalently, a has a unique primary type and no proper non-empty refinement.</data></node><node id="P006-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It separates genuinely closed residuals from budgeted, bridged, selected, probabilistic, out-of-scope, nonclaim, blocked, and externally approved records, so that the cascade layer can compute downstream obligations from the pair consisting of a residual type and a declared status.</data></node><node id="P006-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cost-table admissibility: The base cost table of , together with the asymptotic companion rows used for refinement-chain discharge, satisfies two admissibility properties: Strict descent. For every cost-table or asymptotic companion row Down(a,s)=w , the output multiset w is strictly less than the s</data></node><node id="P006-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It also covers the status-family representative rows for any non-explicit type at budgeted, bridged, moduli, selected, probabilistic, out_of_scope, nonclaim, blocked, and hidden.</data></node><node id="P006-C0068"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Asymptotic soundness: The four asymptotic statuses of satisfy the implication chain eventual_zero implies contractive implies summable implies asymptotic_budgeted. Each asymptotic status closes the colimit residual at level Delta_ limit without producing a fresh atom in the limit. Moreover, if every</data></node><node id="P006-C0072"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">full predicate-level non-membership verification remains framework future work and is stated as scope in the abstract.</data></node><node id="P006-C0075"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For the hierarchy theorem ( ), the companion record exhibits two named finite witness carriers with typed obstruction markers, while full predicate-level non-membership verification is deferred pending a fuller enumeration of the cost table.</data></node><node id="P006-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Status, scope, and nonclaim boundary: The preceding sections develop AOR as a meta-theory of audit-closed carriers. Its formal objects are scope records, PreSB carriers, closure completion, defect strata, residual atoms, discharge cascades, and refinement chains; they are not a direct model of any p</data></node><node id="P006-C0078"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No strong-realism reading of AOR: Membership in AOR_ S is not a metaphysical or philosophical realism claim about the carrier's underlying domain. It is a record-level audit predicate: every declared record is typed, every cited route is audited, every residual is statused, and every nonclaim is reg</data></node><node id="P006-C0083"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The hierarchy theorem exhibits named witness carriers and typed obstruction markers, while full predicate-level non-membership proofs at every level remain framework future work.</data></node><node id="P006-C0088"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">An element of this lattice is a candidate audit extension of the seed carrier: it adds typed record data, route audits, residual statuses, nonclaim entries, and meta-audit discharge records, but it does not change the declared scope.</data></node><node id="P006-C0093"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">full predicate-level non-membership verification remains framework future work.</data></node><node id="P006-C0094"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Full predicate-level non-membership verification remains framework future work and would require substantially more of the 143-entry cost table than the present definition_metadata narrowing covers.</data></node><node id="P006-C0096"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Extended tables and nonclaims ledger: This appendix collects framework data that are too detailed for the body: the representative cost-table subset, the hierarchy witness carriers, the forced-secondary table, and the complete scope ledger. The tables are audit data, not additional theorem statement</data></node><node id="P006-C0099"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete AOR nonclaims ledger: [style=nextline] Membership in AOR_ S is a record-level audit predicate inside a declared scope, not a metaphysical claim about the carrier's underlying domain. This is the canonical nonclaim of . The framework stops at the AOR meta-theory. Downstream applications must</data></node><node id="P006-C0101"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The hierarchy theorem exhibits named carriers and typed obstruction markers, but full predicate-level verification for the hierarchy witnesses remains future work pending a fuller cost-table enumeration.</data></node><node id="P007-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Earlier papers in the fixed-support line of Six Birds Theory established admissible event packages, predictive and recombination quotients, a control-stable Route A flagship corpus, promotion criteria, and the intrinsic promoted-package survivor. They left open the internal carrier status of that su</data></node><node id="P007-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The construction does not prove that the intrinsic branch is a final carrier.</data></node><node id="P007-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">controls are signed off as null-by-design, and the support-only row remains support-only. @ &gt; p 0.19 &gt; p 0.18 &gt; p 0.17 &gt; p 0.16 &gt; X@ →prule Panel / role Count or stable ids Cross-seed classification Scope contract Interpretation for carrier claim Flagship anchors 2 anchors exact readout and law tran</data></node><node id="P007-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the nearby-positive panel blocks it as a real scope limitation rather than by collapsing into noise.</data></node><node id="P007-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The selection-support-enriched same-branch surface is shell-compatible and same-branch but does not repair the nearby-positive limitation</data></node><node id="P007-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sections and leave a sharply bounded result: the lawful same-branch witness survives, the coarse public carrier remains signature-only, the flagship strengthening lane remains protocol-indexed-only, and no cross-seed, full-core, or adjacent-surface upgrade lands.</data></node><node id="P007-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">no broader bridge or general event-geometry claim is made. @ &gt; p 0.22 &gt; p 0.15 &gt; p 0.19 &gt; p 0.18 &gt; X@ →prule Item Status Scope Role Non-claim / limitation Carried carrier surface survived scoped carrier flagship-only, protocol-indexed earned protocol-indexed carrier basis for downstream probes not a</data></node><node id="P007-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Limitations: The limitations are structural. The surviving carrier remains flagship-only; protocol indexing remains required; the public surface remains signature_only; cross-seed reach remains partial, and the four nearby positives remain real scope limitations rather than exact transfers; the broa</data></node><node id="P007-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It is the formal statement of what the paper does and does not claim.</data></node><node id="P008-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The preceding carrier-exactification paper established a scoped carrier layer on the Route A flagship support: a lawful same-branch witness, a non-faithful public signature shadow, a protocol-indexed carrier surface, scoped quadratic carrying, and two proto-event and proto-test pilots. We ask whethe</data></node><node id="P008-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim an accepted test package, shared-event identity, proto-effect fragment, probability valuation, composition result, lawful public carrier, faithful public compression, cross-seed event package, or full-core event package.</data></node><node id="P008-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Prior carrier result and claim contract: The prior carrier-exactification result is deliberately scoped: it is a carrier-exactification statement on the fixed Route A flagship support, not a public event package and not a public carrier theorem. Its lawful object is a refined same-branch witness; it</data></node><node id="P008-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[t] 4pt p 0.25 p 0.20 p 0.26 p 0.22 Inherited object Prior status Use here Nonclaim Refined same-branch witness (carrier side) Lawful refined carrier-side witness Carrier-side conditions in event bridge records Not public-shadow-native</data></node><node id="P008-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">they do not assert that public signatures determine refined carrier conditions.</data></node><node id="P008-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Every accepted claim must carry a complete P_1 – P_6 profile, every active role must have supporting evidence, and every residual limitation must be classified rather than left implicit.</data></node><node id="P008-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Designated surface: A designated surface is a tuple Sigma = (Z,\ f,\ Sigma_f,\ I,\ L,\ ), where Z is a finite carrier set, f : Z → X is a lens, Sigma_f is the induced definability structure, I is a family of admissible instruments, L is a family of selected lifts, and ∈ \ claim-bearing ,\ support-on</data></node><node id="P008-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event bridge record: An event bridge record on Sigma is an eight-tuple B_E = (C,\ P,\ I,\ L,\ V,\ Theta,\ S,\ A), where C is a carrier-side condition, P is a public/readout-side condition, I ∈ I is the declared instrument, L ∈ L is the selected lift (or forgetting map), V is a visibility-and-suppres</data></node><node id="P008-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event admissibility: An event bridge record B_E is admissible on Sigma , written Admissible(B_E, Sigma) , if DS(B_E)\ \ IV(B_E)\ \ SH(B_E)\ \ LS(B_E)\ \ PH(B_E) \ TA(B_E)\ \ CP(B_E)\ \ LA(B_E)\ \ XC(B_E)\ \ CB(B_E), where the ten conjuncts are declared surface, instrument visibility, suppression hon</data></node><node id="P008-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Candidate test family: A candidate test family on Sigma is a tuple T = (\ B_i\ _ i ∈ F ,\ Sigma_T,\ mu ), where F is a finite index set, \ B_i\ _ i ∈ F is a family of event bridge records proposed as members, Sigma_T is the declared test surface, and mu is an assignment of members to declared slots </data></node><node id="P008-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissibility implies bridge well-formedness: If Admissible(B_E, Sigma) holds, then each component of B_E is a well-formed instance of its declared record type and every field required by Definition is populated.</data></node><node id="P008-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Test admissibility has admissible nonempty members: If AdmissibleTest(T, Sigma_T) holds, then T has at least one member, every member satisfies Admissible(B_i, Sigma) , and the slot assignment mu realizes every required slot of Sigma_T .</data></node><node id="P008-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Proto-effect admissibility requires normalization: An admissible proto-effect fragment over an admissible test family carries at least one normalization candidate and a complement-like operation compatible with the partial-addition structure.</data></node><node id="P008-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Final-gate licensing: Each final-gate classification c listed in the permitted final-gate set is licensed by a specific conjunction of accepted-object predicates: c = isolated_proto_events iff ( B_E.\ Admissible(B_E, Sigma))\ \ ( T.\ AdmissibleTest(T, Sigma_T)), and analogously for the other classif</data></node><node id="P008-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Final gate: The final gate aggregates the preceding stages without changing their status. It does not repair the missing test-family slot, does not promote event-level positives into tests, and does not collapse accepted event-level positives into a no-event result. The classification places the run</data></node><node id="P008-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and limitations: The outcome is mixed but precise. The run is not empty at the event level: two scoped protocol-indexed positives survive the event gate, controls, layer placement, quadratic alignment, and exact-six profiling. At the same time, no accepted finite test family is produced. </data></node><node id="P008-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[h] 4pt ! p 0.38 p 0.48 Nonclaim Recorded reason No accepted finite test package accepted_test_count=0 The listed structures are not claimed by the run.</data></node><node id="P009-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Many obstruction arguments exhibit a localized failure mode that appears irreducible at the layer where it is first visible; this paper studies one audited route by which such needle obstructions cease to be obstructions, namely passage to a higher layer where additional admissible dissolving probes</data></node><node id="P009-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">These are framework-bound dissolution results, not a universal singularity-elimination theorem or a claim about any named external problem.</data></node><node id="P009-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The layer-dissolving endpoint theorem shows that, under predictive native bounds, residual bounds, and transport hypotheses, the dissolving currency is bounded uniformly across accepted stages, so no layer-dissolving predictive needles exist within the declared scope.</data></node><node id="P009-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it is not a universal singularity-elimination theorem and does not claim that every mathematical obstruction can be removed by moving to a higher layer.</data></node><node id="P009-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Minimum-spend duality: For a desired response z , define Cost_C(z;L,Gamma) :=∈f\ a^*C_Gamma a: L_Gamma a=z\ . Let L_ Gamma,0 denote L_Gamma restricted to the legal subspace of C_Gamma , after quotienting by Ker C_Gamma . Then Cost_C(z;L,Gamma) =z^*K_ C,Gamma (L)^ z when z∈Ran L_ Gamma,0 =RanK_ C,Gam</data></node><node id="P009-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive currency and the membrane criterion: In many applications the native data is not accepted all at once. It is assembled stage by stage: by refinement, enlargement, or passage to an accepted stage. At an accepted stage j , we have a stage-local instance of the framework from : a carrier, an</data></node><node id="P009-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive native membrane: A native predictive membrane with budget Theta is the bound K_j Theta j accepted. Equivalently, no accepted stage contains a native recombination witness (j,y) with y^*( K_j-Theta)y&gt;0.</data></node><node id="P009-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The diffuse terminal region depicts the dissolved obstruction: no layer-dissolving predictive needle survives within the declared scope ( ).</data></node><node id="P009-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Layer-dissolving membrane master theorem: Assume a formed closure/layer with exact package, null-mode legality, declared native and dissolving probe families, accepted predictive transport, all-six records, and nonclaim boundary. If K^L_j Theta^Y_j, Xi_j Xi^Z_j, and after transport S_j:Z_j→ Z , S_j(</data></node><node id="P009-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">and the nonclaim boundary records that the theorem asserts only this audited endpoint, not a universal singularity-removal principle.</data></node><node id="P009-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Separating anti-invariant readout: The anti-invariant readout separates the fixed locus when psi_-(x)=0 Longleftrightarrow x∈Fix(J) on the visible support of mu . It is quantitatively separating if, for every &gt;0 , there exists m( )&gt;0 such that (x,Fix(J)) implies \|psi_-(x)\| m( ).</data></node><node id="P009-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carrier-side anti-invariant membrane: Let E be an exact packaged carrier with audit C 0 on its legal quotient and anti-invariant native probe family L^-:E→ Y^-. The anti-invariant carrier currency is K^-:=L^-C^ (L^-)^*, with null-mode legality required. A budget record is a positive operator Theta^-</data></node><node id="P009-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Trace identity: If A_X is trace-class , then A_X=∈t_X \|psi_-(x)\|^2 dmu(x). Consequently, A_X=0 if and only if psi_-(x)=0 for mu -almost every visible object x .</data></node><node id="P009-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Separation implies fixed-locus confinement: Assume the anti-invariant readout separates the fixed locus. If A_X=0, then mu(X (J))=0. If the ledger is finite with positive weights, every visible object with positive weight lies in Fix(J) .</data></node><node id="P009-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Completed domination bridge: A completed domination bridge from the object ledger to the carrier membrane is a record A_X K^-+E with E 0 a declared bridge defect. Equivalently, in the exact case E=0 , if A_X=V^*V, K^-=W^*W, then the bridge is exact precisely when there is a contraction T such that V</data></node><node id="P009-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Douglas domination: Let A=V^*V and K=W^*W be bounded positive operators on the same response space. Then A K if and only if there exists a contraction T with V=TW.</data></node><node id="P009-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Duality-confinement membrane theorem: Let X=(X,J,mu,psi,Y, J) be an involutive object ledger with separating anti-invariant readout, as in . Suppose there is a sequence of completed domination records A_X B_n (n=1,2, ) where B_n 0 and B_n→ 0. Then mu(X (J))=0.</data></node><node id="P009-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exhaustive ledger squeeze: If A_ X,n B_n and the moving ledger is vanishing-exhaustive for A_X , then A_X=0. Consequently, under readout separation, visible object mass is confined to Fix(J) .</data></node><node id="P009-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">General obstruction budget: A defected duality-confinement record has the form A_X (1+t)( K^-_n+E_ src,n ) +(1+t^ -1 )E_ br,n for t&gt;0 , where E_ br,n is bridge/domination defect and E_ src,n is source/membrane defect. In a collapse ladder one often has K^-_n Lambda_n^ -1 Theta_0^-. Then the budget i</data></node><node id="P009-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A repair is lawful if it excludes the offending recombination by a declared nonclaim, weakens the claim by raising the relevant budget, strengthens or rewrites the carrier and audit so that the legal quotient has changed, adds native probes that see the residual direction, or completes the missing p</data></node><node id="P009-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict-extension repair contraction: If a strict native extension adds probes L^+= L\M , in the setting of the strict-extension chain rule of , then XiRes_C(D L^+) = XiRes_C(D L) -K_ DM L K_ MM L ^ K_ MD L XiRes_C(D L). Strict contraction occurs exactly in the directions seen by the new probes.</data></node><node id="P009-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lawful transfer record: A lawful transfer record between two exact packaged carriers E_ src and E_ tgt , with audit operators C_ src ,C_ tgt , native probe families L_ src ,L_ tgt , dissolving probe families D_ src ,D_ tgt , and adequacy residuals Xi^ src ,Xi^ tgt , consists of four transport maps T</data></node><node id="P009-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact bridge: A lawful transfer record is an exact bridge when the transport maps deliver currency-block congruences on the source and target legal quotients: T_L^*K^ tgt _ LL T_L=K^ src _ LL , T_D^*K^ tgt _ DD T_D=K^ src _ DD , and the residual-transport map T_Xi satisfies the adequacy-residual con</data></node><node id="P009-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Defective bridge: A lawful transfer record is a defective bridge with defect operator Delta 0 on the target dissolving block when the transport maps deliver T_L^*K^ tgt _ LL T_L=K^ src _ LL , together with the currency-block Loewner domination K^ tgt _ DD T_D K^ src _ DD T_D^*+Delta on the target di</data></node><node id="P009-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Adequacy-aware transfer: A lawful transfer of a layer-dissolving membrane must transport energy, native probes, dissolving probes, and adequacy residuals. Exact bridges transfer the native and dissolving budgets by congruence. Defective bridges produce explicit residual currencies; these must be pai</data></node><node id="P009-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work that aims the framework at a specific external problem must supply its own application-specific records, scope fence, and formalization disclosure.</data></node><node id="P011-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper gives a typed structural answer: within the scope, predictive content collapses when an upstream determining state is exposed to current observation.</data></node><node id="P011-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim that the csl implies a standard P≠ NP separation</data></node><node id="P011-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Computational substrate: A computational substrate sigma is a typed bounded polynomial-time computation context . It consists of: an instance family X_ sigma ; a size function on instances together with a bound N_ sigma , so that only instances of size at most N_ sigma belong to the bounded carrier </data></node><node id="P011-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">History space: Let sigma be a computational substrate and let L be an autonomous lift on sigma . The history space H_ sigma ^ L is the finite typed set of bounded polynomial-time computation histories generated by L on sigma . Each history h∈H_ sigma ^ L records: the substrate input instance; the so</data></node><node id="P011-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Forget-witness map: The forget-witness map pi:M_ sigma ^ L _ sigma ^ L sends each predictive class to the unique current class containing it: pi(Mclass h )=Qclass h . It is well defined because every polynomial-time current observable is also a witness-augmented observable with the witness and conti</data></node><node id="P011-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Autonomous lift: Let sigma be a computational substrate. An autonomous lift on sigma is a typed object L=(gen_ L ,sigma_ L ,replay_ L ) with the following components: gen_ L is a deterministic polynomial-time history-generation procedure. In plain terms, it is a deterministic polynomial-time algorit</data></node><node id="P011-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lift A—Self-generating substrate: An autonomous lift L on sigma is in Lift A if gen_ L generates substrate instances internally from the lift's own state, without an external instance feed. In plain terms, the lift is itself the instance generator: for example, a deterministic algorithm that, given </data></node><node id="P011-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lift C—Internally-seeded random: An autonomous lift L on sigma is in Lift C if gen_ L uses an internal seed to inject randomness into instance generation, while the seed is not visible to current observation. Equivalently, the apparent randomness is controlled by a state variable seen by sigma_ L , </data></node><node id="P011-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lift D—Planted-suppressed internalization: An autonomous lift L on sigma is in Lift D if gen_ L plants witness content into the generated instance but suppresses its visibility from current observation. For example, the lift may construct a 3-SAT instance from a satisfying assignment that it knows i</data></node><node id="P011-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Trace-fingerprint loop class: A trace-fingerprint loop class L_ traceFP on H_ sigma ^ L is a declared family of loops in the predictive-quotient holonomy carrier. Each ∈L_ traceFP acts on histories by a structured trace-level modification h .h . The map h↦ .h preserves the finite carrier's typed str</data></node><node id="P011-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Loop action on Q: Let ∈L_ traceFP and let Qclass h ∈Q_ sigma ^ L . The loop action on Q is LoopActionQ h =Qclass .h . This is well defined because the loop class is compatible with Q-equivalence: if Qclass h =Qclass h' , then Qclass .h =Qclass .h' .</data></node><node id="P011-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Loop action on M: Let ∈L_ traceFP and let Mclass h ∈M_ sigma ^ L . The loop action on M is LoopActionM h =Mclass .h . This is well defined because the loop class is compatible with M-equivalence: if Mclass h =Mclass h' , then Mclass .h =Mclass .h' .</data></node><node id="P011-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">M-vs-Q loop-action asymmetry: The pair (sigma,L) exhibits an M-vs-Q loop-action asymmetry if there exist ∈L_ traceFP and h∈H_ sigma ^ L such that the loop action is trivial on the current class but non-trivial on the predictive class: LoopActionQ h =Qclass h and LoopActionM h ≠Mclass h . Equivalentl</data></node><node id="P011-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Visibility coupling / exposure: Let sigma be a computational substrate, let L be an autonomous lift on sigma , and write sigma_ L :H_ sigma ^ L → S for its internal-state readout. The visibility coupling predicate Exposure(L,sigma) holds if and only if there exist an observable codomain O , a declar</data></node><node id="P011-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive witness content: The pair (sigma,L) has predictive witness content in the selected carrier if there exist histories h,h'∈H_ sigma ^ L such that Qclass h =Qclass h' but Mclass h ≠Mclass h' . Equivalently, the forget-witness map pi:M_ sigma ^ L →Q_ sigma ^ L is not a bijection: M_ sigma ^ L</data></node><node id="P011-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Candidate Structural Law (core): Let sigma be a computational substrate, let L be an autonomous lift on sigma , and let L_ traceFP be the trace-fingerprint loop class on H_ sigma ^ L . Assume the six structural premises (a)–(f) above. Then the following four clauses hold. Dissolution. If Exposure(L,</data></node><node id="P011-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">V-Differential classification: Within the typed substrate apparatus, the following four named substrates admit autonomous lifts of the indicated exposure type. BSD substrate. The Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer substrate , read here through elliptic-curve regulator content , admits autonomous lifts that are w</data></node><node id="P011-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">those consequences fall outside the scope of the present paper and are noted in .</data></node><node id="P011-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">pi_ QP is not a bijection onto P: The projection pi_ QP is not a bijection from Q_ sigma ^ L onto the standard class P . The obstruction has three parts. Histories versus languages. A Q_ sigma ^ L -class is a class of histories, possibly over a single bounded instance. A polynomial-time decision pre</data></node><node id="P011-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">pi_ MNP is not a bijection onto NP: The projection pi_ MNP is not a bijection from M_ sigma ^ L onto the standard class NP . The obstruction is witness identity: M_ sigma ^ L can distinguish different satisfying witnesses for the same instance, through witness-trace features such as Hamming-distance</data></node><node id="P011-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The present paper therefore does not claim a standard-ZFC unconditional proof of P≠ NP . It does not claim circumvention of the natural-proofs, algebrization, or relativization barriers in their native senses. It also does not claim resolutions of the host problems named by the V-Differential classi</data></node><node id="P011-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the broader claim is paper-side with narrowing disclosed here'' The structural shape is mechanized, with explicit premises or finite witnesses exposed. definition / faithful ``realized in Lean as Module.name'' The Lean declaration is the paper definition's typed realization. definition / narrowed su</data></node><node id="P011-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The three nonclaim rows carry no Lean coverage.</data></node><node id="P011-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not claim a bridge from that framework-internal projection to a standard P -vs- NP implication.</data></node><node id="P012-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We introduce a fixed-support route-transport framework in which histories can be equivalent at a current event interface yet differ as predictors of later admissible observations. For each interface, we define a current quotient by present-event indistinguishability and a predictive quotient by equa</data></node><node id="P012-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Route transport package: A route transport package is a typed object T= ( Sigma,I,\ Histories_i\ _ i∈I , \ C_ i→ j \ _ i,j∈I , \ E_i\ _ i∈I , id,comp,push,obs ) consisting of the following data: a fixed support object Sigma ; a family of interfaces I ; for each interface i∈I , a set Histories_i of a</data></node><node id="P012-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Current-event equivalence: Histories h and h' are current-event equivalent at interface i , written h CurrentEquiv_i h', iff they agree on all current admissible events: h CurrentEquiv_i h' Longleftrightarrow e∈E_i, obs_i(h,e)=obs_i(h',e).</data></node><node id="P012-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future-predictive equivalence: Histories h and h' are future-predictively equivalent at interface i , written h FutureEquiv_i h', iff they agree on all later admissible event observations after all admissible continuations: h FutureEquiv_i h' Longleftrightarrow j∈I gamma∈C_ i→ j e∈E_j, obs_j(push_ g</data></node><node id="P012-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive witness: A predictive witness at interface i is a pair of histories h,h'∈Histories_i such that h CurrentEquiv_i h' and (h FutureEquiv_i h' ).</data></node><node id="P012-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For every interface i , future-predictive equivalence refines current-event equivalence: h FutureEquiv_i h' implies h CurrentEquiv_i h' (h,h'∈Histories_i). Consequently, the identity on histories descends to a canonical comparison map pi_i : PredictiveQ_i → CurrentQ_i.</data></node><node id="P012-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For every admissible continuation gamma∈C_ i→ j , pushing representatives along gamma descends to a well-defined predictive transport T^ M _ gamma : PredictiveQ_i → PredictiveQ_j. It also descends to a mixed map T^ M→Q _ gamma : PredictiveQ_i → CurrentQ_j, [h]_ FutureEquiv_i [push_ gamma h]_ Current</data></node><node id="P012-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For every interface i , the canonical map from histories to predictive classes, h [h]_ FutureEquiv_i , is future-sufficient: every future observable at i factors through the predictive quotient PredictiveQ_i .</data></node><node id="P012-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Limitations and non-claims: Beyond the scope boundaries stated in Section , two further limitations deserve mention. The Lean formalization is intentionally partial: it covers the quotient comparison, predictive and mixed transport, the flatness characterization, sufficiency on the reachable image, </data></node><node id="P012-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim to subsume those frameworks.</data></node><node id="P012-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definitions used by the theorem spine: The abstract route-transport package consists of a fixed support object, a family of interfaces, interface-indexed history sets, typed continuation sets, interface-indexed event sets, and the operations of identity continuation, continuation composition, repres</data></node><node id="P012-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lean theorem map: The principal Lean names used in this paper are the following. [leftmargin=*] Refinement: futurePredictiveEquiv_implies_currentEventEquiv Predictive transport: futurePredictiveEquiv_push</data></node><node id="P012-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert generic current-quotient transport or a generic current-quotient loop action.</data></node><node id="P013-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">broader framework theorems are imported and cited, not re-proved here.</data></node><node id="P013-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Claims are scoped to the declared finite carrier, declared run families and protocols, and analyzed dataset</data></node><node id="P013-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The precise novelty conjunction is deferred to , after the carrier, run-layer, and data results are on the table.</data></node><node id="P013-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the artifact and reproducibility map is deferred to .</data></node><node id="P013-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carrier non-definability of the institution: On the declared finite repeated-game carrier, with the frozen rule catalog, prior, stage lenses, and future-observation catalog, the predictive quotient M strictly refines the current quotient Q at the richest stage lens. In particular, there are split pa</data></node><node id="P013-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Stage-lens obstruction: On the declared finite carrier, the closure deficit CD Pi tau of the stage lenses is positive at every declared stage-lens round and horizon. Equivalently, every relevant stage package has a fiber in which histories agree on the current stage observation but induce different </data></node><node id="P013-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Deterministic-substrate identity: On the declared carrier, the ordered rule pair and current public history determine all future play under the declared continuations. Hence H(Y^+_ t,tau X_t)=0 and CD Pi tau =H(Y^+_ t,tau Y_t^Pi).</data></node><node id="P013-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact gap–residual decomposition: Let K^ be the closed base law, the best autonomous stage-vocabulary kernel P(Y^+_ t,tau Y_t^Pi) , and let the canonical institution M refine the stage package. On the declared carrier, CD Pi tau =Gap Pi tau +ResidualEnt Pi tau , where Gap Pi tau = E is the expected </data></node><node id="P013-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Saturation fixed point: On the declared carrier, the iteration of E from the stage partition reaches a fixed point P_ . The fixed point is idempotent, E(P_ )=P_ , and it coincides at the declared loci with the predictive quotient M used in . Thus the canonical institution analyzed above is the satur</data></node><node id="P013-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Material forcing by live resettable labels: There is a declared label-feedback extension on the carrier in which a resettable, non-stage-definable public label is read by live reactive strategies and creates strict common-history predictive refinement. Histories that are identical in the matched bas</data></node><node id="P013-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert universal forcing.</data></node><node id="P013-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Noisy-carrier triage verdict: On the declared positive-noise carrier and tested noise grid, the two-sided coercion-null result survives exactly. One-sided probe witnesses still exist, but their density collapses sharply relative to the deterministic carrier. Every surviving noisy witness is currenti</data></node><node id="P013-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Selection non-descent: On the declared carrier, including the symmetric coordination game, there are histories with the same stage observation under the richest stage lens but with different exact posterior distributions over conventions. The convention distribution is, however, measurable with resp</data></node><node id="P013-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Same-layer selection away from self-duality: Along the declared one-parameter path of coordination games crossing the self-dual point, exact carrier computations give a sign-switching selector: one convention is selected on one side of the payoff asymmetry, the other convention is selected on the ot</data></node><node id="P013-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The promotion certificate: The previous subsections describe what the institutional state does. The last question is whether it is admissible as a promoted layer object, rather than a modeler's relabeling of the old stage vocabulary. The imported promotion standard supplies a gate battery for this p</data></node><node id="P013-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The strict-extension certificate: A certificate, in the sense used here, is a finite package of obligations and evidence. It is meant to prevent a higher-level object from being accepted merely because it has been renamed. The imported strict-extension framework specifies the obligations for certify</data></node><node id="P013-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict-extension certificate on the declared carrier: On the declared finite carrier, the canonical institutional state M=P_ is a certified strict extension of the stage game. The closed base law and exact price are supplied by ; non-factorization is supplied by ; saturation is supplied by ; materia</data></node><node id="P013-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Contribution: The anti-relabeling ledger is part of the result. This paper does not claim the predictive-state or causal-state quotient construction as new; that is a standard way to summarize a process by its predictive consequences . It does not claim the closure-deficit identity, or the optimalit</data></node><node id="P013-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim the predictive-state or causal-state quotient construction as new It does not claim the closure-deficit identity, or the optimality of the best autonomous macro-predictor, as new It does not claim the strict-extension, saturation, or promotion-obligation framework as new</data></node><node id="P013-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion, scope, and outlook</data></node><node id="P013-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope and limitations.: The exact results in this paper live on a specific finite carrier: a memory-one reactive-rule catalog, two main payoff families, a uniform prior, and declared lenses, rounds, horizons, and extensions. Their highest grade is theorem-grade-on-the-carrier, not a general repeated</data></node><node id="P013-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Outlook.: The most direct next construction is a within-agent learning or acquisition carrier, in which the individual agent is treated as a carrier object in the sense of the Six Birds treatment of agency . The failed crystallization bridge in shows that experience-as-composition selection makes th</data></node><node id="P013-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gate predicates: The promotion gates used in are imported from the promotion framework . On this carrier they are applied to the candidate promoted object M=P_ . The promotion claim must be a typed record: the old vocabulary, proposed new vocabulary, comparison map, update laws, evidence, and scope </data></node><node id="P013-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The detailed per-claim evidence table is deferred to .</data></node><node id="P013-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Consolidated nonclaims: [label= .,leftmargin=*] The exact carrier results are scoped to the declared finite carrier: the memory-one reactive catalog, the two payoff families, the uniform prior, and the declared lenses, rounds, horizons, and extensions. They are not general theorems about all repeate</data></node><node id="P014-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Open question: The key open issue is internal structure. A surviving promoted package is more than a convenient label only if it supports its own descended readout and update structure, remains honest under protocol treatment, retains nontrivial content under admissible second-stage recoarsening, an</data></node><node id="P014-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Joint criterion for internal structure: Internal structure is not certified by one scalar statistic. A promoted object counts as internally real only if several conditions are met jointly: descended readout, signature-level descended update, admissible second-stage stability on its retained surface,</data></node><node id="P014-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonreplacement boundary: Evidence recorded on the higher-layer readiness axis, on the nearby-support distinction surface, or on the flagship-local chart side-branch does not by itself alter the package-type verdict. These statement surfaces answer different questions and are not collapsed into one m</data></node><node id="P014-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nearby-support distinctions do not collapse: Readout-surface anchor-local limitation, detector-limited nearby support, and family-level erased-partner support are logically distinct outcome surfaces in the present audit. Positive evidence on one of them does not rewrite a negative or restricted outc</data></node><node id="P014-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Equal-footing comparison rule: For a candidate kind K and a case x ∈ C_ core , the primary audit outcome is attached to the pair (K,x) rather than to the raw number of admissible representatives of kind K over x . Representative multiplicity may contribute to ambiguity statistics, but it does not co</data></node><node id="P014-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bundle/dual non-displacement: Dual augmentation adds no decisive improvement over the non-dual sectioned and bundle-section-fiber routes. In particular, it does not increase supported plateau size, does not repair the closure ambiguity that blocks those routes, and does not displace the intrinsic ca</data></node><node id="P014-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol honesty does not, however, repair the nearby readout limitation</data></node><node id="P014-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Accordingly, the anchor-only outcome is a limitation of the present exact readout interface, not a finding of nearby object absence.</data></node><node id="P014-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Flagship-local chart support: On the flagship patch, the intrinsic candidate admits exact local affine/additive/matrix-like fits that match both the designated readout data and the signature-level action interface. This is a flagship-local, non-gating compatibility result; it does not extend to the </data></node><node id="P014-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[t] @ &gt; p 0.14 &gt; X&gt; p 0.22 &gt; p 0.22 @ →prule Component Current formal content Role in the paper Not yet claimed Definitions candidate-package, section-family, descended readout/update, recoarsening/stability vocabulary stabilizes the paper's abstract language no empirical verdicts proved Criteria pa</data></node><node id="P015-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Previous demonstrations of contextuality-type obstruction start from quantum mechanics or from externally specified measurement scenarios. We show that the same locally-Boolean-but-globally-obstructed structure can arise from an autonomous emergence framework—Six Birds Theory—without importing Hilbe</data></node><node id="P015-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event package: An event package is a quadruple E = ( C,\ B_c\ _ c∈ C , , A), where C is a finite set of contexts, for each c∈ C , B_c= P(A_c) is the finite Boolean event algebra of c , is a cross-context shared-event relation, A is the audit layer, including the admissibility and defect diagnostics </data></node><node id="P015-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Frozen-slice/shared-support object: A frozen-slice/shared-support object is a finite comparison base Sigma = (S,prep,prot,reg), where S is a finite support index set and (prep,prot,reg) record the fixed preparation, protocol, and evaluation regime under which the support is being compared.</data></node><node id="P015-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible global realization: An admissible global realization of an event package E=( C,\ B_c\ _ c∈ C , , A) consists of a finite set Omega and maps pi_c:Omega→ A_c for all c∈ C such that: Context coverage. For every c∈ C and every atom a∈ A_c , there exists omega∈Omega with pi_c(omega)=a . Event </data></node><node id="P015-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible global package: An event package E admits an admissible global package if it admits an admissible global realization in the above sense.</data></node><node id="P015-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Global obstruction: An event package E is globally obstructed if it admits no admissible global package.</data></node><node id="P015-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Proposition layer: The three-axis hierarchy is a disciplined account of how contexts are generated and what kind of claim each comparison may support. The resulting implications and non-implications can be stated compactly. [Mechanism change does not by itself imply lens incompatibility] Variation o</data></node><node id="P015-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Mechanism change does not by itself imply lens incompatibility: Variation of the outer mechanism or control-space point may change which contexts or package families are available, but it does not by itself imply that two contexts form incompatible record algebras on one fixed support object.</data></node><node id="P015-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lens incompatibility does not by itself imply packaging obstruction: Non-nestedness, strict extension, or empirical incompatibility of record algebras on fixed support does not by itself imply that the associated event package is globally obstructed.</data></node><node id="P015-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Packaging obstruction implies no admissible global package: If an event package is globally obstructed, then no admissible global package realizes all of its contexts simultaneously.</data></node><node id="P015-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Obstruction Language and Theorem Spine: This section states the formal core. The proofs are deferred to ; here we isolate the structural statements that later computational sections instantiate in finite form. The central object is the event package E , and the central question is whether it admits </data></node><node id="P015-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible global realization: Let E=(C,\ BoolAlg c \ _ c∈C , ,A) be an event package. If E admits an admissible global realization (Omega,\ pi_c\ _ c∈C ) , then: every context atom is covered by some omega∈Omega ; every context event is realized as an inverse image under the corresponding pi_c ; ev</data></node><node id="P015-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite obstruction certificate: Let E be finite. If some admissible exact global-realization backend for E is infeasible for the accepted shared-event relation, then E is globally obstructed: it admits no admissible global package.</data></node><node id="P015-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Quotient-backed obstruction on fixed support: Let Sigma be a comparison base for a selected family of contexts, and let Q_Sigma be the finite Leibniz quotient of the support by indistinguishability under those contexts. If no family of quotient classes realizes all context atoms while respecting the</data></node><node id="P015-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol-trap exclusion and control principle: Route-sensitive noncommutation diagnostics such as route mismatch, closure deficit, or control-path dependence do not by themselves certify global obstruction. They become formal evidence only when they are realized as failure of global realization for </data></node><node id="P015-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This limitation does not downgrade the accepted lens and packaging obstructions to mere suggestions.</data></node><node id="P015-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The theorem is conditional on the admissibility and Boolean-stability of the shared-event relation on the relevant fixed comparison object.</data></node><node id="P016-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We study recombination-sensitive branch structure on a fixed-support substrate in Six Birds Theory. Building on prior work on event packaging and predictive holonomy, we introduce branch assemblages and two quotient levels at an interface: a branchwise predictive quotient ( K_i ), which captures ind</data></node><node id="P016-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim a general reconstruction of non-Hilbert interference or a formalization of the full benchmark runtime.</data></node><node id="P016-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The claim is narrower: these recombination phenomena arise on fixed support, can be audited exactly, and admit a partial formal backbone on the Six Birds substrate.</data></node><node id="P016-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Formal Theorem Layer: The previous sections are empirical. This section records the supporting theorem layer established in the Lean development. Its role is interpretive: it identifies the abstract no-interference regime, proves that strict refinement obstructs that regime, and supplies one theorem</data></node><node id="P016-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Refinement under detectability: Suppose branchwise summaries at interface i are detectable by a distinguished family of admissible observables. If two assemblages are recombination-equivalent, then they are branchwise-equivalent. Consequently the forgetful map _i : R_i → K_i is well defined.</data></node><node id="P016-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Quotient-lift sufficiency: For each fixed admissible recombination observable, evaluation descends to the recombination quotient. More generally, any function on assemblages that respects recombination equivalence factors through R_i .</data></node><node id="P016-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict refinement obstructs factorization: If there exist assemblages B and B' at interface i such that B _i^ bw B' but B _i^ rec B', then no global branchwise factorization layer can hold. In particular, under detectability, failure of quotient collapse rules out the no-interference regime.</data></node><node id="P016-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Aligned concrete positive instance: There is a concrete aligned finite model for the promoted family n5_d1_w1of4_3of4 with named assemblages B_L and B_R such that B_L ^ bw B_R but B_L ^ rec B_R. Equivalently, the aligned model admits a strict-refinement witness. By the preceding corollary, no global</data></node><node id="P016-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and Limitations: The paper establishes a substrate-level result. On a fixed-support Six Birds architecture, branchwise predictive structure and recombination-sensitive structure can be separated operationally, and that separation can be audited under exact finite controls. The benchmark s</data></node><node id="P016-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not provide a reconstruction of quantum theory, and it does not claim to be the first general treatment of interference beyond Hilbert-space formalisms It does not prove the paired erasure benchmark in full runtime detail, the full cyclic-family generalization, or the dissipative control.</data></node><node id="P016-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Formal Definitions and Theorem Statements: This appendix records the formal objects and theorem statements used in the main text. It is not a replacement for the Lean development and does not reproduce full proofs; it is included to keep the formal vocabulary explicit and scoped.</data></node><node id="P017-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This is a presentational recasting: no new PDE input is introduced, the audit content is unchanged, and the AOR-instance theorem is presented under the carrier-attached discharge-assignment discipline of , with a data-bearing strengthening of the discharge-record fields stated as framework future wo</data></node><node id="P017-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">BG-windowed exact package: At each Bradshaw–Grujic stage j , the BG-windowed exact package Gamma_j collects the admissible BG-window probes (the relevant components of Lphys_ J,t and the windowed value of DBG_ J,t on the active shell P_j ) together with the structural constraints they must preserve:</data></node><node id="P017-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-go NG6: metric-shadow: Weighted or Gevrey-flavored metrics that shrink the apparent residual do not control the fixed BG readout unless the normalized metric still dominates the BG/BKM amplitude. Geometry inputs from Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg partial regularity or Constantin–Fefferman vorticity-d</data></node><node id="P017-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-go NG10: raw nonlinearity illegal as Schur probe: Raw nonlinear observables, including flux quantities, pressure interactions, vortex stretching, and triad terms, cannot enter the native family L^ phys as Schur-complement probes. Lawful entry requires one of the following: [label=( )] tangent lin</data></node><node id="P017-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">LP-transport rescue: Let S_j:L^2(P_j)→ H^ -s be the LP-transport stage operator with s q- , and set p=q- . The half-log staged budget transports to B_j(s) 2^ 2(p-s)j (1+mu L_j ) e^ -2cmu L_j , L_j= lambda_J . For every s&gt;p , B_ j+1 (s) B_j(s) 2^ 2(p-s) &lt;1 . Consequently _j B_j(s)&lt;∈fty on the transpo</data></node><node id="P017-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Substantive discharge of R_4 as a membership component: The source-admission recurrence record R_4 of C_ NS has primary type Delta_ source and forced secondary types Delta_ transport ,Delta_ scale ,Delta_ target ,Delta_ role . Its discharge status decomposes as (Delta_ source ,budgeted per stage)+(D</data></node><node id="P017-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Status, Limitations, and External Interpretation: The principal result is : given the closure records R_1, ,R_9 , every divergence-free initial datum u_0∈ H^ gamma_0 with gamma_0&gt;5/2 on the accepted smooth-data channel satisfies T_ (u_0)=∈fty . The companion of recasts the same records as the AOR-me</data></node><node id="P017-C0051"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The principal result is : given the closure records R_1, ,R_9 , every divergence-free initial datum u_0∈ H^ gamma_0 with gamma_0&gt;5/2 on the accepted smooth-data channel satisfies T_ (u_0)=∈fty . The companion of recasts the same records as the AOR-membership witness for the smooth-data carrier C_ NS</data></node><node id="P017-C0053"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">AOR-instance reading restricted to smooth-data subcarrier: The AOR-instance reading of , including , is restricted to the smooth-data Sobolev subcarrier u_0∈ H^ gamma_0 , gamma_0&gt;5/2 . No AOR-membership claim is made for generic critical-Besov data, weak solutions, distributional carriers, or any cl</data></node><node id="P017-C0056"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work may sharpen the bundle — for example, by replacing the combined half-log analyticity and LP-transport rescue with a stronger analyticity radius that delivers contraction on its own, or by promoting the R-record acceptance fields from Prop -valued predicates to data-bearing structures.</data></node><node id="P018-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds Theory has produced three landed Clay-class conditional closures: Navier–Stokes regularity, the Riemann Hypothesis, and P vs NP, each under the SB closure assumption of Foundations I. This paper does not re-prove those substrate results. It extracts their common meta-theoretic form for a r</data></node><node id="P018-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The scope is deliberately limited: BSD and Hodge are out of scope except as future work or prediction, and no substrate closure is strengthened here to a standard-ZFC unconditional claim.</data></node><node id="P018-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It is modest because it does not claim new unconditional results about the Clay problems.</data></node><node id="P018-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim that the space of mathematical ideas is finite.</data></node><node id="P018-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A closure formation is the background act of organizing an open problem, its admissible source material, and the claimed closure event inside one formal setting. A formed Six Birds closure is therefore a formal closure of an open problem in which the closure event is itself treated as a structural f</data></node><node id="P018-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carrier residual: For the purposes of this paper, the carrier's adequacy residual Xi_C(D L) is the Paper 1 obstruction-residual measure of a typed carrier C against its accepted-imports base, meaning the external mathematical material licensed for that carrier by closure formation: it records the re</data></node><node id="P018-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure-Content Thesis: Let c be a formed closure whose substrate lies in the V-Differential trace-state-only column, and let X be its target statement. The load-bearing structural content needed for c to close X is formed-layer closure content per Foundations I and is named as an accepted recogniti</data></node><node id="P018-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Calibration-Family Structure: There is a non-descending typed calibration family with a thirteen-entry seed catalog. Each seed entry is a calibration in the sense of , and the seed is open-ended: the framework may adjoin further typed calibrations without changing the fact that the initial catalog i</data></node><node id="P018-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bridges-as-Theorems: Every calibration-side typed bridge between calibrations is either a basis-change identity or a substantive theorem. Free amalgamation between calibrations, in which two calibrations are merely placed side by side without a typed relation between them, is not a bridge. In symbol</data></node><node id="P018-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bridge Type-Direction: Calibration-side typed bridges carry a directed source-to-target type signature. For a bridge B with source S and target T , the bridge is symmetric with its reversal if and only if S=T . In particular, when S T , the existence of a typed bridge S→ T does not by itself supply </data></node><node id="P018-C0052"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Named functorial gate: A named functorial gate consists of a name, a typed source, a typed target, a direction, and a stated theorem obligation. It is a typed bridge-as-content: the gate does not assert that the transport has been supplied, but records exactly what a supplied transport would have to</data></node><node id="P018-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Define G_ named to be the three records in . Each record has the five fields in : name, source, target, direction, and theorem obligation. Inspection of the displayed catalog gives exactly three entries, so | G_ named |=3. Moreover, each displayed row has a nonempty name and a nonempty theorem oblig</data></node><node id="P018-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictions and open problems: This section records predictions and open problems only. None of the paragraphs below is used as evidence for the formal statements of Parts I–III, and none is a claim that a missing substrate landing has already been supplied. The common theme is that the present pape</data></node><node id="P018-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert a Hodge closure, and it does not assert that the needed recognition source has already been named.</data></node><node id="P018-C0058"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The open question is whether a future Yang–Mills cascade should be expected to behave like a trace-state-only substrate, like a witness-content-exposing substrate, or like a new column not yet represented in the current V-Differential split.</data></node><node id="P018-C0063"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaims register: This final section is the paper's binding nonclaim register. It collects the exclusions and honesty caveats that govern the earlier sections: what substrate material is out of scope, which claims remain conditional under the SB closure assumption, which recognition sources are im</data></node><node id="P018-C0064"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It collects the exclusions and honesty caveats that govern the earlier sections: what substrate material is out of scope, which claims remain conditional under the SB closure assumption, which recognition sources are imported rather than derived, and which universal meta-claims are candidate future </data></node><node id="P018-C0065"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">BSD has no landed substrate paper in the Tsiokos corpus and is left to future work.</data></node><node id="P018-C0066"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">BSD has no landed substrate paper in the Tsiokos corpus and is left to future work.</data></node><node id="P019-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The formal carrier schema, including the typed fields used to attach the source to the saturated SAT layer, is deferred to .</data></node><node id="P019-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Four-step contradiction: Conditional on the structural recognition source Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden , SAT P. Equivalently, in standard conditional form, Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden implies SAT P. Thus, assuming Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden is supplied as input to the construction, the SAT decision problem is not in </data></node><node id="P019-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds Class-B Irremovability, scoped form: Let L_B be a Class-B carrier-feature law of the standard fixed-presentation polynomial current carrier, analyzed against the basis B_ fix . Then at least one of the following holds: L_B cannot derive Br^ lex _ SAT ^ ! _ Q ; L_B introduces a feature not </data></node><node id="P019-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The limitation is also part of the statement.</data></node><node id="P019-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The nonclaim that the source is not derived inside this paper is recorded in .</data></node><node id="P019-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The only remaining cost is the nonclaim of internal derivation, recorded in</data></node><node id="P019-C0051"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Every residual native to the proof stack is thus by_construction , approved_other , zero , hidden , bridged , blocked , or out_of_scope/nonclaim .</data></node><node id="P019-C0053"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim a standard-ZFC unconditional separation of P from NP .</data></node><node id="P019-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim that the structural recognition source Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden is derivable from the framework's primitive constructions. documents the relevant no-go: the SAT-specific source is tested against three derivation routes — a framework-internal derivation, a reduction from a more</data></node><node id="P019-C0058"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Recognition source not derivable from framework primitives.: This paper does not claim that the structural recognition source Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden is derivable from the framework's primitive constructions. documents the relevant no-go: the SAT-specific source is tested against three derivation rout</data></node><node id="P019-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The Class-B Delta_ horizon residual stratum is explicitly statused as out_of_scope/nonclaim in .</data></node><node id="P019-C0062"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">AOR-instance reading restricted to the formed SAT layer.: The AOR-instance reading of , including , is restricted to the saturated SAT layer T^ ! _ SAT on the standard fixed-presentation polynomial current carrier under the SAT-specific recognition source Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden . No carrier-attached </data></node><node id="P019-C0070"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six mechanisms (P1–P6): The saturated SAT shell carries the following six mechanisms. P_ 1 , machine update. This is the deterministic polynomial-time transducer class admitted on the SAT layer, namely the class K_ poly declared in the audited shell. Update is by lawful polynomial-time machine actio</data></node><node id="P019-C0071"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Recognition-source carrier schema: This appendix records the carrier schema for the recognition source Gamma_ csl-sat-hidden . gave the conceptual content: witness currentization fails on the formed saturated SAT layer under the lawful current instrument. The present appendix gives the typed-record </data></node><node id="P019-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gate audit: Every lawful current observable in E^ ! _ Q , acting on the saturated SAT layer T^ ! _ SAT , passes the following seven gate checks with the typed evidence records carried in its lawfulness data. G_ 1 , no SAT oracle smuggling. The check predicate says that the observable does not invoke</data></node><node id="P019-C0090"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Class-B carrier-property law: A Class-B carrier-property law is a theorem of the form Phi( C) implies O∈ O_ C : T(O), where C is a Six Birds carrier, Phi( C) is an intrinsic feature of that carrier, and the impossibility conclusion follows from the feature itself. The feature is not an external reco</data></node><node id="P019-C0096"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Quotient products, meets, and joins: Let R_1 and R_2 be equivalence relations on X , and suppose that both are respected by every lawful current observable F∈E^ ! _ Q . Define R_ × =R_1 R_2, R_ =EqCl(R_1 R_2). Then R_ × is an equivalence relation respected by every F∈E^ ! _ Q , and R_ is an equivale</data></node><node id="P019-C0102"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Time-stratum refinement: Let E^ [k] denote the family of observables with time bound O(n^k) , with E^ ! _ Q = _k E^ [k] . A time-stratum feature has two possible statuses. At a fixed stratum, an exclusion T E^ [k] does not imply T ^ ! _ Q , since T may lie in E^ [ ] for some &gt;k . Uniformly over all </data></node><node id="P020-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Certifying multiscale anti-localization only at t=0 is not useful unless legality is forward-closed; we lack general, mechanizable conditions under which SPT-legality propagates under time evolution. We propose a slot-audited channelized-feasibility contract in a Hilbert/band/cell model, where legal</data></node><node id="P020-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Upgrading empirical diagnostics into theorem conditions: A numerical audit is not a proof. However, it can be made theorem-aligned if the reported quantities correspond directly to explicit hypotheses and constants in the theorems.</data></node><node id="P021-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Earlier fixed-support papers in Six Birds Theory introduced admissible event packages, predictive quotients, and recombination witnesses. They left open whether a recombination-positive witness family supports a genuine promoted package or only a lower-layer summary. We answer that question on a fro</data></node><node id="P021-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Candidate promoted package: Fix a common support surface together with a designated observable family and an admissible family of lower-layer operations. A candidate promoted package is an abstract package-level description intended to summarize lower-layer states or histories on that fixed-support </data></node><node id="P021-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Induced update law: A candidate carries an induced update law when the admissible lower-layer operations act on the package in a well-defined way that does not depend on the choice of lower representative. If two lower states are identified by the candidate package, admissible continuations must not</data></node><node id="P021-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible second-stage coarse-graining: An admissible second-stage coarse-graining is a further package-level coarse-graining or repackaging that remains admissible relative to the retained observable surface and the induced update law. Not every quotient of the candidate is admissible; admissibili</data></node><node id="P021-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Promoted-package criterion: A candidate package counts as a promoted package on the designated corpus and slice only if all of the following hold: [label=( )] Sufficiency. The candidate supports the designated observable family on the frozen corpus. Induced closure. The admissible lower-layer operat</data></node><node id="P021-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol-indexed promotion: A distinct case occurs when promotion is available only after context or protocol state is internalized into the package. We call this protocol-indexed promotion. The base candidate does not qualify honestly on its own, because its apparent success depends on omitted prot</data></node><node id="P021-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">These cases are not promoted to theorem-core status, and we do not claim a broad route atlas on their basis.</data></node><node id="P021-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and Limitations: The intrinsic route P_ int is the main result because it survives on both flagship anchors, covers the four nearby positives, separates the required controls, and remains honest under the paired artifact audit without requiring representative-selected correction. The repr</data></node><node id="P021-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol-indexed promotion criterion: A protocol-indexed promoted package is one for which the base candidate does not qualify honestly without additional protocol or context state, but an internalized or indexed refinement of that candidate satisfies the promoted-package criterion once the omitted </data></node><node id="P021-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The flagship-oriented witness stub does not claim that the actual Route A flagship computation has been mechanized.</data></node><node id="P022-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper isolates a control-stable intrinsic family inside the fixed-support recombination layer of Six Birds theory. The first three papers of the series established admissible event packages on fixed support, predictive quotients on single histories, and the branchwise and recombination quotient</data></node><node id="P022-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reference-positive witness separation: For the reference-positive flagship anchor, there exists a concrete pair of branch assemblages on fixed support that are branchwise equivalent, agree under the erased-mix observable, and are separated by the screen-history observable at the decisive event. Henc</data></node><node id="P022-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reference-positive no-factorization contrast: For the reference-positive flagship anchor, the decisive recombination separation does not factor through the corresponding branchwise summary layer. In particular, the flagship witness is incompatible with the factorized no-interference comparison targe</data></node><node id="P022-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Completion-partner witness separation: For the completion-partner flagship anchor, there exists a matched concrete witness pair on the paired completion surface such that the same branchwise-versus-recombination contrast survives there as well: the pair agrees on the agreement-side observable surfac</data></node><node id="P022-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Completion-partner no-factorization contrast: For the completion-partner flagship anchor, the decisive recombination-sensitive content likewise fails to factor through the corresponding branchwise summary layer on the relevant support.</data></node><node id="P022-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim that every nearby exact-finite case in the same broad region is positive.</data></node><node id="P022-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and Limitations: The paper establishes a positive result inside the fixed-support recombination layer of Six Birds theory: the isolation of a control-stable intrinsic exact-finite family, carried by two matched flagship anchors in a relative-cycle regime, together with a nearby exact brea</data></node><node id="P022-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nearby breadth exists within the same relative-cycle family, and the erasure side remains useful empirical support, but the paper does not claim a global classification of all recombination-positive families.</data></node><node id="P022-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Formal Definitions and Theorem Statements: This appendix collects the formal definitions and theorem statements used in the main text. At the level of general mathematical form, the quotient and factorization language used here sits near behavioral equivalence, bisimulation, exact aggregation, and s</data></node><node id="P023-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Accounting-style inequalities are central in Six Birds Theory, but they do not by themselves rule out catastrophic extremes: highly localized ``needle'' probes can satisfy global budgets while producing large peaks. We ask when packaging plus accounting forces anti-localization, and propose Reflexiv</data></node><node id="P023-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We are explicit that Reflexive SBT is not proven here to universally drive capacities to zero</data></node><node id="P023-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What this paper does not claim.</data></node><node id="P023-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Eigenvalue characterization: For a subspace U = range(Psi) , _j(U) = _ B ∈ B_j lambda_ (Psi_B^ Psi_B ), where Psi_B^ Psi_B is Hermitian positive semidefinite.</data></node><node id="P023-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Trace uniformity on uniform cells: Assume U is shift-invariant. For the uniform partition B_j (cell size b=N/2^j ), _ B∈B_j _ i∈ B P_ ii = _ i∈ B P_ ii = m N |B| = m 2^j . Equivalently, trace_bound_j(U)=m/2^j .</data></node><node id="P023-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditioning controls cancellation-based localization (informal): Fix a support S with |S| s . For any nonzero v=D_S a , _ B∈B_j \|v_B\|_2^2 \|v\|_2^2 _ B∈B_j \|D_ S,B \|_ op ^2 sigma_ (D_S)^2 . In particular, if sigma_ (D_S) &gt;0 for all |S| s and each atom has bounded cell energy _ B \|d_i\|_ B ^2 a</data></node><node id="P023-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Route-union ledger implies anti-localization for all routes: Assume along a refinement ladder j→∈fty that the union capacity in tends to 0 . Then for every route r , _j(U_ j,r ) → 0 .</data></node><node id="P023-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim that all packaging families admit such a canonicalizer, nor that canonicalization is unique</data></node><node id="P023-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Degenerate case with canonical tie-break: Define Pkg_ can (A,m) to be spectral packaging with a canonical tie-break inside any near-degenerate cutoff block (e.g.\ select the m -subspace minimizing a symmetry-defect proxy). Then for all m_1&lt;m_2 , the sequential and direct routes agree: P_ seq,can = P</data></node><node id="P023-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Capacity functional implies anti-localization: Fix a refinement ladder j→∈fty . (Subspace artifact) If U_j is the feasible subspace at depth j and Cap_ sym (j)→ 0 , then _j(U_j)→ 0 . (Dictionary feasibility) If feasibility at depth j is s -sparse synthesis in dictionary D_j and Cap_ cancel (j)→ 0 , </data></node><node id="P023-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and future work</data></node><node id="P024-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper gives a conditional Six Birds closure of the Riemann hypothesis by moving the question from classical analytic number theory to a formed-layer trace setting. The substrate is the saturated completed Selberg trace closure of the completed Riemann zeta function, equipped with the functional</data></node><node id="P024-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The recognition source Gamma_ SDTC-Selberg Encoded in the Lean formalization as a typed structure carrier, not as a Lean axiom; downstream theorems take a value of this carrier as an explicit hypothesis. See App .: On the saturated completed Selberg trace closure Sel^ ! _ ,tr , the recognition sourc</data></node><node id="P024-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditional landing chain Composed in the Lean formalization with a value of the recognition-source carrier as an explicit hypothesis; see App .: Let Sel^ ! _ ,tr be a saturated completed Selberg trace closure. Under the standing assumption that Sel^ ! _ ,tr is Foundations-II admissible, and given t</data></node><node id="P024-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The scope and nonclaim discipline are the subject of .</data></node><node id="P024-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The AOR instance object attached to Sel^ ! _ ,tr Realized in the Lean formalization with a canonical assembled carrier; see App .: Let S_ RH be the scope consisting of the saturated completed Selberg trace shell Sel^ ! _ ,tr , the completed zeta function Lambda_ , the typed nontrivial-zero ledger Z_</data></node><node id="P024-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissibility-audit discharge Tracked by the formalization harness as a literal-data definition with status by_construction on the audit field of Sel^ ! _ ,tr ; see App .: The Audit_ L field of Sel^ ! _ ,tr has primary residual type Delta_ meta . It is discharged by a bridged construction-time admis</data></node><node id="P024-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The correct status is therefore bridged: Audit_ L carries the admissibility status, while the paper registers a nonclaim of a separate derivation of that status.</data></node><node id="P024-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Typed real-coordinate discharge Tracked by the formalization harness as a literal-data definition with status by_construction; the A3 representation note applies. See App .: The typed real-coordinate parameter used by the RH shell has primary residual type Delta_ presentation . The identification of</data></node><node id="P024-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Therefore the presentation residual is not ignored: it is closed by the explicit construction-parameter record together with the nonclaim of an internal derivation of the classical identification.</data></node><node id="P024-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">AOR-instance theorem: [RH AOR-instance theorem Verified in the Lean formalization against the local refinement-stable AOR predicate over the assembled-carrier fields; the partial alignment relative to the full audit-closure refinement-stable characterisation of is recorded in App . ] Under the disch</data></node><node id="P024-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the discharge atoms of the eight-stratum / twelve-type residual atlas are assembled literally from the shell, the recognition source, and the bridge fields, so the classification carries the same conditional content as Gamma_ SDTC-Selberg implies RH and inherits every preceding scope boundary unchan</data></node><node id="P024-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work.: Three extension targets are natural. First, the admissibility of Sel^ ! _ ,tr should be derived rather than carried as construction-time audit data. A stronger development would unfold the seven Foundations-II schemas as predicates and verify them for the completed Selberg trace instan</data></node><node id="P024-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The substantive recognition-source and translation-interface content is deferred to and , respectively.</data></node><node id="P025-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">When must an operator-valued obstruction associated with an involutive system vanish? This paper answers that question inside the Six Birds framework by naming Self-Dual Trace Confinement (SDTC) as the recognition source for formed closures with genuine involutive self-duality. The mechanism is the </data></node><node id="P025-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cross-substrate examples such as CPT symmetry, gauge invariance, particle–antiparticle duality, quantum self-adjointness, and function-field analogues are structural predictions and validation targets for future work, not theorems established here.</data></node><node id="P025-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Involutive object ledger Realized in the Lean formalization; see App .: An involutive object ledger is a tuple X =(X,J,mu,psi,Y,J_ iso ) consisting of the following data: a visible carrier X ; an involution J:X→ X , so J(Jx)=x for every x∈ X ; a positive measure, or in the finite case a positive wei</data></node><node id="P025-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Anti-invariant object ledger Realized in the Lean formalization; see App .: The anti-invariant object ledger of X is the positive cone element A_ X := ∈t_X psi_ - (x)psi_ - (x)^* dmu(x), psi_ - (x)=P_ - psi(x). Equivalently, A_ X is the Gram-type positive element generated by integrating the anti-in</data></node><node id="P025-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Trace identity Recorded in the Lean formalization by constructor unfolding from the typed anti-invariant-ledger field; see App .: Let A_ X be the anti-invariant ledger of an involutive object ledger X , with anti-invariant readout psi_ - . Then _ X = ∈t_X \|psi_ - (x)\|^2 dmu(x).</data></node><node id="P025-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Separation–confinement Checked in the Lean formalization by typed-bridge composition over the measure-reading bridges; see App .: Let X be an involutive object ledger with anti-invariant ledger A_ X and qualitatively separating anti-invariant readout psi_ - . If A_ X =0 in the typed positive cone, t</data></node><node id="P025-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Quantitative confinement Checked in the Lean formalization by typed-bridge composition with a Markov-step hypothesis; see App .: Let X be an involutive object ledger with anti-invariant ledger A_ X and quantitatively separating anti-invariant readout psi_ - with modulus m . Then, for every &gt;0 , mu (</data></node><node id="P025-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Completed domination bridge: A completed domination bridge from A_ X to a carrier-side anti-invariant currency K^ - consists of a positive defect E in the typed positive cone, with 0 E, together with the domination relation A_ X K^ - +E. The bridge is exact when E=0 and defected when the defect term</data></node><node id="P025-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Douglas factorization, typed-cone form Packaged in the Lean formalization through the typed Douglas-data carrier; see App .: Let A=V^*V and K=W^*W be bounded positive operators on the same response space, represented as positive elements of the typed positive cone. Then A K Longleftrightarrow T\ a c</data></node><node id="P025-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Duality-confinement membrane theorem Verified in the Lean formalization at the typed positive-cone level; see App .: Let X be an involutive object ledger with carrier X , involution J , measure mu , anti-invariant readout psi_ - , and anti-invariant ledger A_ X . Assume that psi_ - is qualitatively </data></node><node id="P025-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exhaustive moving ledger: An exhaustive moving ledger over an involutive object ledger X consists of a sequence of positive cone elements A_ X,n , inclusion transports _n , and positive tails T_n with 0 T_n , such that, for every n , A_ X _n A_ X,n _n^*+T_n. It is vanishing-exhaustive when there are</data></node><node id="P025-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exhaustive ledger squeeze Checked in the Lean formalization; the ambient bound is supplied as a moving-ledger field. See App .: Let X be an involutive object ledger with anti-invariant ledger A_ X . If X admits a vanishing-exhaustive moving ledger, then A_ X =0 . If, moreover, the anti-invariant rea</data></node><node id="P025-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Defected obstruction budget: A defected obstruction budget at level n is a domination record of the form A_ X (1+t) (Kminus_ n +E_ src,n ) +(1+t^ -1 )E_ br,n , where t&gt;0 , where E_ src,n is a source defect, and where E_ br,n is a bridge defect. Both defects are positive cone elements. The unparamete</data></node><node id="P025-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Optimized scalar trace budget Tracked by the formalization harness; the AM–GM step is supplied as a typed-scalar hypothesis. See App .: For a defected obstruction budget at level n , set a_n= (Kminus_ n +E_ src,n ), b_n= E_ br,n , with a_n,b_n≥0 . Then ∈f_ t&gt;0 = ( a_n + b_n )^2. In particular, if a_</data></node><node id="P025-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SDTC is a named structural law, not a derived theorem.: Self-Dual Trace Confinement is named as a recognition source: structural content supplied by formed-layer closure per Foundations I, not derived from framework primitives alone. This paper does not present SDTC as a theorem derived from earlier</data></node><node id="P025-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This is a scope statement on the typed-scalar abstraction, not a math nonclaim about AM–GM itself.</data></node><node id="P025-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work.</data></node><node id="P025-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work.: The first extension target is an operator-theoretic realization of the typed cone. One would instantiate the cone with positive trace-class operators on a Hilbert space, prove the trace identity and positivity-to-zero inside that setting, and connect the Douglas factorization carrier u</data></node><node id="P026-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Earlier work in the Six-Bird program supplies six primitive role families ( P_1, ,P_6 — descent, representability, route mismatch, refinement, packaging, audit), an admissibility and exact-six scope discipline over FATCD , inherited status, threshold, visibility, and audit machinery, and concrete mo</data></node><node id="P026-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">a finite nonclaim register N that records the negative boundary of every admissible claim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Core Calculus Results: The paper records five core finite-calculus theorems in Section 6: No total six-symbol algebra (Theorem 1, Section 6): cell status does not factor through the primitive pair (P_ i ,P_ j ) alone, blocking any total binary algebra on P as a status-decoding map. Typed non-collaps</data></node><node id="P026-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-Go and Countermodel Results: The paper records the cohomological no-drive theorems of Section 8 and the countermodel atlas of Section 9: Forest no-drive (Theorem 15): beta_ 1 (G)=0 implies H^ 1 (G;k)=0 . Exact-form null-drive (Theorem 16): a=dphi implies _ gamma a=0 for every cycle gamma . Nonzer</data></node><node id="P026-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Promotion and Instrument Results: The paper records the promotion-and-stacking theorems of Section 10 and the self-reference theorems of Section 11: Promotion gate soundness (Theorem 19) and strict promotion implies nonfactorization (Theorem 20): any accepted-family promotion verdict requires every </data></node><node id="P026-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Levels are not a substitute for instruments either: a level fixes which kind of content a judgment refers to, while an instrument fixes which content is visible, suppressed, or outside scope and what claim strengths are admissible.</data></node><node id="P026-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Suppressed content is not asserted to be false, and content outside the scope of I is not asserted to be unknowable.</data></node><node id="P026-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">with Gamma a finite typed context, T an admissible theory package, I an instrument, and a claim whose use-set has been audited against the visibility, threshold, audit, and nonclaim records attached to the judgment.</data></node><node id="P026-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Main Negative Scope: The paper does not prove, and does not assume as background, any of the following: a universal exact-six theorem stating that every phenomenon, every theory, or all of mathematics reduces to the six primitive labels; a total binary algebra *: P^2→ P on the primitive labels, or a</data></node><node id="P026-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The domain is a finite tuple of finite typed components: a primitive set, a level set, a host inventory, a content universe, instruments, packages, profiles, witness and update families, defect records, judgment families, status families, gates, dependencies, audits, visibility data, source records,</data></node><node id="P026-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[] @ ll@ →prule [b] 0.47 Record kind [b] 0.47 Reference [t] 0.47 theory package T=(Z,f,Sigma_f,E, A) together with attached Foundations III records [t] 0.47 Subsection 3.3 [t] 0.47 instrument record I [t] 0.47 Subsection 3.4 [t] 0.47 primitive label P_i∈ P [t] 0.47 Subsection 2.2.1 [t] 0.47 witness </data></node><node id="P026-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">suppression is a relation between a record and an instrument, not a property of the record alone, and it does not assert that the suppressed record is false.</data></node><node id="P026-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">a finite nonclaim record N_ T recording what claims about the package are explicitly not asserted.</data></node><node id="P026-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">N_I : the finite nonclaim record of I .</data></node><node id="P026-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">where Gamma is a finite typed context of attached records (witnesses, updates, profiles, level/lens/interface records, visibility records, threshold records, audit records, defect records, and nonclaim records), T is an admissible theory package, I is an admissible instrument, is a claim with a fini</data></node><node id="P026-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The full classifier under which is determined consults the visibility partition Visible_I/Suppressed_I , the bridge policy BridgePolicy_I , the threshold policy ThresholdPolicy_I , the source policy SourcePolicy_I , the audit policy AuditPolicy_I , and the nonclaim record N_I .</data></node><node id="P026-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">When the failure is that the candidate judgment is not covered by the declared profile, host, or instrument, the later judgment classifier records the candidate as outside scope rather than accepted.</data></node><node id="P026-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[] @ ll@ →prule [b] 0.47 Primitive [b] 0.47 Wit(P_i) [t] 0.47 P_1 [t] 0.47 descent success/failure records, rewrite obstruction records, closure-deficit records [t] 0.47 P_2 [t] 0.47 representability and nonrepresentability records, gate decisions, support and capacity records [t] 0.47 P_3 [t] 0.47 </data></node><node id="P026-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[] @ ll@ →prule [b] 0.47 Primitive [b] 0.47 Upd(P_i) [t] 0.47 P_1 [t] 0.47 mark descent failure, install descended update, rewrite, repair [t] 0.47 P_2 [t] 0.47 mark representable or nonrepresentable, gate support, update admissibility record [t] 0.47 P_3 [t] 0.47 record route mismatch, holonomy, co</data></node><node id="P026-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The internal components are P , Lev , Host , Cont , Instr , Pkg , Prof , Wit , Upd , Def , Judg , Status , Gate , Dep , Audit , Vis , Source , and Nonclaim .</data></node><node id="P026-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A pair-observable record is a finite typed tuple recording, in addition to \ i,j\ and mu , the joint witness data, the source-of-truth record, the branch-compatibility record, the visibility record, the threshold record, the audit record, the defect record, and the nonclaim record that the surroundi</data></node><node id="P026-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">N : the nonclaim record attached to the bridge.</data></node><node id="P026-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Dependency and No-Go Judgments: Dependency judgments record typed relationships between content classes under an instrument. They are the working format for the no-go and bridge-required claims that appear throughout the paper, including the route-mismatch-requires-bridge dependency that keeps the d</data></node><node id="P026-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">accepted : the claim is accepted by I under Gamma, T with admissible visibility, threshold, source, audit, and nonclaim data. rejected : the instrument has an admissible refuting witness for the claim. provisional : partial evidence exists and the profile permits provisional status. blocked : the cl</data></node><node id="P026-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaim is a finite family of admissible nonclaim records, attached either to instruments (as N_I ) or to individual judgments.</data></node><node id="P026-C0052"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">a nonclaim record is missing where one is required by the surrounding instrument or judgment</data></node><node id="P026-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">When the bridge does not claim fixed-point structure on the target, the stability gate is recorded as not_required rather than enforced.</data></node><node id="P026-C0060"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">they do not assert any equality relation between P_i and P_j .</data></node><node id="P026-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Corollary: Pair labels alone do not determine cell status. The same primitive pair (P_i,P_j) , with different witness, update, profile, threshold, visibility, audit, or defect data, can produce different cell statuses; this was the working content of Theorem 1, and Theorem 2 records the underlying t</data></node><node id="P026-C0065"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 7 (DescentDefectEquivalence; theorem-grade). With X,Y,q,F as in Theorem 6, define the descent defect</data></node><node id="P026-C0067"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-7 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0070"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 11 (IdempotentSaturation; theorem-grade). Let X be a finite set on H_ fin , and let E:X→ X be a finite self-map. If E^2=E , then for every n 1 ,</data></node><node id="P026-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 13 (FactorizationDefectEquivalence; theorem-grade). With S,pi_0,pi_1 as in Theorem 12, define the factorization defect</data></node><node id="P026-C0076"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Drive Certificate: The drive face P6_ drive ^ H^1 on H_ graph is a finite typed record stating that some cohomology class [a]∈ H^1(G) is nonzero. A positive cycle-supported cohomological drive certificate consists of a finite 1 -cochain a∈ C^1(G) ; a finite cycle gamma∈ Z_1(G) with _gamma a≠ 0 ; the</data></node><node id="P026-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 15 (ForestNoDrive; theorem-grade). For a finite graph G on H_ graph , if beta_1(G)=0 , then</data></node><node id="P026-C0083"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-12 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0086"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-7 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0088"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-12 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0090"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-14 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0094"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-13 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0096"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-16 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim, and Theorem 24 of Section 11 makes the same-level self-audit failure formal.</data></node><node id="P026-C0100"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gate Statuses: Each ordinary gate carries a status from the packet status set GateStatus = \ pass, fail, not_required, not_checked, outside_scope \ .</data></node><node id="P026-C0101"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A gate may be marked not_required when the bridge does not claim the corresponding content</data></node><node id="P026-C0104"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 21 (NonStrictPromotionImpliesFactorization; theorem-grade). Let B_ j→ j+1 be a promotion bridge. If the promotion classifier assigns</data></node><node id="P026-C0106"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gate Results: The gate-results record GateResults(B_ 0→ 1 ^ Toy22 ) assigns: G_ suff =pass , since the bridge data conforms to the schema of Subsection 10.1.1; G_ vis =pass , since the bridge content lies in Visible_ I_ prom ; G_ audit =pass , since the audit record passes AuditPolicy_ I_ prom ; G_ </data></node><node id="P026-C0107"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">They are deferred to future work</data></node><node id="P026-C0108"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No General Stacking Composition Theorem: The paper does not prove a general stacking composition theorem of the form Promote(B_ j→ j+1 ):strict Promote(B_ j+1→ j+2 ):strict implies Promote(B_ j→ j+2 ):strict,</data></node><node id="P026-C0109"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not prove a general stacking composition theorem of the form</data></node><node id="P026-C0111"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No Local-to-Global Promotion Theorem: The paper does not prove a local-to-global promotion theorem of the form ( U_alpha∈cover, Promote(B_alpha on U_alpha):strict ) implies Promote(B_ global ):strict.</data></node><node id="P026-C0112"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not prove a local-to-global promotion theorem of the form</data></node><node id="P026-C0113"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">a positive gluing theorem for compatible local promotion bridges is recorded as future work in Subsection 16.3.2.</data></node><node id="P026-C0114"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Subsection 11.9 discusses instrument transfer and marks its full classifier as future work.</data></node><node id="P026-C0115"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the nonclaim record N_ attached to the claim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0118"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 23 (NoOverreadingSuppression; theorem-grade). Let I be an admissible instrument, a finite claim, and L a finite set of admissible visibility bridges under I . If</data></node><node id="P026-C0121"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each I_n is an instrument in the sense of Subsection 11.1.1, with finite logs, declared visibility policy, declared source policy, declared audit policy, declared nonclaim register, and declared scope.</data></node><node id="P026-C0123"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 25: Finite Rotating-Audit Theorem: This subsection records the finite rotating-audit theorem of Foundations III. The theorem is theorem-grade: it states that, given any finite stack of admissible instruments, there is a one-level extension instrument and an audit bridge that can finitely aud</data></node><node id="P026-C0124"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">delta_ N ^ stack = delta_ instr delta_ bridge delta_ visibility delta_ source delta_ audit delta_ circular delta_ nonclaim delta_ status ,</data></node><node id="P026-C0128"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Define B^ audit _ N→ N+1 to be the rotating-audit bridge of Subsection 11.7.2 with target scope Phi_ N _ N , language map L that translates each I_n -record into I_ N+1 ^ stack 's claim language, visibility map V exposing each record in Phi_ N either directly or through visibility bridges, audit thr</data></node><node id="P026-C0131"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This subsection records the instrument-transfer rule that governs whether a claim accepted under one instrument may be transported to another, and marks the corresponding full classifier as future work.</data></node><node id="P026-C0133"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">requires I and J admissible, in the claim types of both instruments via L_ I→ J , the supporting records visible to J either directly or through visibility bridges of Subsection 11.3.3, audit thresholds declared, the bridge audited, no unstratified circular dependency between in I and the records th</data></node><node id="P026-C0134"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">both are outside the scope of the present paper.</data></node><node id="P026-C0138"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under the admissibility assumptions for H and the instrument I_ AI , the in-scope, visibility, evidence, threshold, audit, and nonclaim conditions for the headline claim pass, so the instrument-indexed verdict is accepted .</data></node><node id="P026-C0140"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under the admissibility assumptions for H and the instrument I_ AI , the in-scope, visibility, evidence, threshold, audit, and nonclaim conditions for the headline claim pass, so the instrument-indexed verdict is accepted .</data></node><node id="P026-C0142"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under the admissibility assumptions for H and the instrument I_ AI , the in-scope, visibility, evidence, threshold, audit, and nonclaim conditions for the headline claim pass, so the instrument-indexed verdict is accepted .</data></node><node id="P026-C0147"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The discipline of the section is uniform: each realization is scoped, each declares its host, instrument, source-of-truth policy, audit policy, visibility policy, and nonclaim register, and each realizes a declared fragment of the calculus.</data></node><node id="P026-C0148"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">M is the realization object: a finite typed record carrying the host data, the instrument, the source-of-truth policy, the audit policy, the visibility policy, and the nonclaim register required by the host</data></node><node id="P026-C0154"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">PICA Nonclaims: The PICA nonclaim register N_ PICA contains, in particular: enumi . enumi . PICA is not the universal six-bird algebra: PICAReal_ fin realizes a declared finite stochastic fragment, not the whole calculus. The recovered 6× 6 multiplicity count is a PICA-profile model fact under the c</data></node><node id="P026-C0155"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Implementation correctness of any concrete PICA implementation is future work and is not certified by this realization theorem</data></node><node id="P026-C0161"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cantor Nonclaims: The Cantor nonclaim register N_ Cantor contains: enumi . enumi . The realization is scoped to the audited Cantor shell S_ aud ; it is not a shell-general theorem and does not assert a strict-bridge passage outside the shell. There is no shell-general strict-bridge theorem; extendin</data></node><node id="P026-C0162"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it is not a shell-general theorem and does not assert a strict-bridge passage outside the shell. the realization does not assert structural facts about external classes of theories unless an admissible inter-theory bridge is supplied.</data></node><node id="P026-C0165"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaims: The graph/cohomology nonclaim register N_ coh contains: enumi . enumi . The drive certificate P6_ drive ^ H^1 with [a]≠ 0 in H^ 1 (G;k) is one drive certificate family, not every possible drive notion. A P_ 3 -route/holonomy witness is not a P6_ drive ^ H^1 assertion in the sense of Secti</data></node><node id="P026-C0166"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert any cross-model fact beyond what the per-row realizations already record</data></node><node id="P026-C0169"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not assert any cross-row equivalence of realizations of the same primitive, and it does not assert any vertical aggregation across columns.</data></node><node id="P026-C0172"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For every primitive P_ i realized by some realization in the table, the calculus does not assert that the four realizations together realize the whole calculus. Two cells in the same row carrying entries for the same primitive P_ i are not identified: the PICA realization of P_ 6 as audit/provenance</data></node><node id="P026-C0179"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Under the admissibility assumptions for H_ DC and the instrument I_ DC , the in-scope, visibility, evidence, threshold, audit, and nonclaim conditions for the headline claim pass, and the instrument-indexed verdict is accepted .</data></node><node id="P026-C0183"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem 32: Strict Extension as No-Filler: This subsection proves Theorem 32: in the finite decorated double-category fragment of Subsection 14.1, a strict extension between two object maps pi_ 0 ,pi_ 1 on a common carrier S is recovered as the absence of a factorization filler in a decorated square</data></node><node id="P026-C0186"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">In particular, the per-square exactness conditions of Subsubsection 14.1.2 do not assert that:</data></node><node id="P026-C0187"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A decorated square carries its own square-status verdict only as part of a full decorated/audited record: witness and update data, language, visibility, threshold, audit, defect, square status, and nonclaim data must be present when the square is used as a BirdInt^ aud _ fin claim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0188"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No General Obstruction Propagation Theorem: The pasting law of Subsubsection 14.1.3 records the aggregation rule for defects under finite strict pasting: delta_ Xi_ 1 Xi_ 2 =delta_ Xi_ 1 delta_ Xi_ 2 delta_ paste (Xi_ 1 ,Xi_ 2 ) . This aggregation is a syntactic record on the finite paste, not a pro</data></node><node id="P026-C0189"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">In particular, Section 14 does not claim a general theorem of the form</data></node><node id="P026-C0191"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Proposition 35: Mechanization Target Feasibility: Proposition 35 (MechanizationTargetFeasibility). The finite audited manifest scope of BirdInt^ aud _ fin is represented by a Lean 4 validation artifact. Specifically: enumi . enumi . every required schema definition listed in lean/manifest.toml is re</data></node><node id="P026-C0193"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Mechanization Scope Nonclaim: The Lean artifact validates the finite audited scope represented by lean/manifest.toml. It does not assert: any theorem, definition, model realization, or audit record not listed in lean/manifest.toml; empirical correctness of any concrete PICA, Cantor, abstract-interpr</data></node><node id="P026-C0194"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert:</data></node><node id="P026-C0196"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Limitations, Nonclaims, and Future Work: This section collects the final scope discipline of BirdInt^ aud _ fin and the future-work boundaries of the paper. Subsection 16.1 restates the positive contribution of the paper across six layers — finite typed interaction calculus, statused profile-relativ</data></node><node id="P026-C0197"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Subsection 16.3 records the six classes of future work that the paper does not undertake.</data></node><node id="P026-C0200"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Countermodel/No-Go Scope: The fourth positive layer is the countermodel atlas of Section 9 and Appendix C. The paper records twelve finite countermodels, each blocking a specific overclaim about the calculus: active package without macro closure (CM_1), active route/holonomy without drive (CM_2), lo</data></node><node id="P026-C0201"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Required Nonclaims: This subsection records the required nonclaims of the paper, organized into six topical groupings: algebra and universality, status, package and strictness, route/refinement/locality, audit, and model. Each nonclaim is a scoped negative statement; together they fix the boundary o</data></node><node id="P026-C0202"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Algebra and Universality Nonclaims: No universal six-symbol algebra. The paper does not assert a total operation : P^ 2 → P that produces a faithful universal interaction algebra over the six primitive labels. The non-factorization of cell status through the primitive pair (Theorem 1, Section 6) blo</data></node><node id="P026-C0205"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Route, Refinement, and Locality Nonclaims: No route-mismatch-implies-drive. The paper does not assert that a P_ 3 route-mismatch witness implies a P6_ drive certificate. CM_2 of Section 9 records a route mismatch without drive, and the cohomology nonclaims of Subsubsection 13.4.4 record the correspo</data></node><node id="P026-C0207"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Model Nonclaims: No model-realization-as-universal-theorem. The paper does not assert that a model_realization verdict promotes to a theorem-grade accepted verdict, or that it proves the declared structure universally, without an admissible bridge. The fragment discipline of Subsection 13.1.2 record</data></node><node id="P026-C0208"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future Work: This subsection records the six classes of future work that the present paper does not undertake. Each class is a scoped pointer to admissible extension targets for BirdInt^ aud _ fin ; none of the entries below is a claim about results in this paper.</data></node><node id="P026-C0209"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work on the claim classifier of Subsection 11.2 includes:</data></node><node id="P026-C0219"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The block closes the boundary of BirdInt^ aud _ fin : the paper records what it asserts, what it does not assert, and what it leaves to future work.</data></node><node id="P026-C0221"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The Theorem Stack: The paper records its earned content as a layered theorem stack: Finite typed calculus and core theorems. Sections 6 and 7 record the no-total-algebra theorem (Theorem 1), the four core finite-interaction theorems (Theorems 2–5), and the nine descent/packaging/completion/strictnes</data></node><node id="P026-C0224"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The earned contribution is the calculus and what it admits — finite, typed, statused, audited, and bounded by an explicit nonclaim register.</data></node><node id="P026-C0228"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[] @ ll@ →prule [b] 0.08 primitive [b] 0.86 witness family [t] 0.08 P_ 1 [t] 0.86 descent success/failure, rewrite obstruction, closure deficit [t] 0.08 P_ 2 [t] 0.86 representability, nonrepresentability, gate, support, capacity [t] 0.08 P_ 3 [t] 0.86 route mismatch, holonomy, commutator, critical </data></node><node id="P026-C0229"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[] @ ll@ →prule [b] 0.08 primitive [b] 0.86 update/effect family [t] 0.08 P_ 1 [t] 0.86 mark descent failure, install descended update, rewrite, repair [t] 0.08 P_ 2 [t] 0.86 mark representable/nonrepresentable, gate support, update admissibility [t] 0.08 P_ 3 [t] 0.86 record route mismatch, holonom</data></node><node id="P026-C0230"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not prove that theorem here.</data></node><node id="P026-C0234"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact-Form Null-Drive (Theorem 16): Statement (recall). Let a∈ C^ 1 (G;k) be a 1 -cochain with a=dphi for some phi∈ C^ 0 (G;k) . Then for every cycle gamma∈Cycles(G) , the cycle integral satisfies _ gamma a=0 . Proof. By definition, the cycle integral is the pairing _ gamma a= a,gamma = _ e∈gamma ep</data></node><node id="P026-C0236"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gating-Affinity Suppression (Theorem 18): Statement (recall). Let G G'=(V',E') be a P_ 2 -gating that sends G to a forest, that is, beta_ 1 (G')=0 . Then H^ 1 (G';k)=0 , and the cycle-supported cohomological drive certificate P6_ drive ^ H^1 is absent-with-record on G' . Proof. By the forest no-driv</data></node><node id="P026-C0239"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A positive refinement-improvement theorem requires a P_4 -monotonicity bridge with explicit hypotheses on the refinement, the update, and the closure functional, recorded as future work in Subsection 16.3.1.</data></node><node id="P026-C0244"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-10 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0246"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NC-11 of Subsection 2.4.3 records the corresponding nonclaim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0249"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each sheet declares the host, the realization object, the realization map, the realized fragment, the actor-update map and informant-witness map (where applicable), the per-judgment realizations, the source-of-truth and provenance discipline (where applicable), the realization theorem, and the reali</data></node><node id="P026-C0252"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The PICA nonclaim register N_ PICA contains the ten nonclaims of Subsubsection 13.2.10: no PICA universality, no 6× 6 universal law, raw status not formal status, action not pair-realness, fallback not real source, ablation evidence is model-grade, P_ 3 not P6_ drive without bridge, dirty records no</data></node><node id="P026-C0253"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">with S_ aud the audited shell-stable comparison domain, T_ 0 the base cocycle-pressure theory, T_ 1 the completion-packaged theory, pi_ 0 the old cocycle-level object map, pi_ 1 the new packaged-object map, F the persistent packaged-strata family, Delta the pressure-gap consequence, A the audit/theo</data></node><node id="P026-C0255"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Realization Theorem and Nonclaims: Model Theorem 34 (Cantor Strict-Bridge Realization; model-grade). If AdmCantorShell(CantorShell_ aud ) holds, the strictness witness records a nonempty Delta_ fact ^ Cantor on S_ aud , and the gate record holds, then Gamma; H_ CantorShell ;I_ Cantor _ aud _ 0→ 1 ^ </data></node><node id="P026-C0256"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The Cantor nonclaim register N_ Cantor contains the ten nonclaims of Subsubsection 13.3.9: shell-bound only, no shell-general theorem, no broader external-class theorem, strictness not macro closure, strictness not drive, pressure gap not KL closure deficit without bridge, pressure gap not strictnes</data></node><node id="P026-C0260"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The AI nonclaim register N_ AI contains: AI is not the whole calculus, soundness is not completeness, closure does not imply descent, representability does not imply sound transformer, refinement does not automatically improve every defect, AI five-role realization does not include full P_ 3 unless </data></node><node id="P026-C0263"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The cohomology nonclaim register N_ coh contains the ten nonclaims of Subsubsection 13.4.4: P6_ drive ^ H^ 1 is one drive certificate family not all drive, P_ 3 holonomy not P6_ drive without a bridge, gating to a forest kills cycle-supported drive only, exact 1 -cochains have zero cycle drive, H^ 1</data></node><node id="P026-C0264"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Combined Nonclaims: The combined nonclaim register records ten cross-model nonclaims that hold uniformly across the four realizations: enumi . enumi . No model realization is the whole of BirdInt^ aud _ fin . PICA is not the universal six-symbol algebra. Cantor is audited-shell scoped. The abstract-</data></node><node id="P026-C0267"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The decorated-square fragment is a secondary organizational layer over the core calculus: it does not replace the directed-cell, pair-observable, promotion, claim, gate, or square status families, and it does not assert a completeness theorem for the calculus.</data></node><node id="P026-C0269"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Decorated Square Schema: A decorated square Xi∈Sq is a finite typed record Xi = ( X, F, q, F^ , src_ Xi , tgt_ Xi , type_ Xi , Decor_ Xi , A_ Xi , V_ Xi , delta_ Xi , N_ Xi ),</data></node><node id="P026-C0271"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This recovery is representational: it records the descent data handled by Theorem 30 inside H_ DC and does not assert that every descent fact in the core calculus reduces to a decorated square.</data></node><node id="P026-C0272"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it records the completion-noncommutation phenomenon at the level of H_ DC and does not assert that every completion-related fact reduces to a decorated square.</data></node><node id="P026-C0274"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">High-Structure Nonclaims: This subsection records the long-form high-structure nonclaims of Subsection 14.5.</data></node><node id="P026-C0275"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">including witness data, update data, language, visibility, threshold, audit record, defect record, square status, and nonclaim data.</data></node><node id="P026-C0276"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No General Obstruction Propagation Theorem: The pasting law of Subsubsection 14.1.3 records a syntactic aggregation rule for defects under finite strict pasting. The appendix does not claim a general theorem of the form ``a defect at one square in a finite pasting diagram propagates to a defect at e</data></node><node id="P026-C0277"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The appendix does not claim a general theorem of the form ``a defect at one square in a finite pasting diagram propagates to a defect at every square downstream of it'' or ``the absence of a defect at one square suffices for the absence of a defect at every square in the diagram.'' Both directions o</data></node><node id="P026-C0278"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">theorem-grade paper claims remain theorem-grade finite claims; model-grade paper claims remain scoped model-realization claims; Proposition 35 remains an audit-grade validation claim.</data></node><node id="P026-C0280"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem Build Order: The theorem build order is the topological order in which the paper's theorems and supporting records are introduced and proved. It is the order under which every consumer of a record is preceded by its producer. The nine-stage build order is:</data></node><node id="P026-C0281"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The build order does not claim closure under any larger dependency closure</data></node><node id="P026-C0284"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This subsection records the deferred claims by future-paper group, mirroring the six classes of future work in Subsection 16.3.</data></node><node id="P026-C0285"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each item below is not deferred to future work but explicitly excluded as a positive claim of BirdInt^ aud _ fin .</data></node><node id="P027-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds Foundations I introduced a stratified closure calculus with six primitive closure roles. The present paper extends that calculus into a theorem-level framework in three parts: a primitive role architecture fixing the meanings, levels, local boundaries, and selected lifts of the six typed c</data></node><node id="P027-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The terminal theorem, informally: A finite audited typed closure description D is a finitely presented collection of typed raw records and annotations subject to finiteness, closure, and honest-bookkeeping conditions. The role vocabulary P1–P6 enters D not as a constraint on admissibility but as a f</data></node><node id="P027-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What the paper does and does not claim: The terminal theorem is scoped exact-six, not unrestricted exact-six. It makes none of the following stronger claims: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] unrestricted exact-six, or any claim about all emergence phenomena; that every D ∈ FATCD activates all six roles or c</data></node><node id="P027-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Primitive closure roles: The six primitive closure roles are [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] P1 — update-vs-quotient compatibility / descent obstruction ; P2 — macro-specification representability ; P3 — enriched route/coherence mismatch ; P3 is not a directionality certificate; P4 — stage/order/transition</data></node><node id="P027-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Level trichotomy: For each role P1, , P6, we distinguish [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] the behavioral level: status, occurrence, or observed behavior of the role in a description; the verification level: proof, witness, certificate, or checkable record supporting a behavioral claim ; the structural-categ</data></node><node id="P027-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Typed non-collapse: Under honest bookkeeping (Definition ) and the repaired alignments of Remark , for every pair of primitive roles (P_i, P_j) with i j , the ``forbidden collapse'' of the corresponding row in Table is not admissible as an identification of P_i with P_j ; the ``permitted interaction</data></node><node id="P027-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Level profile of an admissible claim: Every admissible claim about a description D ∈ FATCD carries a level profile: each claim is located on the behavioral, verification, or structural-categorical level, and each cross-level assertion is accompanied by a translation record in the sense of Definition</data></node><node id="P027-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible visibility record: Every admissible claim carries an explicit visibility record comprising its visible content, its suppressed content, the instruments under which the visibility and suppression are asserted, and the resulting instrument-relative claim strength. Overreading a suppressed c</data></node><node id="P027-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Admissible empirical-bridge claim: An empirical-bridge claim about D ∈ FATCD is admissible only when it carries, in Audit D , [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] the substrate and observable to which the bridge relates; the instrument under which the observable is measured; the level map between the closure-al</data></node><node id="P027-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">by the same proposition it leaves the restricted typed boundary interactions available, so the standing non-claim is the global primitive-independence theorem and the full six-primitives algebra, not the absence of interaction.</data></node><node id="P027-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Raw record: A raw record is a finite typed syntactic or relational feature of a description. The admissible record kinds are [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] object and state records; relation records, map records, and partial-map records; predicate records and comparison records; path or derivation records</data></node><node id="P027-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Annotations: In addition to raw records, a description admits annotations of six declared kinds: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] level annotations Ann_L ; threshold annotations Ann_Theta ; lift annotations Ann_ Lift ; forgetting annotations Ann_ Forget ; instrument-visibility annotations Ann_ Vis ; empiric</data></node><node id="P027-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite description: A description D is finite when [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] Raw D contains finitely many records and annotations; every record or annotation carries finite typed fields; every relation, map, path, trace, or annotation is finitely represented; every reference in a record points to a d</data></node><node id="P027-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audited description: A description D is audited when every claim carried by D has an honest bookkeeping record. The collection of such records is denoted Audit D ⊆ Raw D , subject to the following discipline: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] every promotion records its source level, target level, added data</data></node><node id="P027-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Residual feature; residual classification scheme: Let D ∈ FATCD . A residual feature of D is a raw record or annotation in Raw D whose role-theoretic disposition is not fixed solely by its raw-grammar kind. The residual classification scheme assigns to each residual feature r a single label Classify</data></node><node id="P027-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Non-circularity schema for FATCD: Membership in FATCD is not defined by requiring a decomposition/exhaustion record or by requiring any derived role projection to exist. Tracked in the formalization harness as a conditional schema (SixBirds.non_circularity); see Appendix .</data></node><node id="P027-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">At the domain-definition level the scheme records the available labels but does not assert that those two categories are empty, so it cannot smuggle the exact-six answer into the definition of the domain.</data></node><node id="P027-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Non-overbreadth schema for FATCD: FATCD is structurally restricted and does not admit arbitrary syntactic material: arbitrary unaudited syntactic material cannot enter FATCD, and mentioning a role name without the supporting threshold, witness, level, collapse, and bookkeeping data does not by itsel</data></node><node id="P027-C0071"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Role-projection alignment: Under honest bookkeeping and the witness discipline of Definition , each role projection is constrained as follows. [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] P1: admissible only when the candidate cluster carries a selected update, a quotient or relation record, and a witness of descent co</data></node><node id="P027-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure of probability and uncertainty: Every admissible probability or uncertainty feature of a description D ∈ FATCD whose content takes one of the recognized forms below classifies, within the covered scope, as one of [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] a P2 projection, when the feature is a macro-specifica</data></node><node id="P027-C0074"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure of residual causality: Every admissible residual-causality feature of a description D ∈ FATCD whose content takes one of the recognized forms below classifies, within the covered scope, as one of [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] a P4 projection, as the primary classification when the feature is a di</data></node><node id="P027-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The recognition hypothesis and the classification theorem: [Recognition hypothesis] A description D ∈ FATCD satisfies the recognition hypothesis when every residual feature r ∈ Residual D is recognized: the closure content of r either supplies the witness type of one of the six role meanings of Defi</data></node><node id="P027-C0078"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Recognition hypothesis: A description D ∈ FATCD satisfies the recognition hypothesis when every residual feature r ∈ Residual D is recognized: the closure content of r either supplies the witness type of one of the six role meanings of Definition — so that r , or a finite composite of its constituen</data></node><node id="P027-C0083"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Decomposition/exhaustion record: A decomposition/exhaustion record for D ∈ FATCD is a tuple Dec D = (Raw D , (Chan i D , Status i D )_ i=1 ^ 6 , Residual D , Classify, L, V, Audit D , N ) where [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] the six role channels together with their statuses form the six-channel status se</data></node><node id="P027-C0085"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six-channel exactness and residual exhaustion: For every D ∈ FATCD and every well-formed Dec D in the sense of Definition : [label=( ),topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] the channel sequence (Chan i D )_ i=1 ^ 6 has length exactly six, one per primitive role P1, , P6; every raw feature of D is accounted for at</data></node><node id="P027-C0086"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert six active projections in every record, nor does it assert that a well-formed record is unique.</data></node><node id="P027-C0087"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Channel-versus-activation discipline: Let D ∈ FATCD and let Dec D be a well-formed decomposition/exhaustion record. The following are distinct statements: [label=( ),topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] Six channels: the channel sequence (Chan i D )_ i=1 ^ 6 has length six with the fixed role names P1, , P6. Six</data></node><node id="P027-C0088"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Decomposition records are not unique: A description D ∈ FATCD may admit more than one well-formed decomposition/exhaustion record in the sense of Definition . Distinct well-formed records may differ in level assignments, choice of witness schema, residual classification labels for features admitting</data></node><node id="P027-C0090"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each family is a guardrail that restates a nonclaim already secured by an earlier result, so each is recorded as a remark.</data></node><node id="P027-C0101"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The scoped exact-six theorem: The theorem chain of Sections – and the integrated stress certification of Section together permit the terminal statement. The scoped exact-six theorem combines the definition of FATCD with the lower bounds, the upper-bound classification, and the decomposition/exhausti</data></node><node id="P027-C0102"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scoped exact-six: Let D ∈ FATCD satisfy the recognition hypothesis (Definition ). Then D admits a well-formed decomposition/exhaustion record Dec D with exactly six typed closure-role channels: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] P1: update-vs-quotient compatibility / descent obstruction; P2: macro-specificati</data></node><node id="P027-C0105"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">An unrestricted exact-six theorem would require a domain-free argument for which the present paper does not claim to have the instruments.</data></node><node id="P027-C0107"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the associated nonclaim is that no result here rests on an instrument-free truth.</data></node><node id="P027-C0109"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This posture also explains why the endogenous-instrument meta-limit (Sections and ) is deferred to future work.</data></node><node id="P027-C0112"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope of the terminal theorem: Theorem quantifies over D ∈ FATCD , where FATCD is the finite audited typed closure description domain of Definition . The theorem asserts: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] existence of a decomposition/exhaustion record Dec D with six role channels and one status per channel; </data></node><node id="P027-C0114"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert: [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] decomposition uniqueness or canonicity</data></node><node id="P027-C0115"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Global nonclaims: Each nonclaim below is preserved throughout the paper and is enforced locally by the theorem-chain result that would otherwise overread it. [topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt] No full six-primitives algebra. Scoped exact-six is proved over FATCD; it is not claimed that P1–P6 generate a global</data></node><node id="P027-C0116"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each nonclaim below is preserved throughout the paper and is enforced locally by the theorem-chain result that would otherwise overread it.</data></node><node id="P027-C0117"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it is not claimed that P1–P6 generate a global algebra of closure operations (Remark , Remark ). it does not assert that any D ∈ FATCD is itself a substrate observation.</data></node><node id="P027-C0118"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">An adjacent meta-theoretic question, deferred to future work, is whether the accepted internal instrument family can itself certify both its soundness and its completeness for exact-six judgments formulable inside the same regime. Future work</data></node><node id="P027-C0119"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work: Theorem and the limitations of Section suggest several follow-on research directions. Each subsection below states a direction, not a commitment.</data></node><node id="P027-C0120"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A closely related open question is recognition-completeness: whether every finite audited feature of a description in FATCD reduces to the six role meanings — the recognition hypothesis on which Theorem is conditional (Remark ). establishing or refuting their reducibility is left to future work.</data></node><node id="P027-C0122"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The generator-and-relation structure of P1–P6 beyond typed non-collapse remains open.</data></node><node id="P027-C0123"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">later work can couple this to substrate-specific measurement theory while preserving the nonclaim of empirical realization.</data></node><node id="P028-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Foundations IV is a catalog of layer-agnostic structural laws built on the Foundations I/II/III apparatus. Its organizing question is what must be true, before choosing a substrate—whether analysis, arithmetic, physics, biology, chemistry, economics and finance, linguistics, cognitive and social sci</data></node><node id="P028-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Foundations IV is not a foundational reset of Foundations I/II/III, not a unification claim about the eight axes, and not an exhaustive catalog.</data></node><node id="P028-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure formation: A closure formation is the passage from initial data to the object obtained by closing that data under a declared family of closure rules. We write cl for the formed-closure operation. A formed closure is the completed object relative to the declared rules. A closure-of-record is </data></node><node id="P028-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Current and predictive quotients: Quotient packaging is the standard quotient construction: an equivalence relation is imposed on a carrier and only equivalence classes are retained. The quotient map is denoted pi when no more specific symbol is needed. The current quotient Q is the quotient present</data></node><node id="P028-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">BirdInt judgment: A BirdInt judgment is the Foundations III\ finite audited interaction judgment. In this paper it records that the interaction named in a catalog law is legitimately observable by the declared finite audit data. It is not an informal plausibility assertion; it is the inherited finit</data></node><node id="P028-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Split-pair obstruction: Let q:X→ Q be a source quotient, F:X→ Y a map, and r:Y→ R a target identification. The split-pair obstruction is Delta(q,F,r) = \ (x,x')∈ X× X q(x)=q(x')\ and\ rF(x) rF(x')\ . It is the set of pairs identified by the source quotient but separated after transport to the target</data></node><node id="P028-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Observation signature; access quotient: An observation signature is a declared map sigma_I:H→ Sigma from a carrier H to observation symbols. It determines the access kernel h _I h' Longleftrightarrow sigma_I(h)=sigma_I(h'). The access quotient q_I is the quotient of H by this kernel. It is the quoti</data></node><node id="P028-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Normal-form template: Each catalog law is presented by a six-part normal-form template: setup, theorem, proof spine, case enumeration, interaction status, and nonclaims. The setup names the data on which the law operates. The theorem states the layer-agnostic claim. The proof spine gives the structu</data></node><node id="P028-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert that the laws are equivalent or that the axes are canonical.</data></node><node id="P028-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Observation signature: Let H be a closure carrier. An observation signature on H is a declared map sigma_I:H→Sigma from states to observation symbols. Its access kernel is the equivalence relation h _I h' Longleftrightarrow sigma_I(h)=sigma_I(h'), and its access quotient is the quotient map q_I:H→ Q</data></node><node id="P028-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Adequacy / No-Overread: Let F:H→ W be a current-layer claim over a formed access package. If F is accepted as an exact current claim, then it is constant on the fibers of q_I . Equivalently, there is a map F:Q_I→ W such that F= F _I .</data></node><node id="P028-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-Free-Distinction: A functorial gate using only the existing access quotient cannot produce a strict new current distinction. If a readout d:H→ D is current-visible without additional apparatus data, then _ dist (q_I,d)= , equivalently d= d _I for some d:Q_I→ D . If the obstruction is nonempty, th</data></node><node id="P028-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Hiddenness interface: A hiddenness interface I consists of a closure carrier H_I , a current quotient _I: H_I→ Q_I , a predictive quotient _I: H_I→ M_I refining _I , and the following predicates: determiningStateTyped(I), minimalRelevantUpstream(I), lawfulCurrentExposure(I), together with QuotientEx</data></node><node id="P028-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Hiddenness Normal Form: Let I be a hiddenness interface and let src: Paper7SaturatedSATInstance(I) be the opaque saturated-SAT source certificate. Then determiningStateTyped(I), minimalRelevantUpstream(I), lawfulCurrentExposure(I) iff QuotientExposed(I), PredictiveCollapse(I) iff lawfulCurrentExposu</data></node><node id="P028-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This theorem does not re-prove the saturated-SAT closure result of the PvNP paper , and it does not assert any conclusion about a specific computational complexity class.</data></node><node id="P028-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Diagnosis source data: Diagnosis source data for a closure carrier consists of a certified source predicate saying that the carrier has the declared measurement context, record update discipline, and stability conditions required for a formed predictive closure. In the CSL case, the source predicate</data></node><node id="P028-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">CSL Formed-Closure Law: Let FPC be the class of formed predictive closures C , each carrying a hiddenness interface with current quotient Q and predictive quotient M , together with four declared side-condition predicates genuinelyFormed( C), saturatedCurrentizers( C), determiningStateDeclared( C), </data></node><node id="P028-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This subsection does not instantiate the CSL diagnosis source, does not assert a measurement-theoretic contextuality theorem at the Foundations IV\ layer, and does not close the source obligation unconditionally.</data></node><node id="P028-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Layer Multiplicity: Let H be a history space, let q:H→ Q be a surjective access quotient, and let s:H→ S be a declared role readout. Define the role obstruction and joint role-layer quotient by O_s := \ (h,h')∈ H^2:q(h)=q(h')\ and\ s(h) s(h')\ , q_s(h):=(q(h),s(h)). Then s\ descends through\ q iff O</data></node><node id="P028-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Layer Multiplicity does not classify the number of layers in a carrier, does not prescribe how layers must be presented, and does not assert that multiplicity is finite in every setting.</data></node><node id="P028-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-Needles Stability: Let P be a formed stability package with a layer-dissolving membrane. Suppose P declares: a currency block C on its exterior carrier of size e ; a layer-bridge block L of size y× e and a dissolving-direction block D of size z× e , with block currency pieces K_ LL , K_ LD , K_ D</data></node><node id="P028-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sufficiency Closure: The current quotient Q is sufficient for the declared future family F if and only if every declared future probe f∈ F descends through q . Equivalently, for every f:H→ F_f in F there is a map f:Q→ F_f such that f= f q .</data></node><node id="P028-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict Extension: The extension from q_ old to q_ new is strict if and only if there is a retention map rho: _ new → _ old , q_ old =rho q_ new , and there are histories h,h'∈ H such that q_ old (h)=q_ old (h') but q_ new (h) q_ new (h').</data></node><node id="P028-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No Unstatused Residual: Let R be a residual type, let P be a formed stability package over R , and let st_ P :R S be the declared typed-status classifier supplied by the package. Assume the package discipline makes st_ P single-valued on native residual records. Then r∈ R, st_ P (r)∈ S_ typed , r∈ R</data></node><node id="P028-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The interaction status is BirdInt -compatible because the residual record is part of the audited interaction data: the package must say whether the residual is visible, hidden with a record, outside scope, blocked, priced, or accepted.</data></node><node id="P028-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Descent-Repair Normal Form: Let q:X→ Q be a quotient map, let F:X→ Y be a map, and let r:Y→ R be a target readout. Then r F descends through q if and only if Delta(q,F,r)=∅ . If the obstruction is nonempty, the canonical repairs are: source refinement, which separates the offending source fibers, an</data></node><node id="P028-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Holonomy-Memory Repair Normal Form: The second transport law concerns route dependence: two routes can be indistinguishable at the current quotient while still leaving a predictive difference. [Route residue] Let H be a carrier, let gamma, :H→ Z be two declared routes, let Q:Z→ Q be the current quot</data></node><node id="P028-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Route residue: Let H be a carrier, let gamma, :H→ Z be two declared routes, let Q:Z→ Q be the current quotient, and let M:Z→ M be a predictive quotient refining the current readout. The route residue of gamma and at h∈ H is present when Q(gamma h)=Q( h) but M(gamma h) ( h).</data></node><node id="P028-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Holonomy-Memory Repair Normal Form: For two declared routes gamma, :H→ Z , current quotient Q , and predictive quotient M , memory is forced exactly when there is route residue. Equivalently, if two route outputs agree at Q but differ at M , then no representation through the current quotient alone </data></node><node id="P028-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Local–Global Obstruction Normal Form: The third transport law is the local-to-global image criterion. It separates overlap tests from genuine global existence. [Local-global compatibility] Let I index a declared finite patch system, with local object sets (Q_i)_ i∈ I . Let C⊆ _i Q_i be the subset of</data></node><node id="P028-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Local-Global Obstruction Normal Form: Let G be a set of global states, let F be a set of local-family data, let Comp: F→Prop be the overlap-compatibility predicate, and let R: G F be a declared restriction map such that Comp(R(g)) for every g∈ G . For a∈ F , write Globalizes_R(a) :Longleftrightarrow</data></node><node id="P028-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bridge composition: Let B be the carrier of branch data, let K:B→ Q_K be the branchwise quotient, and let R:B→ Q_R be the recombination quotient. The bridge composition is the use of K as the source quotient and R as the target readout in the split-pair criterion of . Its obstruction is \ (b,b')∈ B×</data></node><node id="P028-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Involutive ledger; anti-invariant readout: An involutive ledger is a carrier X equipped with an involution J:X→ X , so J^2=id . Its fixed locus is Fix(J)=\ x∈ X:J(x)=x\ . An anti-invariant readout is a quantity whose sign changes under J . It is separating when zero trace against the anti-invariant </data></node><node id="P028-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Duality Fixity / Self-Dual Trace Confinement: Let a formed closure carry an involutive ledger with separating anti-invariant readout and trace functional tr . Assume a source instance supplying the self-dual trace-confinement bridge. If a completed domination ladder satisfies A_X B_n and tr(B_n)→ 0,</data></node><node id="P028-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Selection Obstruction / Repair Normal Form: [Selection obstruction] Let a declared symmetry group G act on a carrier X , and let p:X→ X/G be the orbit quotient. For a selectedness predicate :X→\ 0,1\ , the selection obstruction is the split-pair set Delta(p, ) = \ (x,x')∈ X× X: p(x)=p(x')\ and\ (x) </data></node><node id="P028-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Selection obstruction: Let a declared symmetry group G act on a carrier X , and let p:X→ X/G be the orbit quotient. For a selectedness predicate :X→\ 0,1\ , the selection obstruction is the split-pair set Delta(p, ) = \ (x,x')∈ X× X: p(x)=p(x')\ and\ (x) (x')\ .</data></node><node id="P028-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Selection Obstruction / Repair Normal Form: Let G act on a set X and on an orbit set O by rho_X:G× X→ X and rho_O:G× O→ O . Let pi:X→ O be a surjective orbit map, let s:X→\ 0,1\ be a selectedness predicate, let sigma:O→ X be a section, and let b:X→ B be a breaker readout. Define O_s := \ (x,x')∈ X^2</data></node><node id="P028-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking / Branch-Selection Formation: Let C be a closure space with a G -action, let Omega: C→Prop be the ground-state predicate, and let B= C/G be the branch quotient. Suppose a future branch-selection formation source is given for ( C,G,Omega) . Then there is a formation oper</data></node><node id="P028-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Anomaly / Symmetry Obstruction: Let G act on a history space H by alpha:G× H→ H , let q:H→ Q be a surjective quotient, let alpha:G× Q→ Q be a candidate descended action, let D:G× H→\ 0,1\ be a domain predicate, and let r:H→ V be a readout with value action rho:G× V→ V . Define O_ alpha := \ (g,h,h')</data></node><node id="P028-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The theorem does not classify anomalies into carrier-specific forms and does not assert anything about non-symmetric gates.</data></node><node id="P028-C0052"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The theorem does not classify symmetry presentations and does not assert a unique role presentation beyond observable equivalence.</data></node><node id="P028-C0053"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Objectivity as Common Quotient: Let H be a carrier with a family of access quotients q_i:H→ Q_i indexed by i∈ I , a public quotient p:H→ P , and a target readout r:H→ R . Assume each q_i is surjective, p is surjective, and the family satisfies the common-quotient universal property CommonQuotientUni</data></node><node id="P028-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lawhood Descent: Let a formed closure have available access quotient q_I:H→ Q . A declared candidate law P is lawful at the layer if and only if it factors through q_I ; for a predicate, this means P(h)=P(h') whenever q_I(h)=q_I(h') .</data></node><node id="P028-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Object Persistence: Let H_i,H_j be carriers with access quotients q_i:H_i→ Q_i and q_j:H_j→ Q_j , and let gamma:H_i→ H_j be a declared transport. Assume q_i and q_j are surjective. Let viable:Q_j→Prop be a viability predicate, C:H_i× H_j→Prop a relation predicate, and let gamma:Q_i→ Q_j, :Q_j→ Q_k, </data></node><node id="P028-C0056"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Record formation: In a formed closure cl , a fact is a claim candidate that is closure-of-record and whose evidence descends through the available access. A record is the audited witness, carried by the BirdInt ledger, that the claim and evidence have formed at the declared layer.</data></node><node id="P028-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Fact / Record Formation: Let H_0,H_1,H_s,H_t be history spaces with event readout :H_0→ E , continuation gamma:H_0→ H_1 , record readouts rho_1:H_1→ R_1, rho_s:H_s→ R_s, rho_t:H_t→ R_t, transport :H_s→ H_t , descended transport :R_s→ R_t , fact-value maps d_s:R_s→ E_s , d_t:R_t→ E_t , update u:E_s→ </data></node><node id="P028-C0059"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Therefore any claim of complete same-layer self-soundness lies outside the scope of pi .</data></node><node id="P028-C0060"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete self-attestation by the issuer is outside the scope of the regime .</data></node><node id="P028-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete in-certificate self-trust is outside the scope of the protocol .</data></node><node id="P028-C0062"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Interface Mediation: Let q:X→ Q be a source quotient, let beta:Y→ B be a boundary readout, let U:X× Y→ X' be an interface update, and let q':X'→ Q' be the target quotient. Assume q , beta , and q' are surjective. Define O_U := \ ((x,y),(x',y')) : q(x)=q(x'),\ beta(y)=beta(y'),\ q'(U(x,y)) q'(U(x',y'</data></node><node id="P028-C0066"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conservation as Orbit Descent: A quantity C:X→ V is conserved for a declared route or symmetry structure if and only if it descends through the orbit quotient; that is, C(x)=C(x') whenever x and x' lie in the same declared orbit.</data></node><node id="P028-C0068"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Presentation Invariance: Let P be a presentation space with quotient pi:P→Pi , let be an equivalence relation compatible with pi , let s:Pi→ P be a section with pi(s(x))=x , and let T:P→ V be a readout. Let tau:Tuple(P)→Tuple(Pi) be the tuple quotient, let R:Tuple(P)→Prop be a relation, and let a gr</data></node><node id="P028-C0072"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Horizon Sufficiency / Holographic Compression: Let H be a set of histories with an interior–exterior decomposition H I × E , and define pi : I × E B × Q, pi(i,e)=(beta(i), (e)), where beta:I→ B is the boundary readout and :E→ Q is the exterior classifier. Assume pi is surjective, and let t:I× E→ T b</data></node><node id="P028-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not assert the AdS/CFT correspondence or any particular gravitational realization.</data></node><node id="P028-C0074"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Information Loss Normal Form: [Information Loss Normal Form] Let H_0,H_1 be source and later carriers with transport tau:H_0→ H_1 . Let sigma:H_0→ I be source information, rho:H_1→ R a recovery quotient, q_0:H_0→ Q_0 a source quotient, q_1:H_1→ Q_1 a later quotient, and s:H_0→ S a declared signature</data></node><node id="P028-C0075"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Information Loss Normal Form: Let H_0,H_1 be source and later carriers with transport tau:H_0→ H_1 . Let sigma:H_0→ I be source information, rho:H_1→ R a recovery quotient, q_0:H_0→ Q_0 a source quotient, q_1:H_1→ Q_1 a later quotient, and s:H_0→ S a declared signature. Assume that rho tau , q_0 , a</data></node><node id="P028-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Singular Limit Completion: Let P be a set of limiting processes, let :P→ Q be a surjective limit quotient, let j:Q→ Q be a completion map, let u:P→ T be the target-limit readout, let sigma:P→ S be a declared signature-limit readout, let u^ ren :P→ T^ ren be a declared renormalized target-limit reado</data></node><node id="P028-C0078"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complementarity as Non-Joint Access: Let H be a carrier with two access quotients q_A:H→ Q_A and q_B:H→ Q_B . A joint access is a quotient j:H→ J together with maps a:J→ Q_A and b:J→ Q_B satisfying a j=q_A, b j=q_B . Assume q_A and q_B are separately admissible. Then q_A _ acc q_B iff (J: Type)(j:H→</data></node><node id="P028-C0084"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Locality / Domain of Dependence: Let H be a history set with local quotient :H→ L , target readout t:H→ T , signature sigma:H→Sigma , update map u:H→ H' , and target quotient q':H'→ Q' . Assume is surjective. Define O_ (t)= \ (h,h'): (h)= (h')\ and\ t(h) t(h')\ . Then LocalToDomain( ,t) iff O_ (t)=∅</data></node><node id="P028-C0085"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Law–State Split: Let X be a closure space with moduli quotient mu: X→ M , readout r: X→ V , and descended readout r:M→ V satisfying r mu=r . Let tau: X→ X_2 be a transport and mu_2: X_2→ M_2 a target moduli quotient. Then r respects mu iff x,y∈ X,\ mu(x)=mu(y) implies r(x)=r(y), LawReadout(r) iff m,</data></node><node id="P028-C0088"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Common-Source / Nonlocal Correlation Normal Form: The famous physical instance of correlations that admit no local common-source explanation is the Einstein–Podolsky–Rosen / Bell situation ; the layer-agnostic statement below records the descent-to-common-source obstruction abstracted from that subs</data></node><node id="P028-C0089"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Common-Source / Nonlocal Correlation Normal Form: Let H be a history space with source quotient sigma:H→ S , shared interface :H→ I , access quotients q_A:H→ L_A , q_B:H→ L_B , and readouts r_A:H→ A , r_B:H→ B . Let :H→ K be the correlation readout. Then CommonSource(sigma) iff r_A:S→ A, r_B:S→ B,\ </data></node><node id="P028-C0091"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Unification as Common Refinement: Let C_U, C_A, C_B be parent and child closures with quotients q_U: C_U→ Q_U , q_A: C_A→ Q_A , and q_B: C_B→ Q_B . Let _A: C_U→ C_A and _B: C_U→ C_B be interpretations, and let pi_A:Q_U→ Q_A , pi_B:Q_U→ Q_B be quotient projections. For claim sets K_A,K_B,K_U , lifts </data></node><node id="P028-C0093"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure Completeness Boundary: Let R be a residual type with predicates hasStatus:R→Prop and adequate:R→Prop . Let D be a direction type with predicates inScope:D→Prop , claimed:D→Prop , and namedOutside:D→Prop , and let v∈\ accepted,open,refused\ be a meta-audit verdict. Then ResidualCoverage iff r</data></node><node id="P028-C0094"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reflective closure operator: The AOR apparatus assigns to a carrier C over a scope S a reflective closure Cl_ SB (C) , a unit _ SB (C):C Cl_ SB (C), and a residual Xi_ SB (C) . The object Cl_ SB (C) is the audited closure completion of C ; the unit compares the raw carrier with that completion; and </data></node><node id="P028-C0095"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Direct CER no-go: Let S be a declared scope, let C be a class of carriers, let BareReal_ S : C→Prop be bare physical realisability, and let Cl_ S : C→ C be the formal closure operator over S . Under the reduct-obstruction hypothesis there exists a carrier C∈ C such that BareReal_ S (C) and C Cl_ S (</data></node><node id="P028-C0098"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sketches and future work</data></node><node id="P028-C0099"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sketches and future work: This chapter records pointers, not new laws. The preceding chapters state the 52\ laws that belong to the Foundations IV\ catalog. Here we record structural candidates that appeared in the atlas work but were not promoted to catalog rows. The reason is not that the candidat</data></node><node id="P028-C0100"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Foundations IV\ does not claim that the 52-row catalog is complete under future developments.</data></node><node id="P028-C0101"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaims and caveats: This chapter is the consolidated nonclaims landing for Foundations IV. The per-axis chapters state the local caveats where they matter; here the scope fence and the prepared objection mitigations are collected in one place. [label=N ., leftmargin=*] Foundations IV\ does not re</data></node><node id="P028-C0102"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not assert that the axes form a categorical, fibrational, monadic, or otherwise privileged structure.</data></node><node id="P028-C0103"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The contribution is the uniform normal-form treatment of 52\ layer-agnostic laws, together with explicit proof, provenance, and nonclaim discipline.</data></node><node id="P028-C0104"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the source formulation remains future work.</data></node><node id="P029-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The Six Birds corpus through certifies states: each of its laws fixes a single map, quotient, package, or declared finite family and proves a static classification about it. Nothing in the corpus certifies runs — liveness (every orbit eventually earns a certificate), amortized or transfinite cost ov</data></node><node id="P029-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper solves no open problem and claims none: Collatz, Erd o s–Straus, Lychrel, Recam\'an, the Langton's-ant highway, Gilbreath, and the exact chromatic number of the plane all remain open, and each law's nonclaims say so explicitly.</data></node><node id="P029-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper solves no open problem and claims none: Collatz, Erd o s–Straus, Lychrel, Recam\'an, the Langton's-ant highway, Gilbreath, and the exact chromatic number of the plane all remain open, and each law's nonclaims say so explicitly.</data></node><node id="P029-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Promotion, SAU, and the boundary certificate.: Promotion is passage to a richer layer — C from R , ordinals from N , the 2 -adics from the odd integers — via a recorded bridge. The SAU pattern (Strict Audited Utility): a promoted object U that does not descend may still legally solve a lower-layer p</data></node><node id="P029-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A membrane is a budget condition holding at every accepted stage, and its failure is a critical pair with exactly five lawful repairs (declared nonclaim, raised budget, rewritten carrier/audit, genuinely new probes, completed transport family) — same-package repetition is not a repair.</data></node><node id="P029-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Residual accounting uses the AOR status vocabulary , including the four asymptotic statuses recalled in , and the corpus nonclaim register is in force throughout — most prominently: route mismatch never implies drive content (G8's bridge is conditional on its named hypotheses, which is legal)</data></node><node id="P029-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Methodology: eccentric problems as theorem detectors: This section has no counterpart in Foundations IV, and the addition is deliberate: the catalog's laws were not invented and then illustrated, they were mined, and the mining procedure is a first-class contribution of this paper. The raw material </data></node><node id="P029-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Run the candidate against the full Foundations IV catalog (exact statements, not summaries), the Foundations V laws, the nonclaim register, and the AOR status list. nonclaim compatibility (a law that needs an unconditional version of a forbidden inference is dead)</data></node><node id="P029-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Hidden Amortized Solvency (G1) is the liveness law and the flagship of the catalog: it splits the eventual-solvency question into an unconditional reduction and a conditional discharge schema whose named hypotheses are exactly what remains open for Collatz.</data></node><node id="P029-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Hidden Amortized Solvency, Part (a): bad-tail reduction: Pointwise liveness is equivalent to emptiness of the infinite bad-tail intersection: ( x ∈ X A ,\ k 1 :\ V_k(x) &gt; 0 ) Longleftrightarrow _ k 1 N_k = . Points of A are terminally discharged before the membrane test.</data></node><node id="P029-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Hidden Amortized Solvency, Part (b): ghost-discharge schema: Assume: [H-G1-ghost-convergence] for a computable neighborhood system U_h(Gamma) in X with _h U_h(Gamma) = Gamma : every infinite bad thread based at a nonterminal native point has a limit in X , every such limit lies in Gamma , and hence </data></node><node id="P029-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">liveness remains open because both boxed hypotheses are unproved .</data></node><node id="P029-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Transfinite Escrow: Assume: [H-G2-stage-coherence] for every nonterminal step of the run, e_ k+1 (pi_ k+1 (T_k(x_k)) ) &lt; e_k (pi_k(x_k) ); (W, &lt;) is well-founded. Then no infinite nonterminal run exists: every run reaches A in finitely many steps. When W carries an effective height extractor for the</data></node><node id="P029-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Amortized Potential Currency: For every finite run prefix s_0 → → s_n , _ i=1 ^ n a_i = _ i=1 ^ n a_i + Phi(s_0) - Phi(s_n) _ i=1 ^ n a_i + Phi(s_0), the inequality because Phi(s_n) 0 . Consequently, if a declared class of runs has a_i B for every step, then every length- n prefix satisfies _ i=1 ^ </data></node><node id="P029-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carry-Horizon Confinement: No-go schema. Assume [H-G5-unbounded-horizon] for every fixed depth m there are a step j and states y, z in the declared carrier with q_m(y) = q_m(z) but different declared future probe values — a nonempty step-indexed split-pair obstruction Delta(q_m, R_b^ j , r_j) ≠ . Th</data></node><node id="P029-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For base 10, no analogous certificate is known: 196 remains open, and finite searches, however deep, are bounded evidence only .</data></node><node id="P029-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Endogenous Needle Generation: Covering schema. Assume [H-G6-dominant-pressure] there are a finite exception set E ⊆ X , a cofinal exhaustion W_1 ⊆ W_2 ⊆ with _M W_M = X E , and for each M a finite block decomposition of times after some N_M such that whenever H_n(W_M) is nonempty at the start of a b</data></node><node id="P029-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Adversarial Mobility Confinement: Fix a board, mobility rule, and devil budget, and consider: [H-G7-escape-strategy] a declared angel strategy sigma_A from finite visible histories to legal moves, with an invariant I_n(H_n) and a proof that against every legal devil strategy, sigma_A preserves I_n a</data></node><node id="P029-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Odometer Abelianization: Let (C, V, \ m_v\ , L) be a move system with initial configuration x , and assume: [H-G8] (abelian compatibility) for every configuration c and all distinct sites v ≠ w : if L_v(c) and L_w(c) both hold, then firing either move preserves legality of the other, the two composi</data></node><node id="P029-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The claim is conditional on that gate and on termination</data></node><node id="P029-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Defect-Evacuation-to-Transport: Let a run of an extended local system carry the setup data above. Positive schema. Assume: [H-G9-census] the run carries an exact defect ledger, and there is a finite N such that for all n N the unresolved defect measure is non-increasing after accounting for bound an</data></node><node id="P029-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The highway conjecture — that every finite initial configuration reaches the 104 -step highway — remains open</data></node><node id="P029-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lossful Boundary Persistence: Assume: [lossful] D is not injective; [H-G10-protect] for every state x : I(x) b(D(x)) = rho(b(x)) — the exact functional relationship, with no unstated target value; [H-G10-regen] for every state x : I(x) I(D(x)) ; [initial invariant] I(x_0) . Then for every n 0 : I(x_</data></node><node id="P029-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Local-Rule Global-Anti-Symmetry: Assume: [H-G11-hierarchy] every globally admissible C -configuration carries the declared hierarchy uniquely at every scale; the hierarchy is locally forced by the rules of C ; and for every nonzero period vector p some scale k has forced marker or block structure th</data></node><node id="P029-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite Witness Radiation: Assume [H-G12-finite-witness] some finite W ⊆ X has a valid cert_k(W) proving (G[W]) &gt; k . Then X has no proper k -coloring: a global coloring would restrict to a proper k -coloring of G[W] , contradicting the certificate; hence (X, E) &gt; k . Assume also [H-G12-homogeneity] </data></node><node id="P029-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Thin-Orbit Saturation: This is a schema with imported mathematical payload. Assume: [H-G13-thin] Lambda is Zariski dense in the relevant algebraic group with [G_ Z : Lambda] = ∈fty ; [H-G13-expansion] the congruence quotient family has a uniform spectral gap for the declared generators; [H-G13-congr</data></node><node id="P029-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cluster-A currency-to-status bridge: (A) C7 solvency. Fix the G1 data (T, &lt;, A) and a nonterminal point x , and let badtail_j(x) ∈ \ 0, 1\ indicate membership x ∈ N_j in the j -th bad-tail membrane. Then pointwise liveness at x — some k 1 with V_k(x) &gt; 0 — holds if and only if badtail_j(x) is eventu</data></node><node id="P029-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nothing is deferred here — the disposition is final unless a genuinely temporal residual is someday exhibited.</data></node><node id="P029-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaims and caveats: Nonclaims are load-bearing in this series: a law's value depends on what it declines to assert as much as on what it proves. The individual law sections keep their prose focused on setup, statement, and proof; this section consolidates the caveats, in four registers, and close</data></node><node id="P029-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Open problems, left open.: This paper solves no open problem and reports no progress on one. Specifically: the Collatz conjecture remains open (G1's Part (b) hypotheses [H-G1-ghost-convergence] and [H-G1-separation] are named obligations, and the 2^L - 1 shadowing family is evidence against fixed-de</data></node><node id="P029-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper solves no open problem and reports no progress on one. Specifically: the Collatz conjecture remains open (G1's Part (b) hypotheses [H-G1-ghost-convergence] and [H-G1-separation] are named obligations, and the 2^L - 1 shadowing family is evidence against fixed-depth strategies, not evidenc</data></node><node id="P029-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Each law is exactly as conditional as the corpus's nonclaim register requires, and several hypotheses are load-bearing by design.</data></node><node id="P030-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The structural laws of the Six Birds corpus through Foundations IV are stated from the standpoint of an external theorist: the theorist declares a lens, detects an obstruction, installs a repair, prices a residual, and runs the audit. This paper makes the closure apparatus part of the carrier. An en</data></node><node id="P030-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theory package and formed object: A theory package is T=(Z,f,Sigma_f,E, A), where Z is a finite carrier, f a declared lens, Sigma_f its definability structure, E a packaging or completion endomap, and A an audit. A formed object is a declared fixed point of the completion, with the associated record</data></node><node id="P030-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Imported primitive roles and theorem surfaces: The repair generator and later proof spines use the six primitive roles from Foundations I–III. They are not six psychological faculties or six biological mechanisms; they are typed structural moves. 0.94 @ lX@ →prule Role Imported structural reading P1</data></node><node id="P030-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six-part normal form: Each E-law is presented in the following order: Setup; Theorem; Proof spine; Case enumeration; Interaction status; Nonclaims. The layer-instantiation paragraph that follows is not a seventh proof field. It identifies candidate realizations and the bridge data each must provide.</data></node><node id="P030-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Repair join: Order quotients by refinement: Q_1 Q_0 when Q_1 is finer than Q_0 . For quotients Q,R on a common carrier, the repair join Q R is the coarsest quotient refining both. Source repair by R is the move Q↦ Q R .</data></node><node id="P030-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predictive surplus: For current quotient Q , declared predictive baseline M , and horizon tau , first require the certified order comparison CD_tau(M) CD_tau(Q). For rational scores define S_tau(QVert M):=CD_tau(Q)-CD_tau(M), which is nonnegative exactly under that comparison. In the operator realiz</data></node><node id="P030-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carried record: A record rho is carried by a system S under scope C if: rho is a coordinate of the carrier state rather than an analyst annotation; its updates occur in the support of S 's transition kernel; and the accepting source record is generated by S 's trajectory under the declared scope, ne</data></node><node id="P030-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E-system: An endogenous closure system, or E-system, is S=( T,I_S,Lambda_S, R_S), where T is a formed package, I_S a carried instrument, Lambda_S a carried ledger of budgets, prices, and statused residuals, and R_S a carried generator mapping certified defect records to candidate typed P1–P5 repair </data></node><node id="P030-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closed-loop scope: A challenge history H is closed-loop for S when CompleteChallengeAuditHistory(H) and every repair-typed occurrence in its complete audit inventory is carried in the sense of D3. External repair may occur in the run and must remain in the complete audit, but its occurrence makes th</data></node><node id="P030-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Probe economy: A probe economy consists of a finite candidate catalog P , a carried active family L_t⊆ P , an exposure budget B_ exp ∈Lambda_S , and three typed moves: allocation within L_t , acquisition of a strict extension L_t↦[L_t;M] , and retirement. Acquisition is strict when (L_t,M) ; in the </data></node><node id="P030-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Repair-join semilattice laws: Let q:X→ A , r:X→ B , and s:X→ C be quotient maps, represented by their fibers. Then: q r q and q r r ; if t q and t r , then t q r ; q q is fiber-equivalent to q ; q r is fiber-equivalent to r q ; and (q r) s is fiber-equivalent to q (r s) ; and replacing q or r by a f</data></node><node id="P030-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Carried-source exclusion: Let rho have fine source tag sigma , carrier-generation flag g , scope flag i , carrier-coordinate evidence, and a declared trajectory whose steps lie in the carrier kernel. Then rho is carried only if sigma∈\ committed_state, audited_cell_records\ , g=i=true. In particular</data></node><node id="P030-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lawful D4 repair step: For pre-state z , post-state z' , defect record d , generated move m= R_S(d) , and audit record a , a lawful repair step requires: carried evidence for d , m , and a ; detection of d by the active carried instrument; instrument permission for the move's repair sort; membership</data></node><node id="P030-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Repair-step conjuncts are necessary: Every D4 lawful repair step entails detection, gate permission, a kernel transition, and re-audit. Removing any one of those four records makes the same candidate step unlawful.</data></node><node id="P030-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Probe-move separation: For a lawful D6 probe economy: allocation preserves active support and cannot activate a new probe; acquisition adds a catalog probe and requires (L,M) ; retirement requires the probe to be active before the move and absent afterward; and same-family saturation prevents lawful</data></node><node id="P030-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not prove that this joint access is in an admissible access class.</data></node><node id="P030-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E1: Internalization: Under the Setup and the closed-loop, binding, no-free-distinction, finite-forcing, promotion-soundness, no-schedule-trap, empty Delta_ endo , complete-status, and family-budget certificates detailed below, branch elimination certifies the carried repair family \ rho_t\ contained</data></node><node id="P030-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Detailed theorem family and proof: The compact statement above factors into four mechanized results. Their existing hypotheses should remain visible rather than being hidden in a new paper-level certificate. They are: closed-loop scope; persistence with discharge; binding challenge by the declared h</data></node><node id="P030-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Positive normal form.: E1_Internalization does not construct a repair family from raw recurrence. The endogenous branch of CompleteInternalizationStatus already contains EndogenousRepairFamilyValid; the theorem eliminates the other four branches and projects that witness. For each challenged episode</data></node><node id="P030-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E2: Bounded Reflexivity: A carried self-audit claim whose subject and accepting instrument occupy the same level and which has no level-shift bridge is not accepted. For an audited repair claim with the supplied complete stack, non-saturation, FIII extension, and fresh-bridge certificates, the forma</data></node><node id="P030-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Detailed theorem family and proof: E2 uses a carried tower, not an existential hierarchy that happens to fit in the carrier. A level record contains a level index, a strictly increasing tag, the instrument record at that level, and the set of lower tags it audits. CompleteCarriedInstrumentTower requ</data></node><node id="P030-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E3: Self-Maintaining Reclosure: Persistence of an endogenous package has two separately typed stability conditions. In challenge-free operation they have the form E_t^2 E_t _ object-level closure , App_ t+1 = m(z_t), d_ app (App_ t+1 ,App_t) _ carried apparatus reinstatement and stability . Under ch</data></node><node id="P030-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim the untested reverse nonimplication.</data></node><node id="P030-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E4: Repair Compilation: A lawful compilation candidate R K_ ^ compiled requires a certified top-down channel, a passing memory-only comparator, saturation strictness, and a carried annotation or decompilation record. The compiled case additionally carries independent descent and post-compilation sil</data></node><node id="P030-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Detailed theorem family and proof: A compilation candidate begins with a window containing at least two carried invocations of the same attributed repair. Obstruction reduction and stability of the repair payload's idempotence defect are separate supplied predicates, not fields certified by the wind</data></node><node id="P030-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lawfulness certificate.: CompilationLawful is the conjunction of five independent gates: the lower rewrite is tied to a certified top-down channel; the FIII promotion record is accepted; a memory-only carrier fails to reproduce the descent; the repair is not same-family saturated; and the compiled o</data></node><node id="P030-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E5: Reclosure Collapse: An E-system is collapsed relative to C exactly when no move in the complete admissible carried inventory restores viability descent. It is irreversibly collapsed when, in addition, the repair generator is unreachable, the apparatus-level viability kernel is empty, and the rea</data></node><node id="P030-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Failure to prove irreversibility alone does not prove recoverability.</data></node><node id="P030-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E6: Attention: For a finite convex access host with positive costs, a supplied KKT witness, a binding exposure budget, and genuine scarcity, the certified optimal allocation has lambda_ exp &gt;0 : selected probes equalize marginal residual discharge per marginal cost at lambda_ exp , while unselected </data></node><node id="P030-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Detailed optimization theorem and proof: The finite convex host assumption abbreviates substantial data: finite active support; a nonempty convex feasible polytope; a convex residual (or concave discharge) objective; differentiability or a declared subgradient; positive carried marginal costs on eve</data></node><node id="P030-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E7.1 — Alarm Fatigue (unformalized corollary): The source catalog records a further consequence that is not part of the E7 Lean module. Suppose viability-coupled positive excess persists across a declared horizon, the preemption channel has finite carried capacity, and the total discharge available </data></node><node id="P030-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A general theorem translating the E2 carrier-capacity bound into the E7.1 discharge bound remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E8: Control-Price: For a declared component claim with linked carried signal, positive-cost KKT witness, and complete control-price status, the component_lawful case identifies a shadow-price component aligned with lambda_j=- Phi^* b_j when the envelope derivative exists on the binding set, and pass</data></node><node id="P030-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E8.1: Audit capture: If the carrier may raise its own residual budget Omega , budget inflation can coincide with a healthy ledger report while the original-lineage blind spot remains positive. A lawful system must therefore distinguish discharge from lineage-changing budget repair. Capture evidence </data></node><node id="P030-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A theorem from E8 shadow prices to offline allocation remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E9: Curiosity: For a finite nonempty candidate inventory that is sound and complete for admissibility, a maximum exists for any supplied rational acquisition discharge divided by supplied cost. In the Xi paper-host specialization set the discharge equal to the conditional trace contraction and requi</data></node><node id="P030-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E10: Cognitive Demarcation: Positive cognitive-package evidence is exactly repair descent, certified difference-making, and carried provenance for the same package/class claim. Under a complete status record, the package receives cognitive status only when that positive evidence is present and all s</data></node><node id="P030-C0051"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E10.1: Intention: Positive intention evidence consists of a carried package that, before the outcome, strictly restricts action support toward a declared target class, with the restriction top-down-channel certified and coherent under every member of a nonempty carried declared future-probe list. Un</data></node><node id="P030-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">These are direct operational instantiations, conditional on the comparator and inventory certificates.</data></node><node id="P030-C0056"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The proposed bridge from planning/execution restriction to E11's institutional comparison remains open and is not used below.</data></node><node id="P030-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E11: Institutional Rewrite: The upper package has structural rewrite evidence when a certified top-down channel links its interventions to supp K_ ^ Theta_i K_ ^ Theta_j or E_ ^ Theta_i E_ ^ Theta_j . It is stack_active when, in addition, Delta_ stack = and no constitutive evidence exists for the co</data></node><node id="P030-C0059"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the general E4-to-E11 bridge remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0060"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E12: Individuation: Given CompleteIndividuationStatus, exactly one of the six statuses below holds for a declared candidate. The positive content stored in its IntegratedCase is maximality under the joint closure conditions Repair(I) ⊆ I, Budget(I) ⊆ I, Record(I) ⊆ I, B(I) is viability-sufficient an</data></node><node id="P030-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the general theorem connecting every paid bridge to E12 entry closure remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0066"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E13: Repair Transport: Four related positive evidence forms hold. Communication evidence requires s to induce a paid, interface-mediated, role-preserving carried receiver repair with strict same-class delta reduction. Teaching evidence adds repairs sourced from B 's own committed state in an A -abse</data></node><node id="P030-C0067"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Two-carrier normal forms and theorem-family proof: The bridge is not an analyst's pairing of two time series. It carries a joint bridge record, a linked defect, source and target ledger entries, and a shared discharge-comparator certificate, with the appropriate occurrence carried by each system. Ch</data></node><node id="P030-C0069"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E13.2: Teaching capacity (E13_TeachingCapacity): Suppose a carried status record rho occurs for the teaching claim , has tag taught, and satisfies the full TaughtCase( ,rho) . Then Taught( ). The supplied case contains communication evidence for the same token, package, bridge, and challenge claim, </data></node><node id="P030-C0071"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E13.4: Symbolic repair (E13_SymbolicRepair): Suppose a carried status record rho occurs for the symbolic claim , has tag symbolic, and satisfies the full SymbolicCase( ,rho) . Then Symbolic( ). The supplied case contains a paid carried bridge and a declared context family, reactivation in every list</data></node><node id="P030-C0072"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Coercion-null priority (E13_CoercionNullExcludesLowerPriority): For one full claim , assume raw coercion-null evidence and a complete E13 status classifier. Then none of the six lower-priority judgments holds: ( ) ( ) ( ) ( ) ( ) ( ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E14: Reconsolidation: For a carried retrieval conflict with F9 coverage, after provenance defect, silent rewrite, ordinary-read, outcome-collision, and unrealized-disposition evidence have been excluded, exactly one lawful substantive disposition occurs: InstallsRecordJoin(m_t,R_c,m_ t+1 ), or Refin</data></node><node id="P030-C0075"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A stronger theorem from E6/E8 budget prices to the repair/coarsening frequencies remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0076"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E15: Offline Reclosure: Given complete carried debt, phase-flow, budget, and status records, exactly one E15 status in holds. The alternation_required case verifies strict online deficit and a recurrent lawful offline substrate: external exchange is gated to zero, the exact freed allocation is assig</data></node><node id="P030-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The landed E15 theorem family: Fix one full offline-reclosure claim : one debt scope, carried debt inventory, operating schedule, shared budget allocation, recurrence bound, and preregistered tolerance record. The status predicates below are always relative to that same , economy, probe move, bindin</data></node><node id="P030-C0080"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Offline capacity is exactly the freed allocation (E15_OfflineCapacityExactlyFreed): For a supplied DerivedOfflineBudgetGeometry built from the claim's exact economy, move, binding budget witness, and shared allocation record, _ off = _ on +f, _ on &lt; _ off , where f is that geometry's freed online ex</data></node><node id="P030-C0082"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The conclusion is therefore conditional on the complete classifier and full online-sufficient case, not merely on D _ on .</data></node><node id="P030-C0089"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Off-inventory flow cannot certify offline operation (E15_OffInventoryFlowCannotCertifyOffline): Fix the claim's schedule, complete phase-flow inventory F , complete external-exchange inventory, and derived budget geometry. If a flow record f_0 F , then w:GenuineOfflinePhaseEvidence, w.flowRecord=f_0</data></node><node id="P030-C0091"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">E16: Adaptability: An E-system exhibits coherent adaptability exactly when the two routes are currently indistinguishable but leave a claim-linked difference in held-out repair-capacity distributions that survives all declared controls and is not already an outstanding E15 residue. Such a record has</data></node><node id="P030-C0095"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Current-event blindness (E16_CurrentAuditBlindness): Raw coherent-adaptability evidence for implies CurrentEventEquiv(h_gamma,h_ ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0097"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol artifact is not coherent adaptability (E16_ArtifactNotAdaptability): For a complete classifier, raw artifact evidence for implies ( ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0098"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Flat claim is not coherent adaptability (E16_FlatNoAdaptability): For a complete classifier, raw flat evidence for implies ( ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0099"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Flattenable residue is not coherent adaptability (E16_FlattenableNotAdaptability): For a complete classifier, raw flattenable evidence for implies ( ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0101"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Dissipative residue is not coherent adaptability (E16_DissipativeNotAdaptability): For a complete classifier, raw dissipative evidence for implies ( ).</data></node><node id="P030-C0102"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bounded perturbation failure yields evidence-backed rejection (E16_BoundedPerturbationFailureRejected): Suppose a repair-ledger holonomy candidate matches , its declared perturbation bound and perturbation-trial inventory are carried and complete, and an inventory-member in-bound trial supplies boun</data></node><node id="P030-C0105"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Endpoint-owned trial populations: The landed declaration is |E16_SwappedRouteTrialPopulationRejected|. It states the following. Let T_gamma, T_ be the two complete route-conditioned trial inventories. If u∈ T_gamma and u is not eligible for endpoint h_ , then Eligible(u,h_gamma) u T_ .</data></node><node id="P030-C0106"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The second flat arm is claim-linked (E16_FlatSecondArmUsesClaimLinkedPair): Given gated flat-control evidence for , if the two claim endpoints are not future-predictively equivalent, then there exists a distribution pair P such that DistributionsMatchClaim(P, ) NoDeclaredRepairCapacityDifference(P).</data></node><node id="P030-C0107"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">An unrelated equal pair cannot replace the claim pair (E16_UnrelatedEqualPairCannotReplaceClaimDifference): Let P_ carry a declared repair-capacity difference and match . Let P_0 be another pair that does not match . Then DistributionsMatchClaim(P_ , ), ( DistributionsMatchClaim(P_0, ) NoDeclaredRep</data></node><node id="P030-C0108"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Despite its established declaration name, it does not prove causal identification of route transport against an undeclared confound.</data></node><node id="P030-C0109"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It is the finite-inventory limitation that prevents the paper from claiming robustness against every latent variable.</data></node><node id="P030-C0110"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">this is a packaging limitation, not a missing paper proof.</data></node><node id="P030-C0111"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The later theorem is conditional on an imported or host-supplied predicate, comparator, inventory, or recognition result that it does not re-prove.</data></node><node id="P030-C0112"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">D6 does not prove attention optimality, exposure truth, or catalog honesty.</data></node><node id="P030-C0113"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Open theorem-bridge ledger.: The following interfaces remain open or explicitly unformalized at the end of the current catalog. @ &gt; p 1.15in &gt; p 1.05in X@ →prule Interface Current status What has not been proved E4–E11 open No theorem maps an E4 compiled repair to E11's carried institutional label, </data></node><node id="P030-C0114"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not prove that the fixture catalogs are empirically complete, that a domain bridge is honest, or that the status frequencies generalize beyond the constructed carriers.</data></node><node id="P030-C0115"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope of a future positive result.: Even the full conjunction would not define biological life, prove that all living systems realize E1–E16, establish consciousness, or derive a thermodynamic arrow from protocol holonomy. It would provide a finite biological realization of a narrower normal form: c</data></node><node id="P030-C0116"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Deferred candidates and future work: The catalog is intentionally incomplete. The following candidates have recognizable shapes but do not yet possess a distinct theorem, obstruction set, and recognition interface. Global or conscious access. Simultaneous descent into memory, action, and report may </data></node><node id="P030-C0117"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not claim that D4 is necessary and sufficient for life, that every living system realizes all sixteen laws, or that the catalog settles borderline cases.</data></node><node id="P030-C0118"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No universal definition of life.: The paper gives laws that living systems can instantiate and a precise self-maintenance distinction of direct biological importance. It does not claim that D4 is necessary and sufficient for life, that every living system realizes all sixteen laws, or that the catal</data></node><node id="P030-C0120"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">General audit-capacity pricing remains open.</data></node><node id="P030-C0122"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The E6/E9 bundle is one reviewed unit. ^ E13's literal status is notRequired_with_intercarrier_scope_passed: it does not claim a local/global gluing theorem, while its inter-carrier scoping check passed.</data></node><node id="P030-C0123"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol internalization/completion/currentization was also not exercised and is recorded as a limitation. The undeclared-proxy row therefore records a governance limitation even though the declared-support classifier returns coherent_adaptability.</data></node><node id="P030-C0124"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">[htbp] @ lX@ →prule Artifact Limitation Declaration JSONL and TOML manifest Their 1,261-row agreement checks declared name/module/law metadata.</data></node><node id="P030-C0126"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the theorem does not assert indefinite online persistence.</data></node><node id="P031-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We present a math-only framework in which theories arise as fixed points of idempotent operators and open-endedness requires changing the operator rather than iterating it. Under minimal hypotheses of composability plus limited interface access with bounded observation, we show that the operator voc</data></node><node id="P031-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The organizing picture: a three-certificate loop: The results of this paper can be read as a minimal calculus for building hierarchical structure while keeping directionality honest. The core observation is that three notions often conflated in informal discussions are logically distinct and have di</data></node><node id="P031-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite theory package: Assume A_FIN. A finite theory package is a tuple T = (Z,\ f,\ Sigma_f,\ E,\ A) where: Z is a finite carrier set (microstate space). f:Z→ X is a lens (coarse description). The induced definability structure Sigma_f is the finite sigma -algebra (equivalently, partition) on Z gen</data></node><node id="P031-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure operator: Let (L, ) be a poset. A map c:L→ L is a closure operator if for all x,y∈ L : (Extensive) x c(x) . (Monotone) x y implies c(x) c(y) . (Idempotent) c(c(x)) = c(x) .</data></node><node id="P031-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closed points / objects: Given a closure operator c:L→ L , its closed points (or objects of the theory determined by c ) are the fixed points Fix(c) := \ x∈ L : c(x)=x\ .</data></node><node id="P031-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closed points are antitone in closure strength: If c d are closure operators on L , then Fix(d)⊆ Fix(c) . Equivalently: if d(x)=x then c(x)=x .</data></node><node id="P031-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">One-step stabilization under iteration: Let c be a closure operator on L . Define the iterate c^ (n) by c^ (0) =id and c^ (n+1) = c c^ (n) . Then for all x∈ L and all integers n 1 , c^ (n) (x) = c(x).</data></node><node id="P031-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strictly stronger closure: For closure operators c,d on L , write c d (`` d is strictly stronger than c '') if c d and there exists an x∈ L such that d(x)≠ c(x) .</data></node><node id="P031-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure ladder: A closure ladder is a sequence of closure operators (c_n)_ n∈ N on L such that c_n c_ n+1 for all n . The fixed-point sets form a nested chain Fix(c_0) Fix(c_1) Fix(c_2) by Lemma .</data></node><node id="P031-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Idempotent endomap: Let A be a set (or type). An idempotent endomap on A is a function e : A → A such that e e = e , i.e.\ e(e(a)) = e(a) for all a∈ A .</data></node><node id="P031-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Fixed points: For an endomap e : A → A define its fixed-point set Fix(e) := \ a∈ A : e(a)=a\ . We interpret Fix(e) as the collection of ``objects'' completed by e .</data></node><node id="P031-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Idempotents split: Let e : A → A be an idempotent endomap. Let i:Fix(e) A denote inclusion, and define r:A→ Fix(e) by r(a):=e(a) (viewed as an element of Fix(e) ). Then r i = id_ Fix(e) and i r = e . In particular, e restricts to the identity on Fix(e) and Fix(e) canonically identifies with the imag</data></node><node id="P031-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">TV idempotence defect; D-IC-02: Let \| \|_ TV denote total variation on Delta(Z) , i.e. \|mu-nu\|_ TV = 12\|mu-nu\|_1 . The idempotence defect of E_ tau,f is delta_ tau,f := _ mu∈ Delta(Z) \|E_ tau,f (E_ tau,f (mu)) - E_ tau,f (mu) \|_ TV . Guardrail. A small idempotence defect delta(E) is a saturat</data></node><node id="P031-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Prototype stability at scale tau: For each x∈ X define the (scale- tau ) stability of the prototype u_x by s_ tau,f (x) := \|E_ tau,f (u_x)-u_x \|_ TV = \|U_f(Q_f(u_xP^tau)) - U_f(delta_x) \|_ TV . We say u_x is -stable if s_ tau,f (x) .</data></node><node id="P031-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Approximate idempotence from retention: T-IC-02. Assume A_FIN+A_LENS and fix E_ tau,f as in Definition . Define a retention error _ tau,f := _ x∈ X \|Q_f(u_xP^tau)-delta_x \|_ TV . Then: (Prototype stability bound) For all x∈ X , s_ tau,f (x) _ tau,f . (Idempotence defect bound) delta_ tau,f _ tau,f</data></node><node id="P031-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No false positives: Assume A_FIN+A_LENS. If the lifted system has zero asymmetry at horizon T , then every coarse observation has zero asymmetry at the same horizon. There are no false positives from forgetting variables. Coarse-graining can hide irreversibility (false negatives), but it cannot crea</data></node><node id="P031-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Autonomous m -phase protocol: D-PROT-02. Fix alpha∈[0,1] and define an autonomous Markov chain on the product space Z:=X× Phi by P ((x,phi),(x',phi') ) :=alpha 1_ x'=x S (phi,phi') +(1-alpha) 1_ phi'=phi K_phi(x,x'). We call this the lifted (phase-included) protocol model.</data></node><node id="P031-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">``P3 needs P6_drive'' under autonomy (C-ACC-01): Under A_FIN+A_REV+A_AUT+A_ACC+A_NULL (no affinities anywhere in the lifted state space), protocol holonomy alone does not yield sustained arrow-of-time in the autonomous accounted model. For any stationary distribution of the lifted autonomous chain, </data></node><node id="P031-C0051"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Predicates and definability: A (Boolean) predicate is a function h:Z→\ 0,1\ . We say that h is definable from the theory f (equivalently: Pi_f -measurable) if it is constant on each block: x∈ X,\ z,z'∈ B_x, h(z)=h(z'). Equivalently, there exists h:X→\ 0,1\ with h= h f .</data></node><node id="P031-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite forcing lemma / physical forcing lemma: Assume A_FIN+A_LENS. Let f:Z→ X be a theory with blocks Pi_f=\ B_x\ _ x∈ X , and sample h:Z→\ 0,1\ uniformly from \ 0,1\ ^Z (i.i.d.\ fair bits across microstates). Then non-definability is generic: P(h is not definable from f)=1-2^ -(N-K) .</data></node><node id="P031-C0057"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Assume A_FIN+A_LENS and let f:Z→ X have blocks Pi_f=\ B_x\ _ x∈ X . For each block B_x , P(h is constant on B_x)=2^ 1-|B_x| , and by a union bound, P( x∈ X such that h is constant on B_x)\ \ _ x∈ X 2^ 1-|B_x| . In particular, if every block has size at least m 2 , then P(h splits every block) \ \ 1 </data></node><node id="P031-C0061"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Interface lens (D-META-LENS-01): An interface lens is a map f: P→ X to a set of observables. It induces a kernel equivalence x _f y iff f(x)=f(y) . Limited access means x≠ y with x _f y . This abstracts the notion of a coarse-graining lens f:Z→ X used elsewhere: here the lens acts on processes/happe</data></node><node id="P031-C0066"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">P1: operator rewrite: P1 is a rewrite of the substrate operator: replace a Markov kernel P by a new kernel P' (or a finite family \ P^ (m) \ ), thereby changing the endomap mu↦ mu P^tau and the induced empirical endomap E_ tau,f . This is a change in the operator itself, not an external schedule.</data></node><node id="P031-C0068"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">P3: autonomous protocol holonomy: P3 is modeled in the autonomous lifted form on Z:=X×Phi . The phase phi∈Phi evolves by an internal kernel S , and conditioned on phi the microstate updates by K_phi . No external schedule is assumed in the main text; externally scheduled stroboscopic protocols are n</data></node><node id="P031-C0070"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">P5: packaging: P5 is an idempotent endomap e whose fixed points Fix(e) are the packaged objects of a given theory. This is an endomap notion (Section ). It is not an order-closure.</data></node><node id="P031-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim every system self-organizes</data></node><node id="P031-C0074"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Non-claim. This subsection does not assert that coarse descriptions ``override'' micro laws.</data></node><node id="P031-C0079"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim that closure ladders arise automatically in arbitrary dynamics, nor that any particular choice of lens f or timescale tau is preferred.</data></node><node id="P031-C0080"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Appendix D frames the Zeno decision frontier as a research outlook for when Zeno can or cannot be ruled out in the abstract bridge setting.</data></node><node id="P031-C0081"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Outlook: forthcoming instantiations This paper isolates a math-only emergence calculus.</data></node><node id="P031-C0082"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Outlook: forthcoming instantiations: This paper isolates a math-only emergence calculus. It uses a theory package (lens/definability, completion, audit). It is paired with the six primitive moves that change what theories can exist and what objects they recognize. A series of companion papers will i</data></node><node id="P031-C0095"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Passivity with storage: A causal bridge Z: U→ U is passive with storage (cf.\ ) if there exist: (i) a state space X , (ii) a nonnegative storage functional S:X→[0,∈fty) , and (iii) for each input u∈ U a state trajectory x_u:[0,T]→ X , such that for all 0 s t T , S(x_u(t)) - S(x_u(s))\ \ ∈t_s^t u(tau</data></node><node id="P031-C0115"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Feasibility gate and induced coercive norm (ID: D-ECT-GATE-01): Fix a finite horizon T&gt;0 , a port Hilbert space U_j , and write U_j:=L^2([0,T];U_j) . Let G_j:U_j→ U_j be a self-adjoint positive semidefinite operator. Define the feasible input class U_j^ feas :=\ u∈ U_j:\ u(t)∈ Ran(G_j)\ for a.e.\ t\</data></node><node id="P031-C0122"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Sectorization bounds the minimal mode count (ID: L-ECT-MODE-UB-01): Assume a sectorization as in Definition . Then any sector-respecting atomic decomposition of Z_j can be merged within each sector to produce a sector-respecting decomposition with at most |Q_j| nonzero atoms. In particular, m_j^ \ \</data></node><node id="P032-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds theory studies finite systems observed through deterministic coarse-grainings (``lenses'') using a small audited vocabulary of primitives. This paper proves eight no-go theorems that constrain when common emergence narratives can be made honest in a finite, checkable setting. For a finite </data></node><node id="P032-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Main results (eight fronts).: All results are stated for finite systems and deterministic lenses. The theorem statements are collected in Section , and the later theorem-front sections refer back to those labels. In one line each, the fronts are: Arrow/DPI: deterministic observation cannot increase </data></node><node id="P032-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Main Results: We state the eight no-go theorems. Each theorem is followed by a brief remark clarifying scope. [NG_ARROW_DPI] Let mu∈Delta(X) , let P:X×X→[0,1] be row-stochastic, and let T 1 . Define the micro path law on X^ T+1 by P^ (T) _ mu,P (x_ 0:T )=mu(x_0) _ t=0 ^ T-1 P(x_t,x_ t+1 ). Let rev_T</data></node><node id="P032-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_ARROW_DPI: Let mu∈Delta(X) , let P:X×X→[0,1] be row-stochastic, and let T 1 . Define the micro path law on X^ T+1 by P^ (T) _ mu,P (x_ 0:T )=mu(x_0) _ t=0 ^ T-1 P(x_t,x_ t+1 ). Let rev_T(x_ 0:T )=(x_T, ,x_0) and let :X→Y be deterministic with pointwise extension ^ (T) (x_ 0:T )=( (x_0), , (x_T)) </data></node><node id="P032-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_PROTOCOL_TRAP: Let be finite, let P: × →[0,1] be row-stochastic, and let pi be a stationary distribution satisfying detailed balance pi(x) P(x,x')= pi(x') P(x',x) for all x,x'∈ . Then for every horizon T 1 , (rev_T)_ # P^ (T) _ pi, P =P^ (T) _ pi, P implies KLBig(P^ (T) _ pi, P \ Big\|\ (rev_T)_ </data></node><node id="P032-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_FORCE_FOREST: Let G=(V,E) be a finite undirected forest. Let a be an antisymmetric function on oriented edges, so that a(u,v)=-a(v,u) and a(u,v)=0 whenever \ u,v\ E . Then there exists a potential phi:V→R such that for every oriented edge (u,v) , a(u,v)=phi(v)-phi(u).</data></node><node id="P032-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_FORCE_NULL: Assume a(u,v)=phi(v)-phi(u) on oriented edges of a finite graph. Then for every closed walk v_0, ,v_k=v_0 , _ i=0 ^ k-1 a(v_i,v_ i+1 ) = 0.</data></node><node id="P032-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_MACRO_CLOSURE_DEFICIT: Let (X,P) be a finite Markov chain, let mu∈Delta(X) , let Pi:X→Y be deterministic, and fix tau 1 . For each x∈X , define the packaged future law p_x^ (tau) (y') := Pr_ mu,P [Pi(X_ t+tau )=y' X_t=x]. For a candidate macro kernel K:Y×Y→[0,1] , define L_tau(K) := _ x∈ X mu(x) </data></node><node id="P032-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_OBJECT_CONTRACTIVE: Let P be a stochastic matrix on finite Y . Let T_P:Delta(Y)→Delta(Y) be the operator nu↦ nu P , and let lambda(P) be the Dobrushin contraction coefficient in total variation. Assume lambda(P)&lt;1 . If nu,nu'∈Delta(Y) satisfy the -stability bounds TV(nu,nu P) , TV(nu',nu'P) , the</data></node><node id="P032-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_LADDER_IDEM: Let e:S→ S satisfy e e=e . Then for all integers n 1 , e^ n =e.</data></node><node id="P032-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">NG_LADDER_BOUNDED_INTERFACE: Let f:X→Y be deterministic with finite image im(f) . Define Def(f):=\ g f : g:Y→\ 0,1\ \ . Then |Def(f)| = 2^ |im(f)| . Consequently, if |im(f)|&lt;∈fty , there is no infinite strictly increasing sequence of pairwise distinct f -definable predicates.</data></node><node id="P032-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Deterministic DPI for KL: Let Omega be a finite set and let g:Omega→Omega' be deterministic. For any probability laws P,Q∈Delta(Omega) , D_ KL (g_ # P \| g_ # Q) D_ KL (P \| Q).</data></node><node id="P032-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Detailed balance implies path-law reversal invariance: Assume pi∈Delta(X) is stationary for K and satisfies detailed balance: pi(x) K(x,x') = pi(x') K(x',x) for all x,x'∈ X. Then for every T 0 , P_T^ pi,K = P_T^ pi,K,rev .</data></node><node id="P032-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Antisymmetric edge labelling / 1 -form: An antisymmetric edge labelling on G is a function a: E→ R such that a(v,u)=-a(u,v) for all (u,v)∈ E .</data></node><node id="P032-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cycle rank: Let c(G) be the number of connected components of G . The cycle rank (first Betti number) of G is beta_1(G) = |E|-|V|+c(G). In particular, beta_1(G)=0 if and only if every component of G is a tree. These cycle-space facts are standard in graph theory; see .</data></node><node id="P032-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Rooted parent map on a tree: Let G=(V,E) be a finite tree and fix a root r∈ V . For each vertex v≠ r there exists a unique neighbor par(v) of v lying on the unique simple path from r to v .</data></node><node id="P032-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">KL mixture decomposition: Let (w_i)_ i∈ I be nonnegative weights on a finite index set I , not all zero, and let (P_i)_ i∈ I be probability laws on a finite set Omega . Define W := _ i∈ I w_i, R := 1 W _ i∈ I w_i P_i. Then for every probability law Q on Omega , _ i∈ I w_i D_ KL (P_i \| Q ) = _ i∈ I </data></node><node id="P032-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditional mutual information identity: In this finite deterministic setting, I(X_t;Y_ t+tau Y_t) = _ x∈ X mu(x) D_ KL (p_x^ (tau) \| p_ Pi(x) ^ (tau) ).</data></node><node id="P032-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Dobrushin contraction in total variation: For all mu,nu∈Delta(Y) , d_ TV (mu P,nu P) lambda(P) d_ TV (mu,nu).</data></node><node id="P032-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict contraction gives a unique fixed distribution: Assume lambda(P)&lt;1 . Then there exists a unique distribution pi∈Delta(Y) with pi P=pi . Moreover, for every mu∈Delta(Y) and every integer t 0 , d_ TV (mu P^t,pi) lambda(P)^t d_ TV (mu,pi).</data></node><node id="P032-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">If e e = e , then e^ n =e for all integers n 1 .</data></node><node id="P032-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The map A⊆ im(f) \1_A f is a bijection between P(im(f)) and Def(f) . Consequently, |Def(f)| = 2^ |im(f)| .</data></node><node id="P032-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Potential construction on a rooted tree: Let G=(V,E) be a finite tree with root r , and let a: E→R be antisymmetric. Define phi(r)=0 and, for v≠ r , phi(v)=phi(par(v))+a(par(v),v). Then a(u,v)=phi(v)-phi(u) for every oriented edge (u,v)∈ E .</data></node><node id="P032-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite strictly increasing chains are bounded: If S is a finite set, then every strictly increasing chain of distinct elements of S has length at most |S| .</data></node><node id="P033-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Protocol-driven dynamics often conflate route dependence (noncommutativity/holonomy of update steps) with an arrow of time, making time-asymmetry audits vulnerable to scheduling artifacts. We introduce an autonomous lifted-protocol formulation and a path-reversal audit decomposition, operationalize </data></node><node id="P033-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim a general characterization of all nonequilibrium protocols</data></node><node id="P034-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Formal incompleteness explains why no sufficiently expressive effective theory captures all arithmetic truth, but it does not by itself provide a structural language for comparing theory growth. This paper develops such a language by treating a formal theory as a packaged closure object evaluated on</data></node><node id="P034-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Vendor-backed PICA evidence is used only to discipline primitive roles and scope, not as proof support.</data></node><node id="P034-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Vendor-backed PICA evidence is used only to discipline primitive roles and scope, not as proof support.</data></node><node id="P034-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Its arithmetic lift is conditional on an explicit, finite external dependency contract.</data></node><node id="P034-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (persistent growth witness).: Let pkg=(X,op) be a closure package. A point x∈ X is a persistent growth witness for pkg if there exists n 1 such that op^ [n+1] (x)≠ op^ [n] (x), where op^ [m] denotes the m -fold iterate of op . This is the internal notion of strict growth used in the base </data></node><node id="P034-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (comparison domain). A comparison domain is a subset D⊆ E of operators admissible for comparison on the frozen slice s . The paper keeps D explicit because efficiency claims are always relative both to a ledger and to a chosen admissible domain.</data></node><node id="P034-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (comparison domain).: A comparison domain is a subset D⊆ E of operators admissible for comparison on the frozen slice s . The paper keeps D explicit because efficiency claims are always relative both to a ledger and to a chosen admissible domain.</data></node><node id="P034-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (representative alignment target). Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E be a comparison domain, let K⊆ E be a canonical representative family, and let e∈ D . One says that e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) if there exists c∈ K D such that Agree</data></node><node id="P034-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (representative alignment target).: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E be a comparison domain, let K⊆ E be a canonical representative family, and let e∈ D . One says that e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) if there exists c∈ K D such that Agre</data></node><node id="P034-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Primitive roles in the theorem setup: With the definitions now in place, the primitive-role assignment used by the paper can be stated without ambiguity. P5 is the fixed-package object: it appears here as the closure package pkg=(X,op) . The base theorem concerns exactly this object under idempotent</data></node><node id="P034-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">One-step saturation: Let pkg=(X,op) be a closure package. Then for every x∈ X and every n∈ N , op^ [n+1] (x)=op(x). Equivalently, once the fixed package has acted once, further self-iteration does not move the result any farther.</data></node><node id="P034-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No persistent growth in a frozen package: Let pkg=(X,op) be a closure package and let x∈ X . Then x is not a persistent growth witness for pkg .</data></node><node id="P034-C0030"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict growth requires package change: Suppose a persistent growth witness is claimed for what is being treated as a single fixed closure package. Then the fixed-package description fails. Equivalently, any genuine persistent strict-growth witness forces package change.</data></node><node id="P034-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Frontier dominance: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, and let e,f∈ D . One says that e dominates f on s , written FrontierDominates_s(e,f), if Y_s(e) Y_s(f), K_s(e) K_s(f), and at least one of these inequalities is strict.</data></node><node id="P034-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Agreement on the support cone: Let C⊆ X , and let e,f∈E . One says that e and f agree on the cone C , written AgreeOnCone_C(e,f), if e(x)=f(x) for all x∈ C.</data></node><node id="P034-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Dominance is stable under cone-equivalent replacement: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , and let e,f,f'∈ D . Assume: FrontierDominates_s(e,f) , AgreeOnCone_ C_s (f,f') , s is invariant on its cone. Then FrontierDominates_s(e,f').</data></node><node id="P034-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Efficiency transfers to a cone-equivalent representative: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , and let e,e'∈ D . Assume: FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e) , AgreeOnCone_ C_s (e,e') , s is invariant on its cone. Then FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e').</data></node><node id="P034-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem is the key comparative transfer result. It shows that efficiency belongs, on a frozen slice, not to an arbitrary syntactic representative but to the cone-equivalence class that the slice can actually see.</data></node><node id="P034-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Representative alignment target: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆E , let K⊆ D , and let e∈ D . One says that e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) if there exists c∈ K such that AgreeOnCone_ C_s (e,c).</data></node><node id="P034-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Restricted alignment: Let s=(Y_s,K_s,C_s) be a frozen slice, let D⊆ E , let K⊆ D , and let e∈ D . Assume: FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e) , e satisfies the representative alignment target relative to (s,D,K) , s is invariant on its cone. Then there exists c∈K such that FrontierEfficient_ s,D (c) and Agre</data></node><node id="P034-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not claim that every efficient object is globally identical to a canonical representative, nor that every possible ledger collapses to the same family.</data></node><node id="P034-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditional arithmetic lift: Assume (A1) – (A6) . If FrontierEfficient_ s,D (e), then there exists c∈ K such that ArithmeticCanonicalityTarget(s,D,C_s,K,c).</data></node><node id="P034-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Boundary of the theorem: Theorem is the theorem-bearing summit of the paper. It is conditional, not unconditional. It does not claim a global canonical-collapse theorem, and it does not assert that every possible ledger or support regime selects the same representative family. It says only that once</data></node><node id="P034-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not claim a global canonical-collapse theorem, and it does not assert that every possible ledger or support regime selects the same representative family.</data></node><node id="P034-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not prove the package-change bridge theorem. It does not prove the restricted alignment lemma.</data></node><node id="P034-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not prove an unconditional global canonicality theorem.</data></node><node id="P034-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper also does not claim that the full Six-Birds or PICA interaction space has been reduced to one theorem-bearing summary.</data></node><node id="P034-C0053"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This appendix records the claims that remain outside the scope of the manuscript.</data></node><node id="P034-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not prove an unconditional global canonicality theorem.</data></node><node id="P034-C0055"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim to have internally discharged the full arithmetic literature boundary.</data></node><node id="P035-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This paper does not claim a solution to the Clay problem.</data></node><node id="P035-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This theorem does not prove any PDE statement.</data></node><node id="P035-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What this paper does not claim.</data></node><node id="P036-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">we do not claim novelty for proposing it.</data></node><node id="P037-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">studies strict-test-to-event-package admissibility on fixed support in Six Birds Theory. The starting point is the substrate: accepted new-layer strict tests on support, with the strict-test partition rung valid by inheritance. The paper asks whether this strict-test substrate can be advanced to eve</data></node><node id="P037-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Shared-event identity in the strong sense is not claimed</data></node><node id="P037-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Paper 10 result statement: The paper has two paper-10-new contributions. First, a protocol-indexed shared-identity layer is constructed: 9 same-role cross-context edges are accepted at protocol-indexed identity lane scope across the three H6 PICA carriers ( , ). Second, the per-role bridge-level law</data></node><node id="P037-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nonclaims summary: Paper 10 does not claim shared-event identity in the strong sense, and it does not accept identity at the three rejected lanes intrinsic identity lane, lawful-same-branch identity lane, or lift-only identity lane. It does not certify an admissible cross-carrier comparison base at </data></node><node id="P037-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Paper 10 does not claim H2 strict-test closure, does not claim strict realization of the H2 readout slot, and does not reconstruct H6 structure from H2</data></node><node id="P037-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Proto-effect outcome lattice rung (future work): The proto-effect outcome lattice rung is the downstream rung that begins only after accepted event-level and accepted-package input is available. Conceptually, it asks whether proto-effect probes can be evaluated on accepted packages and whether their</data></node><node id="P037-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Paper 10 does not claim it. records the methodological discipline that applies the taxonomy</data></node><node id="P037-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This nonclaim does not undo the protocol-indexed acceptance of .</data></node><node id="P037-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event-gate threshold criterion attempt and named missing subobject: The event-gate threshold construction attempt starts from the Theta component of the inherited event-bridge record. Methodologically, the intended criterion is conjunctive: one admissible event-level threshold statement is needed fo</data></node><node id="P037-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It says that an incomplete construction may not be silently upgraded to a stronger result, that a preserved nonclaim may not be turned into an inherited positive, and that a formal proof gap may not stand in as an axiom.</data></node><node id="P037-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The second flag, no_old_layer_transfer_axiom, protects the H2 boundary: no axiom or constant may stand in for an unfinished proof that would convert an old-layer nonclaim into an old-layer positive.</data></node><node id="P037-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The acceptance does not assert shared-event identity in the strong sense, and it does not assert identity through intrinsic identity lane, lawful-same-branch identity lane, or lift-only identity lane</data></node><node id="P037-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event-gate threshold criterion non-constructibility: The event-gate threshold criterion is the criterion-level conjunction of the role-specific admissible event-level threshold statements. In the Paper 10 setting, the three conjunctive roles are readout-agreement role, quadratic-witness role, and tr</data></node><node id="P037-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Event-level verdict consolidation: The event-level verdict is consolidated by the conjunctive threshold-plus-controls rule. A new-layer event-level record is accepted only if it passes the event-gate threshold criterion and then survives the event-level controls. gives the first obstruction: the thr</data></node><node id="P037-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">That nonclaim is inherited from Paper 9 and is preserved here: Paper 10 records a pair-observable trace, not an H2 strict readout test .</data></node><node id="P037-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The proto-effect outcome lattice rung is downstream of that event-level input and remains future work.</data></node><node id="P037-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Proto-effect, effect algebra, Born, composition: future work: The downstream structures named at the proto-effect outcome lattice rung are future-work targets in Paper 10, not negative results or hidden refutations. A proto-effect fragment would require accepted packages on which candidate operation</data></node><node id="P037-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Layered-verdict summary: summarizes the layered verdict. The rows separate the inherited strict-test partition rung, the protocol-indexed shared-identity sibling axis, the protocol-indexed event-level rung, and the downstream proto-effect outcome lattice. The protocol-indexed shared-identity layer i</data></node><node id="P037-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">event-level control survival criterion construction. proto-effect outcome lattice rung (future work) requires further extension none Proto-effect operation structure</data></node><node id="P037-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem schemas: The kernel contains 14 theorem schemas: typed-level statements encoding admissibility discipline, gate consistency, and no-shortcut prohibitions. A schema states how one typed discipline may be used when its hypotheses are available, or what cannot be inferred from a weaker discipli</data></node><node id="P037-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Decision logic over the seven-outcome enumeration: The final-gate decision is an ordered case analysis over the series-canonical seven-outcome enumeration. The first case, event_package_accepted , would require accepted event-level records, an event-package candidate, and a promoted package. It is e</data></node><node id="P037-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Final claim boundary: records the final claim boundary for Paper 10. Its first row group separates results inherited unchanged from Paper 9: the strict-test partition rung, the native three-slot strict domain, and H2 boundary preservation as an inherited nonclaim. The second row group records paper-</data></node><node id="P037-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Its first row group separates results inherited unchanged from Paper 9: the strict-test partition rung, the native three-slot strict domain, and H2 boundary preservation as an inherited nonclaim. inherited unchanged from Paper 9 yes (count = 3) B Native three-slot strict domain accepted on each new-</data></node><node id="P037-C0035"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This is the part of the result that would survive unchanged if future work later supplied the missing bridge-level subobject.</data></node><node id="P037-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Upstream nonclaim preservation: Seven inherited nonclaims from the Paper 7 and Paper 9 line remain in force: faithful public compression is not claimed; a lawful public carrier is not earned; no canonical selected lift is asserted; H2 strict-test closure is not claimed; H2 readout strict realization</data></node><node id="P037-C0037"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Scope and limitations: The scope of Paper 10 is the strict-test-to-event-package admissibility question on fixed H6 PICA support, beginning from the accepted strict-test substrate inherited from Paper 9. Paper-10-new claims are layer-local at H6 PICA; the event-package candidate, proto-effect fragme</data></node><node id="P037-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the event-package candidate, proto-effect fragment, effect algebra, Born valuation, and composition readiness all remain future work</data></node><node id="P037-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definitions: H2 denotes the inherited H2 boundary layer, and H6 denotes the H6 PICA new-layer support used by Paper 10. The mnemonics are paper-local shorthand for the layer vocabulary inherited from Paper 9 . They are notation, not new layers introduced by this paper. The strict-test partition rung</data></node><node id="P037-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Gate predicates: This gate decides at which lane an identity-edge attempt is accepted, if any. In Paper 10 the accepted edges are treated in . The gate classifies identity scope and does not by itself license event-level records. This gate asks whether the paper-10 substrate certifies an admissible </data></node><node id="P037-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lean theorem schemas: The Lean kernel contains 14 theorem schemas, summarized in the body at . This appendix records their Lean-side form without reproducing all fourteen declarations. The schemas follow the inherited formal-kernel convention of the series: they state typed conditional structure, no</data></node><node id="P037-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This schema does not assert that any particular paper-10 record satisfies the predicate.</data></node><node id="P037-C0044"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The proof does not import an empirical witness as a theorem and does not assert that a paper-10 artifact satisfies the antecedent.</data></node><node id="P037-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Schema legality and empirical content: The Lean development for Paper 10 establishes schema legality and conditional proof structure; it does not establish empirical witness existence. The empirical content of the paper is supplied by the artifact pipeline: records the construction methods and gate </data></node><node id="P037-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Paper 9 Preserved nonclaim under role-specific descent.</data></node><node id="P037-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete nonclaims ledger: This ledger collects the complete set of structures that Paper 10 explicitly does not assert. Some entries are inherited nonclaims carried forward from earlier papers; others are paper-10-new rejections or downstream consequences of the per-role bridge-level law basis obst</data></node><node id="P037-C0048"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This ledger collects the complete set of structures that Paper 10 explicitly does not assert.</data></node><node id="P037-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">This remains downstream future work beyond the present proto-effect rung.</data></node><node id="P038-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Why the raw trajectory is not the theorem object: The continuous substrate evolves by the full six-mechanism update law x↦ F(x), x∈ S_ aud . This trajectory is the source of the mathematical structures we study, but it is not itself the theorem object. A theorem stated directly about raw trajectorie</data></node><node id="P038-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Continuous full-loop lawfulness on the audited shell: Let S_ aud be the audited shell-stable class of continuous substrate states described above. Assume that the full update law F satisfies the shell-stable hypotheses just listed: well-posed continuous operator dynamics, full six-mechanism causal c</data></node><node id="P038-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">In particular, this section does not claim: a pressure theorem beyond the audited shell, a shell-general pressure theorem, a theorem about the packaged-object space O_1 , or any direct stratumwise thermodynamic distinction theorem.</data></node><node id="P038-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cocycle pressure closure on the audited shell: Let S_ aud be the audited shell-stable class from Section , and let a_n(s;x) be the selector-weighted operator growth observable associated with the base cocycle theory T_0 . Define Phi_n(s):= _ x∈S_ aud a_n(s;x). Assume the shell-stable hypotheses of t</data></node><node id="P038-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict theory extension on the audited shell: Let S_ aud be the audited shell-stable class, let pi_0:S_ aud → O_0 be the cocycle-level object map of the base theory T_0 , and let pi_1:S_ aud → O_1 be the packaged-object map defined by the completion theory T_1 . Assume: the cocycle-pressure theory T</data></node><node id="P038-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem is the paper’s open-endedness theoremlet. It shows that the packaged-object theory is not merely a more elaborate visualization of the cocycle-pressure theory. It defines a deeper theory on the same audited shell.</data></node><node id="P038-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We stress again what the theoremlet does not claim. It does not claim a broader theorem class beyond the audited shell. It does not claim a shell-general extension theorem. It does not claim an external/non-SFT breadth theorem.</data></node><node id="P038-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper therefore does not claim a direct stratumwise root-separation theorem.</data></node><node id="P038-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The section does not claim an exact KL-based closure-deficit identity as a closed theorem.</data></node><node id="P038-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conditional pressure disintegration on the audited shell: Let S_ aud be the audited shell-stable class, let P_ T_0 (s) be the closed cocycle pressure object of the base theory T_0 , and let F(x) be the persistent packaged strata of the extended theory T_1 with weights w_ Sigma (x) . Define Delta_ T_</data></node><node id="P038-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The continuous six-mechanism substrate machinery is adapted from prior six-birds/PICA-style work, and the paper does not claim that substrate-generation mechanism itself as its main novelty.</data></node><node id="P038-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and future work: The theorem package proved in this paper is intentionally narrow in scope and correspondingly strong in closure. The main result is not a broad theorem about all context-dependent Cantor systems, nor a shell-general theorem, nor a theorem of non-SFT breadth. It is a close</data></node><node id="P038-C0027"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Whether the extension and disintegration theoremlets can be widened to a larger shell or a more canonical class remains open.</data></node><node id="P038-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Wider parameter-shell results.: The present result does not prove that the audited-shell class can be replaced by a substantially wider parameter shell. That remains future work.</data></node><node id="P038-C0031"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Whether stronger package-conditioned or measure-theoretic consequences can be obtained remains open.</data></node><node id="P038-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work: There are four natural future directions. Broader shell stability. One can ask whether the audited shell can be widened while preserving all four theoremlets simultaneously.</data></node><node id="P038-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">If future work succeeds, the natural direction is not to abandon this object in favor of a broader but weaker statement, but to widen the class while preserving the same object-level architecture.</data></node><node id="P038-C0034"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">In particular, the manuscript does not claim an exact KL-based closure-deficit theorem.</data></node><node id="P039-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The deep questions around dark energy — why the cosmological constant is so small against quantum-field-theoretic expectations, why it is comparable to the matter density now, whether the DESI-era preference for an evolving, phantom-crossing equation of state signals new physics, and why every alter</data></node><node id="P039-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A fundamental is not refuted and its value is not derived</data></node><node id="P040-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">it does not assert that every promoted extension is useful, that hidden promoted objects are lower-layer content, or novelty for the classical constructions themselves.</data></node><node id="P040-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The scope is intentionally narrow.</data></node><node id="P040-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">abstracts the shared shape into a typed profile skeleton and proves a transfer theorem: a profile validated in one case is portable to another under matching audit hypotheses, but the transfer remains conditional on target-side validation, so it does not identify objects across domains.</data></node><node id="P040-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Instrument: An instrument is a finite typed record I=(vis_I,Theta_I,BridgePolicy_I,AuditPolicy_I, SourcePolicy_I, N_I), where vis_I partitions content into \ visible,suppressed,outside,unknown\ , Theta_I gives threshold rules, BridgePolicy_I gives admissible bridge types, AuditPolicy_I gives require</data></node><node id="P040-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite promoted-use trace: A finite promoted-use trace for a lower problem profile P_0 is a tuple T=(D,V,E,r,o,lambda, , B,A_ T , N,v_ ), where: D=(V,E) is a finite directed acyclic graph. An edge u→ v means that the node v depends on the node u . r records the rule, operation, bridge, or audit step</data></node><node id="P040-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Accepted promoted-use trace: A finite promoted-use trace T is accepted, written AccTrace( T), when all of the following hold: the final answer is a lower accepted answer: a^ _ T ∈Ans_0(P), Eval_ Theta_0 (delta_0(a^ _ T ))=pass; every bridge in B belongs to the declared admissible bridge family; ever</data></node><node id="P040-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strict and essentially promoted trace: An accepted trace T is strict, written StrictTrace( T), when its promoted interface separates content collapsed by its lower interface. Concretely, there exist comparison states or trace presentations s,s' such that pi_0(s)=pi_0(s') and pi_1(s)≠ pi_1(s'), or eq</data></node><node id="P040-C0036"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Tautology barrier: The SAU framework is not only the sufficient condition SAU implies Useful. For finite typed audited strict-use traces, the reverse extraction also holds: StrictUsefulTrace implies -shaped certificate. Thus SAU is a normal form for strict audited promoted use, not merely a name for</data></node><node id="P040-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No reduction to notation: Under the hypotheses of Theorem , the promoted layer is not merely a notational variant of the lower layer at the declared interface.</data></node><node id="P040-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nontrivial usefulness: Under the hypotheses of Theorem , the promoted layer supplies an accepted lower answer that the fixed lower interface does not supply.</data></node><node id="P040-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit failure An overreading, smuggling, provenance, or replay check remains open.</data></node><node id="P040-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU diagnostic tag set: The diagnostic tag set of a raw package is the finite set Diag_ SAU ( C)⊆ \ accepted,convenient,non_strict,not_hidden, unreturned,irrelevant,failed_audit, smuggled,overread \ assigned by the following rules: accepted∈Diag_ SAU ( C) only when the claim status is accepted, F_ S</data></node><node id="P040-C0047"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Counterfeit SAU patterns: A candidate bridge has one of the following counterfeit profiles when it resembles SAU informally but lacks a required field: Ornamental extension: Strict(B), U _q, but C(U) _B a^ . The promoted layer is richer but unreturned. Mere notation: C(U) _B a^ , but delta_ fact (pi</data></node><node id="P040-C0049"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Acceptance boundary for SAU: A raw package C can be validated as an SAU proof package only if F_ SAU ( C)= . Conversely, if the six witness fields are verified under the declared instruments and F_ SAU ( C)= , then C validates to an SAU proof package: ValSAU( C).</data></node><node id="P040-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Conversely, if the six verified witness fields supply saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, solving, and audit safety, and F_ SAU ( C)= records that none of the corresponding failure conditions remains open, then the package validates.</data></node><node id="P040-C0054"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lower-layer literalization claim: A lower-layer literalization claim for a promoted structure U , written Lit_0(U), is a claim that asserts one of the following at the lower layer without an additional admissible bridge for U itself: U∈ T_0 ; U _q ; U is directly visible under I_0 ; the lower layer </data></node><node id="P040-C0064"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Pairwise sort distinction of the four canonical profiles: The four canonical profile labels RootComp, Coeff, InvDesc, ObsLed are pairwise distinct in the typed basic grammar. In particular, a proof that a boundary is of one canonical type does not by itself prove that it is of another canonical type</data></node><node id="P040-C0068"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Classification is not by naming: The classification theorem does not say that a bridge is useful because it has been named useful. For finite strict useful traces whose boundary lies in the basic grammar, the necessity theorem first extracts a hidden-support/descending-boundary normal form, and the </data></node><node id="P040-C0075"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The quarter-turn does not descend: The update G does not descend through q : G _q. Equivalently, delta^ split _1(q,G)≠ .</data></node><node id="P040-C0077"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Root-composite profile: The four-phase promotion realizes the root-composite normal form G _q, G^2 _q F. Thus the hidden half-step is not a lower-layer operation, while its composite is a lower-layer operation.</data></node><node id="P040-C0086"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert that the hidden quarter-turn itself has become a lower real-shadow state or a real scalar.</data></node><node id="P040-C0088"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not license membership claims about the hidden promoted operation U=G , and the nonclaim register in explicitly records G (Y) , G _q , and that i is not a real-shadow state.</data></node><node id="P040-C0106"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Irreducible non-linear polynomials have no base-field root: For the above p , no element a∈ K satisfies p(a)=0. Consequently, a∈Cand_0(P_ Gal ), delta_ Gal,0 (a)=1.</data></node><node id="P040-C0111"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A promoted root need not descend: For any root alpha∈ L of p , one has alpha K. Thus an individual promoted root is not accepted as lower-layer content.</data></node><node id="P040-C0112"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No base-field evaluation retract for a non-base root: There is no K -algebra homomorphism r:K[alpha]→ K such that r(alpha)=alpha or, more generally, such that r(alpha) is a root of p in K .</data></node><node id="P040-C0120"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The nonclaim register is the explicit refusal to infer alpha∈ K from these descended invariant data.</data></node><node id="P040-C0132"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The formal one-dimensional triangle ledger corollary is deferred to the methods appendix with the other worked graph computations.</data></node><node id="P040-C0137"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cohomology supplies an SAU obstruction certificate: Let Gamma be a finite connected graph with H^1(Gamma;k)≠0 , and let a∈ C^1(Gamma;k) satisfy [a]≠0 . Then the cohomology package gives a Strict Audited Utility certificate for the saturated obstruction instance of the descent-use problem: Coh . More</data></node><node id="P040-C0141"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The six fields are the saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, accepted-answer, and nonclaim components established for the imaginary case in .</data></node><node id="P040-C0143"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The value-only saturation, first-jet strictness, non-descent of , coefficient descent, accepted derivative functional, and nonclaim discipline are developed in .</data></node><node id="P040-C0149"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The four cases share a structural pattern: saturation of a lower problem, strictness of the promoted interface, a non-descending support, an audited descended expression, an accepted lower answer, and a nonclaim discipline.</data></node><node id="P040-C0155"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Profile equivalence does not imply object identity: If C _ Profile C' , it does not follow that the promoted structures U and U' are equal, isomorphic, or members of the same lower theory layer.</data></node><node id="P040-C0158"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Profile equivalence: Two candidate packages C and C' are profile-equivalent, written C _ Profile C', when their substrate erasures are isomorphic as typed SAU skeletons. This means that their six witness obligations have the same role shape, but it does not assert an isomorphism between their underl</data></node><node id="P040-C0163"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Validation of the target requires F_ SAU ( C_t)= , so the target supplies its own saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, solving, audit, and nonclaim witnesses.</data></node><node id="P040-C0168"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU synthesis: usefulness through recurring audited profiles: Within the scope of the results developed above, mathematical usefulness is explained by the recurrence of strict audited descent profiles across theory layers. More precisely, whenever a lower problem family P is SAU-covered by a finite </data></node><node id="P040-C0169"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The outlook separates what these results establish from the questions they leave open. Outlook</data></node><node id="P040-C0170"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What follows from the results: Within finite typed audited promoted-use traces, the paper supplies a profile-level account of strict mathematical usefulness. What this opens for further work is a disciplined way to ask whether a family of lower problems is covered by recurring audited profiles. [Exp</data></node><node id="P040-C0171"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Explanatory compression: Suppose a finite registry R SAU-covers a finite problem family P . The registry provides explanatory compression when many problem instances are certified by fewer recurring profile skeletons. A crude count is _ SAU ( P, R)=| P|-| R|, with the understanding that this number </data></node><node id="P040-C0173"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Portability without collapse: SAU explains why the same mathematical method can appear in different domains without claiming that the domains are literally the same. What recurs is descent profile+audit grammar, not necessarily substrate, ontology, or hidden object identity.</data></node><node id="P040-C0174"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit smuggles the answer Source policy, audit policy, no-smuggling defect, and nonclaim register are explicit Lean does not prove the whole paper Lean wording is disclosed by coverage type: substantive proof language is reserved for direct, computational, or definitional rows</data></node><node id="P040-C0175"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Summary and limits: The results give a theorem-grade normal form for strict mathematical usefulness in finite typed audited promoted-use traces: when a saturated lower problem is solved or certified by essentially using promoted-only structure, the trace forces hidden non-descent: a support that doe</data></node><node id="P040-C0176"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim novelty for the classical constructions themselves, and hidden promoted objects are not asserted as lower-layer content.</data></node><node id="P040-C0180"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Certificate grammar: This subsection records the appendix-bound certificate grammar that is not duplicated in the body. It starts with the role convention behind the six certificate channels, then records the useful-extension and exact-six variants, and closes with the profile-shape definitions used</data></node><node id="P040-C0181"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds role convention: Throughout this paper the six primitive role labels, imported from Foundations I , are used with the following fixed meanings: P_1 = descent/closure/rewrite/transport of content, P_2 = representability/gating/admissibility, P_3 = route mismatch/holonomy/path or protocol co</data></node><node id="P040-C0182"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strictly useful promoted extension: A promotion bridge B_ 0→1 is a strictly useful promoted extension of T_0 for the lower problem P when there exist U,C,a^ such that SAU( P_0,B_ 0→1 ,U,C,a^ ) holds. The accepted claim form is Gamma; T_0 T_1;I_B Useful(B_ 0→1 ,P):accepted. The claim means: the promo</data></node><node id="P040-C0183"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact-six SAU certificate: An SAU certificate is exact-six when, in addition to Definition , the bridge record determines an inherited FATCD -style description D_B whose six role channels all have active projections: Status_i(D_B)=active_projection, pi_i(D_B)\ exists, i=1, ,6. Equivalently, the brid</data></node><node id="P040-C0187"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Obstruction-ledger profile: A bridge has obstruction-ledger profile when lower local data fails to descend globally, but the failure itself becomes a promoted audited object. In a cochain complex, H^n= d_n/imd_ n-1 records the obstruction class, and [a]=0 Longleftrightarrow the desired global descen</data></node><node id="P040-C0190"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU failure modes: A candidate promotion bridge fails to certify strict audited utility if any of the following occur: No saturation: the lower interface already supplies an accepted answer. No strictness: delta_ fact (pi_0,pi_1)= , so the promoted interface factors through the lower one. No promote</data></node><node id="P040-C0194"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Even-dimensional obstruction: Let V be a nonzero finite-dimensional real vector space and let J:V→ V be a real linear map satisfying J^2=-I. Then _ R V is even. In particular, no one-dimensional real vector space carries such a linear operator.</data></node><node id="P040-C0198"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact-six role witnesses for the imaginary-number bridge: The bridge B_ Imag carries role-aligned witnesses for all six Six Birds channels: (W_1,W_2,W_3,W_4,W_5,W_6). Read as an inherited channel description D_ Imag , these witnesses satisfy Status_i(D_ Imag )=active_projection and have active proje</data></node><node id="P040-C0206"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Calculus as coefficient-descent usefulness: The dual-number promotion is strictly useful for first-order differentiation at a point. It supplies an accepted lower-layer answer that the value-only lower interface cannot supply, while claiming downstairs only the audited coefficient [ ]f(x+ )=f'(x), n</data></node><node id="P040-C0208"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Invariant-descent profile: The Galois package has invariant-descent profile: alpha _ T_0 , C(alpha) _ B_ Gal K whenever C(alpha) is one of the declared Galois-invariant audits, such as C_ orb (alpha), Tr_ L/K (alpha), Nm_ L/K (alpha), or any G-fixed expression in L.</data></node><node id="P040-C0219"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete nonclaims ledger and extended tables: This appendix records the scope fence used throughout the paper. The entries below are drafting nonclaims: they do not weaken the theorems, but state what the theorems are not meant to assert. @ &gt; p 0.42 &gt; p 0.50 @ →prule Nonclaim Scope discipline Usefu</data></node><node id="P041-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not derive continuum theories or prove analytic bounds</data></node><node id="P042-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Randomness is often treated as either ontic indeterminacy or epistemic ignorance. We develop a third, operational account within Six Birds: layer-relative randomness is the predictive residue of non-closure under a chosen packaging and budget. For a micro process X_t and a packaging : X Y , we defin</data></node><node id="P042-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim that all randomness is reducible to hidden microstructure, nor do we identify route mismatch with genuine irreversibility.</data></node><node id="P042-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Expected KL form: The micro-closure deficit can be written as CD_tau(Pi) = _ x ∈ X pi(x) D_ KL ( p_x^ (tau) \| p_ Pi(x) ^ (tau) ), where, in the finite case, D_ KL (p\|q) := _ y' ∈ Y p(y') p(y') q(y') , with the convention 0 (0/q)=0 .</data></node><node id="P042-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">tau -closed packaging: A packaging Pi is tau -closed if for every x,x' ∈ X with Pi(x)=Pi(x') , one has p_x^ (tau) = p_ x' ^ (tau) as distributions on Y.</data></node><node id="P042-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure, sufficiency, and vanishing deficit: If Pi is tau -closed, then CD_tau(Pi)=0 . Conversely, if pi(x)&gt;0 for every x ∈ X and CD_tau(Pi)=0 , then Pi is tau -closed.</data></node><node id="P042-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Route mismatch lower bound: If the induced packaged rows coincide with the stationary-conditioned rows, then CD_tau(Pi) 1 2 (RM_tau^ stat (Pi) )^2.</data></node><node id="P042-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Markov Coarse-Graining Benchmark: We begin with the controlled finite-state benchmark in which the exact object CD_tau(Pi) is available by construction. The benchmark consists of four families: exactly lumpable chains , perturbed-lumpable chains with heterogeneity parameter alpha ∈ \ 0,0.2,0.5\ , me</data></node><node id="P042-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Budgeted Prediction: The controlled Markov benchmark established what unresolved closure is. The complementary question is what it costs to buy the missing distinctions back once the current package has already failed to stabilize them. To expose that accounting side directly, we fix the strongest h</data></node><node id="P042-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Auxiliary Clustering Result: This appendix records the representation-learning result that we treat as auxiliary rather than core evidence . The experiment asks whether increasing representational budget, measured by the number of learned clusters k , automatically reduces predictive non-closure whe</data></node><node id="P043-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Many sciences rely on maps of macroscopic behavior—phase diagrams, stability charts, bifurcation sets, and feasibility regions—but emergence itself is rarely treated as a mapping problem. We introduce emergence atlases: subsets and scalar fields on control space indicating where a macro-description </data></node><node id="P043-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Control space and admissible macro-descriptions: A control space U is any set of variables over which macro-law feasibility is to be charted. A control point u ∈ U may include a microscopic law parameter, a packaging or lens, a staging depth tau , a protocol or mechanism variable, and a budget coord</data></node><node id="P043-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Emergence atlas: Fix a scientific predicate Q expressing the additional property one wishes to chart, such as boundedness, closure, geometry-compatibility, sustained directionality, or some other macro-level property. The emergence atlas associated with Q at tolerance delta 0 is A_ Q (delta) = Bigl\</data></node><node id="P043-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Atlas fields: Besides feasible-set atlases, one may chart scalar fields over the same control space. Two generic examples are the closure field CE^ _ Q (u) = ∈f Bigl\ [t] CE(u;Pi,L) : (Pi,L)∈C(u), NT(u;Pi,L), AS(u;Pi,L), Cost(u;Pi,L) B(u), Q(u;Pi,L) Bigr\ , and the budget frontier B^ _ Q (u) = ∈f Bi</data></node><node id="P043-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Effective information as a slice, not a verdict.: The most direct packaging-space support for the paper's comparison thesis is the EI slice itself. Figure plots EI against closure while encoding objecthood and budget. What it shows is exactly the relationship the atlas view predicts: EI is informati</data></node><node id="P043-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the paper does not claim a universal cross-domain cost unit.</data></node><node id="P043-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nor does it treat optional future work as pending evidence.</data></node><node id="P044-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Next-token prediction is not, by itself, a theory of compositional content: finite predictive statistics can agree while the induced quotients compose differently under larger contexts. We study an alternative, non-gradient formulation in which a language model is an audited ledger of finite theory </data></node><node id="P044-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Its primitive objects are typed audited layers, explicit quotient maps, audit and nonclaim records, and promotion gates that determine whether a proposed extension can be claimed . In ML terms, the calculus specifies what counts as evidence, what must be logged, what must remain a nonclaim, and when</data></node><node id="P044-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Notation: closure deficit, idempotence defect, strict-extension certificate: Three runtime objects carry the measurable status of a candidate layer. The closure-deficit statistic CD_ tau is a conditional-information residual statistic at lookahead tau that measures unresolved structure under the cur</data></node><node id="P044-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six-gate promotion panel: Candidate layers enter the LTM ledger through a six-gate promotion panel, not through an unconstrained score threshold. The conjunctive panel is in the spirit of verifier-based and process-supervised pipelines that license outputs only after explicit per-step checks pass . </data></node><node id="P044-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Other relation kinds are deferred to future work and appear as paper-level limitations in</data></node><node id="P044-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Typed gate-failure breakdown: The rejected promotion attempts did not collapse onto a single failure mode. All 20 committed births were closure-saturation-driven, and carrier exactification passed for 1,967 of the 1,968 strict-growth evidence rows, with one row remaining upstream of that relabeling-</data></node><node id="P044-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Halt-verdict architecture.: The halt-verdict classifier reports one of seven typed verdict classes, paired with required nonclaim references, distinguishing accepted, provisional, blocked, outside-scope, failed-audit, absent-with-record, and below-threshold outcomes. The locked run's pass-end produc</data></node><node id="P044-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion and Limitations: The runtime models a frozen-parent regime: parent layers are committed once and not subsequently updated, and bidirectional inter-layer substrate flow is not modelled. Both halves are deferred to future work as a joint pair, because unfreezing parents without bidirectiona</data></node><node id="P044-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Both halves are deferred to future work as a joint pair, because unfreezing parents without bidirectional flow models a half-loop no published characterization covers, with the underlying causation/enablement distinction following</data></node><node id="P044-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the deferred parent-dynamics and bidirectional-flow halves of the framework remain as joint future work, and this paper does not claim either half in isolation.</data></node><node id="P044-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure-package layer: A typed audited layer is a pair Tlayer i =(Xspace i , i ), where Xspace i is a finite microstate space and i :Xspace i →Xspace i is an idempotent self-map, i i = i . The induced equivalence relation on Xspace i identifies two microstates when they lie in the same pre-image of </data></node><node id="P044-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Closure-deficit statistic: Given a layer Tlayer i and a declared lookahead tau , the closure-deficit statistic CD_ tau is the conditional-information residual measuring how much next- tau -token structure on Xspace i remains unresolved by the quotient map i . Schematically, for an observed current m</data></node><node id="P044-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Idempotence/self-consistency defect: Given a layer Tlayer i , lookahead tau , and readout f , the idempotence/self-consistency defect delta_ tau,f is the empirical fixed-point gap measuring deviation from i i = i on observed data through f . A typical estimator is a total-variation or analogous dist</data></node><node id="P045-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We ask not merely whether layer birth occurs, but how many primitive kinds of birth are experimentally accessible. Within the Six-Birds framework, birth events can be organized by activation of packaging ( P5 ), directed circulation ( P6_ drive ), and staging anomaly ( P4 ). We show that these primi</data></node><node id="P045-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim stabilized universality exponents.</data></node><node id="P045-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Section discusses scope, limitations, and what remains open.</data></node><node id="P045-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The paper does not claim that these observables exhaust every possible diagnosis of birth</data></node><node id="P045-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the point is that the confirmed claim belongs specifically to the canonical manual channel, and that this limitation should be carried honestly into the paper.</data></node><node id="P045-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">That is why this paper presents the map as the main synthetic payoff and treats exponent-level universality as future work rather than as a completed result.</data></node><node id="P045-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Discussion: The main result of this paper is not a final exponent-level universality classification. It is a primitive taxonomy and observable separation of layer-birth classes. Within the Six-Birds framework, the experimentally accessible low-complexity births are organized by activation of P5 , P6</data></node><node id="P045-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">That limitation does not weaken the present paper.</data></node><node id="P045-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What remains open is therefore clear. The paper leaves the field with a controlled rubric, a confirmed set of low-complexity classes, and an observable map that future work will have to respect.</data></node><node id="P045-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Their canonical confirmation is therefore restricted to the manual channel, and their non-manual-lens behavior is carried as a quantified caveat. This is a limitation, but it is also a substantive measurement result: staging-anomalous births appear to be empirically more fragile under lens changes t</data></node><node id="P046-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We emphasize that this paper does not establish new theorems about or zero distributions</data></node><node id="P046-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We show, in a self-dual toy family with a positivity constraint, that tightening feasibility can confine zeros to a symmetry locus—a pattern that motivates (but does not prove) ``passivity ledger'' interpretations of zero confinement.</data></node><node id="P046-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">``A mathematics is A theory'': porting the theory-package schema: The framework packages an instantiation as a theory package: a carrier (micro) space, a lens of observation, a completion/packaging rule, and an audit/defect principle . For the mathematics instantiation we use the same shape, with ``</data></node><node id="P046-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Mathematics theory package: A mathematics theory package is a tuple T = (Z,\ f,\ Sigma_f,\ E,\ D) where: Z is a carrier of discrete protocol objects at a chosen stage (e.g.\ grid functions, truncated sums/products, finite compositions, Cauchy sequences of rationals). f:Z→ X is a lens (a coarse descr</data></node><node id="P046-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The passivity toy illustrates a pattern—tightening a positivity constraint can confine zeros to a symmetry locus—but it does not claim generality.</data></node><node id="P047-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We study emergence of macroscopic lawfulness in a minimal stochastic substrate defined on a finite state space with a time-dependent micro kernel P . A closure interaction algebra composed of six primitives is specified by an enable matrix \ 0,1\ ^ 6 6 , yielding up to 36 directed actor informant in</data></node><node id="P047-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work Several follow-on experiments would strengthen both the physics-like narrative and the algebraic/PICA contribution.</data></node><node id="P047-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Future work: Several follow-on experiments would strengthen both the physics-like narrative and the algebraic/PICA contribution. Controlled- tau reruns. Run matched- tau sweeps (or fixed- tau protocols) for Baseline vs Full conditions to separate closure effects from timescale effects. Alternative l</data></node><node id="P048-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Confluence is usually read as order-independence of reduction, while flatness is usually read as path-independence of transport. This paper argues that their local cores coincide once rewriting is equipped with its missing two-dimensional cells. The relevant object is not the bare reduction graph bu</data></node><node id="P048-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Bare graph surrogates are not diagnostic for confluence: The graph-only surrogates above do not diagnose confluence, even in the small terminating finite-ARS regime explored in this paper.</data></node><node id="P048-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reduction 2-complex: For the purposes of this paper, a reduction 2-complex associated to a rewriting system R consists of the following data. A set of 0 -cells, namely the states, words, or terms under consideration. A set of directed 1 -cells, namely the one-step rewrites x → y . A set of generatin</data></node><node id="P048-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Boundary data and fillers: Let sigma be a generating 2 -cell with source x and one-step branches to y_ and y_r . Its boundary data consist of x → y_ and x → y_r. A filler for sigma is a common descendant w together with directed paths y_ →^* w and y_r →^* w. When such a filler exists, we call w a jo</data></node><node id="P048-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Joinability: Two states b,c ∈ X are joinable if there exists some d ∈ X such that b →^* d and c →^* d.</data></node><node id="P048-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Normal forms: A state n ∈ X is a normal form if there is no state m ∈ X such that n → m .</data></node><node id="P048-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Local confluence: The rewriting system (X,→) is locally confluent if for all a,b,c ∈ X , a → b and a → c implies b and c are joinable.</data></node><node id="P048-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reflexive joinability: Every state is joinable with itself.</data></node><node id="P048-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Elementary flatness iff local confluence: A rewriting system is elementarily flat if and only if it is locally confluent.</data></node><node id="P048-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Confluence and normalization: The rewriting system (X,→) is confluent if for all a,b,c ∈ X , a →^* b and a →^* c implies b and c are joinable. It is normalizing if for every a ∈ X there exists some normal form n such that a →^* n.</data></node><node id="P048-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Confluence and normalization give a unique normal form: Assume that (X,→) is confluent and normalizing. Then for every a ∈ X there exists a unique normal form n such that a →^* n .</data></node><node id="P048-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reachable critical pairs capture the observed local obstruction set in the curated string examples: For the curated string examples E005 – E008 , the reachable critical pairs match the graph-extracted local peaks, and their defect signatures agree.</data></node><node id="P048-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The curated left-linear term-rewriting bridge exhibits the same local generator pattern: In the curated left-linear term-rewriting examples TRS002 – TRS004 , reachable critical pairs agree with graph-visible local peaks, and their defect status agrees with the local confluence behavior observed in t</data></node><node id="P048-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Completion as holonomy elimination in the explicit before/after pairs: In the explicit string pair E007 – E008 and the explicit term-rewriting pair TRS003 – TRS004 , the added rule leaves the local generator intact while eliminating its defect. In both cases: the relevant local branching source pers</data></node><node id="P048-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What remains theorem-writing work: What remains is no longer an experimental gap. It is theorem packaging and statement refinement. The first remaining task is the full general theorem package for finite terminating left-linear term rewriting systems in the target form suggested by the paper: a conf</data></node><node id="P048-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The Lean layer therefore secures the paper's proved core while leaving the computational bridge, artifact layer, and full deferred TRS theorem package outside the scope of formalization.</data></node><node id="P049-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A standing question in foundations is whether the deep problems of distinct domains — why the Standard Model has the structure it does, and why quantum mechanics and general relativity fail to combine — share any common explanatory machinery. We report that one frozen, layer-agnostic structural calc</data></node><node id="P049-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SELECT — the machinery picks a structure out of a space, on declared conditions (e.g., the SM gauge algebra, conditional on clean-separation).</data></node><node id="P049-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Non-circularity has a computed control: a product parent satisfying the same bare requirement yields 3/23 , so the value 3/8 genuinely depends on the declared source rather than being baked in (GROUND, conditional on that source).</data></node><node id="P049-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The all-structures theorem upgrade (lemma L60 → L64) remains open and is tracked as such.</data></node><node id="P049-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Summary table: [] @ lll@ →prule [b] 0.28 question [b] 0.31 verdict (grade) [b] 0.34 basis [t] 0.28 exclude 99.3% of chiral gauge structures [t] 0.31 COMPUTE, unconditional [t] 0.34 closure chain on 11 , 990 structures [t] 0.28 select su(2) (3) (1) [t] 0.31 SELECT, on clean separation [t] 0.34 Delta_</data></node><node id="P049-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The fork is conditional on the common-carrier premise — that QM and GR may be modeled as access quotients of one carrier at all.</data></node><node id="P049-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Prediction 1 — Entanglement does not determine geometry (strong it-from-qubit fails): The contested program. A prominent line in quantum gravity (``it-from-qubit'', ER=EPR, entanglement-builds-geometry) holds that bulk spacetime is fully determined by boundary entanglement: given the complete entang</data></node><node id="P049-C0012"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">conditional on the grounded premise of §5.3</data></node><node id="P049-C0013"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Prediction 2 — Gravity does not mediate entanglement (BMV-null): The contested program. The most anticipated near-term probe of quantum gravity is the BMV proposal (Bose et al.; Marletto–Vedral) : place two masses in spatial superpositions, let only gravity couple them, and test whether they become </data></node><node id="P049-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">conditional on the grounded premise of §5.3</data></node><node id="P049-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">frame-transfer-limited — this does not prove the physical proton stable.</data></node><node id="P049-C0017"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">we do not claim the SM and quantum gravity are ``the same physics'', only that one structural grammar describes both and links them — and that the links are falsifiable (Predictions 2–3 are the shared record rung made testable).</data></node><node id="P049-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the product control ( 3/23 ) failing to discriminate [t] 0.05 5 [t] 0.26 N_ gen -blindness, all N [t] 0.22 PROVE-BLIND (theorem) [t] 0.40 an N -sensitive gate in the frozen chain for an even-doublet anomaly-free unit [t] 0.05 6 [t] 0.26 QM–GR fork (no weak-RT ladder) [t] 0.22 COMPUTE (conditional on</data></node><node id="P049-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">row 14 is its exposure to its own program. discussion-and-outlook Discussion and outlook</data></node><node id="P049-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nearest upgrades (precisely-typed open problems): The program's open problems are not vague: each is a named lemma or audit with a stated success criterion. (i) The L60 → L64 lemma — mass-closure charge-linking forbids a second neutral record channel — upgrades Prediction 3 to an all-structures law.</data></node><node id="P049-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">the reviewer independently checked the framework source text and confirmed the subsumption claim is fair (``saying max-flow/min-cut/bit-thread RT instantiates the currency-constraint/shadow-price schema is fair''), with the stated non-claim boundary (recognizes/organizes RT</data></node><node id="P050-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Work on multi-scale organization and audit-based directionality lacks a minimal-substrate theorem: key primitives are often realized only by smuggling in external schedules, ad hoc audits, or declared quotients, making results hard to verify and compare across substrates. We introduce a no-smuggling</data></node><node id="P050-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We are explicit that we do not establish a universal physical substrate or continuum limit: our conclusions are semantic and rest on exact finite-state audits and controlled constructions rather than empirical natural-system data.</data></node><node id="P050-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Substrate (informal): A substrate S consists of: (i) a state carrier Z (finite in our canonical machine model), (ii) a composable dynamics primitive (autonomous in the canonical model), and (iii) an admissible family of deterministic observational maps (lenses) from Z to coarser description spaces. </data></node><node id="P050-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Realization (informal): A realization of a primitive on S is a specification of internal operators and constraints on S that instantiate the primitive without introducing external oracles. Concretely, realizations may be given by endomaps on distributions or predicates, deterministically induced coa</data></node><node id="P050-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No smuggling: A construction smuggles a primitive if it requires information or structure not representable within the substrate state and its admitted operators. Equivalently, a primitive is realized without smuggling if it is definable using only: (i) the substrate state carrier, (ii) the admitted</data></node><node id="P050-C0014"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Packaging-respecting maps preserve packaging equivalence: If h is packaging-respecting, then x _e y implies h(x) _ e' h(y) .</data></node><node id="P050-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit monotonicity: Let A be an audit functional that assigns a nonnegative number to a process observed at a given scale. We say A is monotone under deterministic coarse-graining if for any lens f:Z→ X , the audited value at the macro scale is no larger than at the micro scale: A (process on X ) A </data></node><node id="P050-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Empirical packaging endomap: Given (P,f,tau) and prototypes U_f , define the empirical packaging endomap E_ tau,f :Delta(Z)→Delta(Z) by E_ tau,f (mu) := U_f (Q_f(mu P^tau) ). Equivalently, in matrix form (with Q the Z× X lens matrix and U the X× Z prototype matrix), E_ tau,f = P^tau Q U, mu ↦ mu E_ </data></node><node id="P050-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Markov-chain expansion and finiteness of Sigma_T: Let P be a time-homogeneous Markov kernel on a finite set Z , let rho∈Delta(Z) , and let (Z_0, ,Z_T) P_ rho,T . If P_ Z_ t+1 Z_t &gt;0 whenever (Z_t,Z_ t+1 ) occurs with positive probability (in particular, if P has bidirected support), then Sigma_T(rho</data></node><node id="P050-C0021"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Canonical minimal machine class S_: A substrate belongs to S_ if it consists of: (i) a finite carrier Z , (ii) an autonomous time-homogeneous Markov kernel P on Z , (iii) an admissible family of deterministic lenses f:Z→ X closed under composition (refinement chains), (iv) a family of internal packa</data></node><node id="P050-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Realizability of P1–P6 on S_: Every substrate in S_ admits realizations of primitives P1–P6 without smuggling. More concretely, for any (Z,P) with lenses and induced packaging operators as in , there exist internal operators/constraints that instantiate: dynamics/composition (P1), limited access via</data></node><node id="P050-C0024"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Normal form and compilation up to packaging: Realizability alone does not turn a language into a machine: we also want a notion of normal form and mutual simulation. The appropriate equivalence notion is packaging equivalence induced by idempotent(-like) endomaps ( ). The Lean appendix mechanizes th</data></node><node id="P050-C0025"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Compilation to S_ up to packaging equivalence: Let S be a substrate that realizes P1–P6 without smuggling in the sense of . Assume further that the realized operators are finitary (state and registers can be encoded in a finite carrier) and autonomous once all protocol state is represented. Then the</data></node><node id="P050-C0026"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No-smuggling separations: The following substrate features are necessary (up to equivalent internal encodings) to realize the corresponding primitives without smuggling: [leftmargin=*, itemsep=0.35em] Autonomy requires phase-in-state (P3). If a substrate permits staged protocols but does not represe</data></node><node id="P051-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Time is often treated as a primitive background parameter, and empirical discussions frequently conflate three distinct notions—event ordering, clock measurement, and irreversible directionality—making it difficult to determine when a layer has time versus when time is an artifact of description. Bu</data></node><node id="P051-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not derive physical spacetime here</data></node><node id="P051-C0003"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">P2 asserts that causation is always conditional on feasibility.</data></node><node id="P051-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Theorem-like claims (informal) that we will audit: The rest of the paper treats the claims below as auditable rather than assumed. We state them informally here and test them in a minimal finite-state laboratory.</data></node><node id="P051-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Results I: arrows and clocks: We now audit the first two pillars of time-as-closure: (i) the existence of an arrow (irreversible bookkeeping) and (ii) the existence of viable ticks (repeatable staged measurement). Throughout, the connection to SBT is direct: arrows are primarily accounting coupled t</data></node><node id="P051-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Together, these results reinforce the SBT view that time is conditional on closure and feasibility.</data></node><node id="P051-C0015"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">First, we do not claim that the laboratory's Phi is ``physical time'' Second, we do not claim that entropy production is the unique arrow</data></node><node id="P052-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">When do we say a geometric layer exists? (Coherence schema): A single computed distance matrix is not yet ``geometry.'' Geometry is a claim of coherence under refinement: the packaged points, induced dynamics, and cost structure must stabilize across staging and across a ladder of lenses. We use the</data></node><node id="P052-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For that reason we do not claim an intrinsic geometry independent of packaging</data></node><node id="P052-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We present four exhibits—plane-like (grid), sphere-like (curved regime), Sierpi\'nski (fractal regime), and anisotropic deformation under constraints—and interpret them through the diagnostics introduced in Section . (Curvature as holonomy is deferred to Section .)</data></node><node id="P052-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Importantly, this does not claim Euclidean geometry is fundamental</data></node><node id="P054-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Across disciplines, stable descriptive layers rely on small sets of variables that budget feasibility, accumulate along protocols, or appear as exchange rates between incompatible objectives. We call such variables currencies. This paper gives a layer-relative definition of currency within the Six B</data></node><node id="P054-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim to have formalized every layer transition or every scientific use of a currency.</data></node><node id="P054-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">and it does not prove that every higher-layer shadow price arises from every lower-layer ledger.</data></node><node id="P055-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We present a reproducible computational framework for defining and measuring agency in finite controlled Markov kernels without assuming goals, utilities, or homunculi. The framework operationalizes three complementary metrics: (i) viability kernels (greatest controlled-invariant safe sets under led</data></node><node id="P055-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Theory object). A theory object is an object inside a theory: a packaged macro label (or invariant package) that is stable under the closures induced by T at the relevant horizon. Operationally, low idempotence defect means the macro labels behave like objects under composition; nonempty</data></node><node id="P055-C0006"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Theory object).: A theory object is an object inside a theory: a packaged macro label (or invariant package) that is stable under the closures induced by T at the relevant horizon. Operationally, low idempotence defect means the macro labels behave like objects under composition; nonempt</data></node><node id="P055-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Successor support). We use robust support semantics: the set of possible successors under (s,a) is Post(s,a) := \ s' ∈ S : P[a,s,s']&gt;0 \ . All viability statements below are with respect to Post (not expectations): a move is safe only if every nonzero-probability successor stays safe.</data></node><node id="P055-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Successor support).: We use robust support semantics: the set of possible successors under (s,a) is Post(s,a) := \ s' ∈ S : P[a,s,s']&gt;0 \ . All viability statements below are with respect to Post (not expectations): a move is safe only if every nonzero-probability successor stays safe.</data></node><node id="P055-C0011"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Safe set).: A safety predicate Safe:S→\ true,false\ specifies which states count as viable. We emphasize that ``safe'' can encode more than survival; in later sections we also use safety to encode coherence (e.g., requiring a repaired/noise-free internal bit).</data></node><node id="P055-C0020"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Feasible empowerment). To respect P _2 /P _6 , we restrict the sequence set by ledger feasibility. Given costs c(a) and budget r(s_0) , a sequence alpha is feasible if _ t=0 ^ H-1 c(a_t) r(s_0) . Feasible empowerment is the capacity of the restricted channel: Emp_ feas (s_0;H,f) := Cap (</data></node><node id="P055-C0022"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Macro lens and fibers). Let pi:S→ X be a macro lens (coarser than f in general). For a macro label x∈ X , define its fiber S_x := \ s∈ S : pi(s)=x\ .</data></node><node id="P055-C0023"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Macro lens and fibers).: Let pi:S→ X be a macro lens (coarser than f in general). For a macro label x∈ X , define its fiber S_x := \ s∈ S : pi(s)=x\ .</data></node><node id="P055-C0028"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Agent as a theory object, operational). Fix a theory layer T (kernel + feasibility + ledger), lenses (f,pi) , horizons (H,tau) , and a maintenance policy mu for E . We call the induced package an agent theory object at this scale if (i) the viability kernel K is nonempty for the chosen s</data></node><node id="P055-C0029"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition (Agent as a theory object, operational).: Fix a theory layer T (kernel + feasibility + ledger), lenses (f,pi) , horizons (H,tau) , and a maintenance policy mu for E . We call the induced package an agent theory object at this scale if (i) the viability kernel K is nonempty for the chosen </data></node><node id="P055-C0033"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Result: equality at H=1 , divergence for H 2: Figure shows the median feasible empowerment (bits) as a function of horizon H . At H=1 , the curves coincide. This is expected: a single step cannot exploit protocol holonomy because there is no order-of-composition effect in a one-element sequence. For</data></node><node id="P055-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We do not claim thermodynamic optimality or a specific physical interpretation here</data></node><node id="P055-C0040"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Outlook Three directions follow naturally.</data></node><node id="P055-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Outlook: Three directions follow naturally. First, extend the substrate to multi-agent settings where P _2 constraints and P _4 tokens can encode commitments, ownership, and norms. Second, move from exact kernels to approximate learned models while preserving the audit posture (hashing, manifests, a</data></node><node id="P056-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">We propose that to be real is to have closed: that reality is not a substance, a substrate, or a set, but a ledger of survived closures. A thing is real when it is a package that earned its objecthood through audited gates and pays, continuously, to keep it. The proposal is grounded in the Six Birds</data></node><node id="P056-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Load-bearing chapters end with a paragraph of nonclaims: things the chapter deliberately does not assert.</data></node><node id="P056-C0004"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Enumerating nearly twelve thousand chiral gauge structures token-blind, the closure chain forces a thin skeleton: the Standard Model's gauge algebra (conditional on one separation condition), ^2 _W = 3/8 at the embedding point, exact proton stability.</data></node><node id="P056-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The corpus does not derive physical spacetime, does not resolve the foundations of relativity, and does not claim its constructed times are the physicist's coordinate.</data></node><node id="P056-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">a strict extension is licensed.'' A black hole's interior, read this way, is a nonclaim boundary drawn by nature.</data></node><node id="P056-C0008"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Read philosophically: why is there something rather than nothing is, in this grammar, a well-typed open problem.</data></node><node id="P056-C0009"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What happens to a civilization after the crossing is an open question, the ninth of this book, and we will not pretend to close it.</data></node><node id="P056-C0010"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">One entry remains open in these books, and it will not be closed.</data></node><node id="P057-C0002"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Logic layer: Fix a microstate space Z , a lens f : Z → X , and an evaluation window tau . A logic layer at (f,tau) consists of a nontrivial family B ⊆ Sigma_f of binary packaged predicates together with induced macro-updates such that: Stability. Each b ∈ B is metastable on the timescale tau . Closu</data></node><node id="P057-C0005"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Definition gives four requirements for a logic layer: stability, closure, feasibility semantics, and auditability. This section turns those four requirements into measurable quantities and summarizes the controlled laboratories in which they are evaluated. The important methodological point is that </data></node><node id="P058-C0001"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What makes a mathematical object useful when it does not live in the layer where the problem is posed? Imaginary numbers are not real, infinitesimals are not real scalars, roots of irreducible polynomials need not lie in the base field, and cohomology classes are not vertex potentials. This paper st</data></node><node id="P058-C0007"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Instrument: An instrument is a finite typed record I=(vis_I,Theta_I,BridgePolicy_I,AuditPolicy_I, SourcePolicy_I, N_I), where vis_I partitions content into \ visible,suppressed,outside,unknown\ , Theta_I gives threshold rules, BridgePolicy_I gives admissible bridge types, AuditPolicy_I gives require</data></node><node id="P058-C0016"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audited descending expression: Let B_ 0→1 be a promotion bridge with promoted structure U and expression former C . The expression C(U) descends with audit to a lower answer a^ , written C(U) _ B a^ , when: C(U) has the profile-appropriate descent property, such as qC(U)=F^ q , coefficient extractio</data></node><node id="P058-C0018"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Finite promoted-use trace: A finite promoted-use trace for a lower problem profile P_0 is a tuple T=(D,V,E,r,o,lambda, , B,A_ T , N,v_ ), where: D=(V,E) is a finite directed acyclic graph. An edge u→ v means that the node v depends on the node u . r records the rule, operation, bridge, or audit step</data></node><node id="P058-C0019"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Accepted promoted-use trace: A finite promoted-use trace T is accepted, written AccTrace( T), when all of the following hold: the final answer is a lower accepted answer: a^ _ T ∈Ans_0(P), Eval_ Theta_0 (delta_0(a^ _ T ))=pass; every bridge in B belongs to the declared admissible bridge family; ever</data></node><node id="P058-C0032"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Tautology barrier: The SAU framework is not only the sufficient condition SAU implies Useful. For finite typed audited strict-use traces, the reverse extraction also holds: StrictUsefulTrace implies -shaped certificate. Thus SAU is a normal form for strict audited promoted use, not merely a name for</data></node><node id="P058-C0038"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No reduction to notation: Under the hypotheses of Theorem , the promoted layer is not merely a notational variant of the lower layer at the declared interface.</data></node><node id="P058-C0039"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Nontrivial usefulness: Under the hypotheses of Theorem , the promoted layer supplies an accepted lower answer that the fixed lower interface does not supply.</data></node><node id="P058-C0041"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit failure An overreading, smuggling, provenance, or replay check remains open.</data></node><node id="P058-C0042"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU diagnostic tag set: The diagnostic tag set of a raw package is the finite set Diag_ SAU ( C)⊆ \ accepted,convenient,non_strict,not_hidden, unreturned,irrelevant,failed_audit, smuggled,overread \ assigned by the following rules: accepted∈Diag_ SAU ( C) only when the claim status is accepted, F_ S</data></node><node id="P058-C0043"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Counterfeit SAU patterns: A candidate bridge has one of the following counterfeit profiles when it resembles SAU informally but lacks a required field: Ornamental extension: Strict(B), U _q, but C(U) _B a^ . The promoted layer is richer but unreturned. Mere notation: C(U) _B a^ , but delta_ fact (pi</data></node><node id="P058-C0045"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Acceptance boundary for SAU: A raw package C can be validated as an SAU proof package only if F_ SAU ( C)= . Conversely, if the six witness fields are verified under the declared instruments and F_ SAU ( C)= , then C validates to an SAU proof package: ValSAU( C).</data></node><node id="P058-C0046"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">For the converse, the six verified witness fields supply saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, solving, and audit safety, while F_ SAU ( C)= records that none of the corresponding failure conditions remains open.</data></node><node id="P058-C0050"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Lower-layer literalization claim: A lower-layer literalization claim for a promoted structure U , written Lit_0(U), is a claim that asserts one of the following at the lower layer without an additional admissible bridge for U itself: U∈ T_0 ; U _q ; U is directly visible under I_0 ; the lower layer </data></node><node id="P058-C0052"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Reduced SAU boundary trace: A reduced SAU boundary trace is a normal-form trace B= ( P_0,B_ 0→1 ,U,C,a^ ;A_ B , N ) obtained from a finite strict useful trace by the boundary-normal-form theorem of , such that: U is the hidden support subtrace or generated hidden support of the promoted calculation;</data></node><node id="P058-C0060"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Pairwise sort distinction of the four canonical profiles: The four canonical profile labels RootComp, Coeff, InvDesc, ObsLed are pairwise distinct in the typed basic grammar. In particular, a proof that a boundary is of one canonical type does not by itself prove that it is of another canonical type</data></node><node id="P058-C0063"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Basic classification soundness: Let T be a finite strict useful trace whose reduced boundary is basic. Then there exists a validated SAU certificate whose profile is classified by R_ Basic : StrictUsefulTrace( T) and BasicSAU(Red( T)) implies C, p ValSAU( C) and p∈Gen_ fin ( R_ Basic ). Moreover, Us</data></node><node id="P058-C0064"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Why the classification is non-tautological: The classification theorem does not say that a bridge is useful because it has been named useful. It says that, for finite strict useful traces whose boundary lies in the basic grammar, the necessity theorem first extracts a hidden-support/descending-bound</data></node><node id="P058-C0071"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The quarter-turn does not descend: The update G does not descend through q : G _q. Equivalently, delta^ split _1(q,G)≠ .</data></node><node id="P058-C0073"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Root-composite profile: The four-phase promotion realizes the root-composite normal form G _q, G^2 _q F. Thus the hidden half-step is not a lower-layer operation, while its composite is a lower-layer operation.</data></node><node id="P058-C0074"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cardinal minimality of strict phase promotion: Let q:X Y be a surjective finite shadow map. Suppose there is an update G:X→ X such that G does not descend through q . Then |X|≥ 4. The four-phase carrier C_4 realizes this lower bound while also satisfying G^2 _q F. Consequently C_4 is a cardinal-mini</data></node><node id="P058-C0080"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The remaining fields are the bridge audit, accepted lower output a^ _ Imag , and nonclaim register declared in , read through the certificate format of .</data></node><node id="P058-C0082"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not assert that the hidden quarter-turn itself has become a lower real-shadow state or a real scalar.</data></node><node id="P058-C0084"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">It does not license membership claims about the hidden promoted operation U=G , and the nonclaim register in explicitly records that G (Y) , G _q , and that i is not a real-shadow state.</data></node><node id="P058-C0102"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Irreducible non-linear polynomials have no base-field root: For the above p , no element a∈ K satisfies p(a)=0. Consequently, a∈Cand_0(P_ Gal ), delta_ Gal,0 (a)=1.</data></node><node id="P058-C0107"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">A promoted root need not descend: For any root alpha∈ L of p , one has alpha K. Thus an individual promoted root is not accepted as lower-layer content.</data></node><node id="P058-C0108"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">No base-field evaluation retract for a non-base root: There is no K -algebra homomorphism r:K[alpha]→ K such that r(alpha)=alpha or, more generally, such that r(alpha) is a root of p in K .</data></node><node id="P058-C0116"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The nonclaim register is the explicit refusal to infer alpha∈ K from those descended invariant data.</data></node><node id="P058-C0128"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The formal one-dimensional triangle ledger corollary is deferred to the methods appendix with the other worked graph computations.</data></node><node id="P058-C0133"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Cohomology supplies an SAU obstruction certificate: Let Gamma be a finite connected graph with H^1(Gamma;k)≠0 , and let a∈ C^1(Gamma;k) satisfy [a]≠0 . Then the cohomology package gives a Strict Audited Utility certificate for the saturated obstruction instance of the descent-use problem: Coh . More</data></node><node id="P058-C0137"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The six fields are exactly the saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, accepted-answer, and nonclaim components established for the imaginary case in .</data></node><node id="P058-C0145"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">The four cases share a structural pattern: saturation of a lower problem, strictness of the promoted interface, a non-descending support, an audited descended expression, an accepted lower answer, and a nonclaim discipline.</data></node><node id="P058-C0151"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Profile equivalence does not imply object identity: If C _ Profile C' , it does not follow that the promoted structures U and U' are equal, isomorphic, or members of the same lower theory layer.</data></node><node id="P058-C0154"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Profile equivalence: Two candidate packages C and C' are profile-equivalent, written C _ Profile C', when their substrate erasures are isomorphic as typed SAU skeletons. This means that their six witness obligations have the same role shape, but it does not assert an isomorphism between their underl</data></node><node id="P058-C0159"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Validation of the target plan is the additional condition F_ SAU ( C_t)= , so the target has its own saturation, strictness, non-descent, descent, solving, audit, and nonclaim witnesses.</data></node><node id="P058-C0164"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU synthesis: usefulness through recurring audited profiles: Within the scope of the theorem stack developed in this paper, the usefulness of mathematics is explained by the recurrence of strict audited descent profiles across theory layers. More precisely, whenever a lower problem family P is SAU-</data></node><node id="P058-C0166"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">What the paper unlocks: Within the scope of finite typed audited promoted-use traces, the paper gives a profile-level explanation of strict mathematical usefulness. What this unlocks for further work is not a catalog of future domains, but a disciplined way to ask whether a family of lower problems </data></node><node id="P058-C0167"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Explanatory compression: Suppose a finite registry R SAU-covers a finite problem family P . The registry provides explanatory compression when many problem instances are certified by fewer recurring profile skeletons. A crude count is _ SAU ( P, R)=| P|-| R|, with the understanding that this number </data></node><node id="P058-C0169"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Portability without collapse: SAU explains why the same mathematical method can appear in different domains without claiming that the domains are literally the same. What recurs is descent profile+audit grammar, not necessarily substrate, ontology, or hidden object identity.</data></node><node id="P058-C0170"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Audit smuggles the answer Source policy, audit policy, no-smuggling defect, and nonclaim register are explicit Lean does not prove the whole paper Lean wording is disclosed by coverage type: substantive proof language is reserved for direct, computational, or definitional rows</data></node><node id="P058-C0171"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Contribution statement and limits: The contribution is a theorem-grade normal form for strict mathematical usefulness in finite typed audited promoted-use traces. When a saturated lower problem is solved or certified by essentially using promoted-only structure, the trace forces hidden support that </data></node><node id="P058-C0176"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Certificate grammar: This subsection records the appendix-bound certificate grammar that is not duplicated in the body. It starts with the role convention behind the six certificate channels, then records the useful-extension and exact-six variants, and closes with the profile-shape definitions used</data></node><node id="P058-C0177"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Six Birds role convention: Throughout this paper the six primitive role labels are used with the following fixed meanings: P_1=descent/closure/rewrite/transport of content, P_2=representability/gating/admissibility, P_3=route mismatch/holonomy/path or protocol comparison, P_4=refinement/staging/glui</data></node><node id="P058-C0178"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Strictly useful promoted extension: A promotion bridge B_ 0→1 is a strictly useful promoted extension of T_0 for the lower problem P when there exist U,C,a^ such that SAU( P_0,B_ 0→1 ,U,C,a^ ) holds. The accepted claim form is Gamma; T_0 T_1;I_B Useful(B_ 0→1 ,P):accepted. The claim means: the promo</data></node><node id="P058-C0179"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact-six SAU certificate: An SAU certificate is exact-six when, in addition to Definition , the bridge record determines an inherited FATCD -style description D_B whose six role channels all have active projections: Status_i(D_B)=active_projection, pi_i(D_B)\ exists, i=1, ,6. Equivalently, the brid</data></node><node id="P058-C0183"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Obstruction-ledger profile: A bridge has obstruction-ledger profile when lower local data fails to descend globally, but the failure itself becomes a promoted audited object. In a cochain complex, H^n= d_n/imd_ n-1 records the obstruction class, and [a]=0 Longleftrightarrow the desired global descen</data></node><node id="P058-C0186"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU failure modes: A candidate promotion bridge fails to certify strict audited utility if any of the following occur: No saturation: the lower interface already supplies an accepted answer. No strictness: delta_ fact (pi_0,pi_1)= , so the promoted interface factors through the lower one. No promote</data></node><node id="P058-C0187"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Even-dimensional obstruction: Let V be a nonzero finite-dimensional real vector space and let J:V→ V be a real linear map satisfying J^2=-I. Then _ R V is even. In particular, no one-dimensional real vector space carries such a linear operator.</data></node><node id="P058-C0190"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Exact-six role witnesses for the imaginary-number bridge: The bridge B_ Imag carries role-aligned witnesses for all six Six Birds channels: (W_1,W_2,W_3,W_4,W_5,W_6). Read as an inherited channel description D_ Imag , these witnesses give Status_i(D_ Imag )=active_projection and active projections p</data></node><node id="P058-C0193"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Calculus as coefficient-descent usefulness: The dual-number promotion is strictly useful for first-order differentiation at a point. It supplies an accepted lower-layer answer that the value-only lower interface cannot supply, while claiming downstairs only the audited coefficient [ ]f(x+ )=f''(x), </data></node><node id="P058-C0194"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Invariant-descent profile: The Galois package has invariant-descent profile: alpha _ T_0 , C(alpha) _ B_ Gal K whenever C(alpha) is one of the declared Galois-invariant audits, such as C_ orb (alpha), Tr_ L/K (alpha), Nm_ L/K (alpha), or any G-fixed expression in L.</data></node><node id="P058-C0200"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">SAU_Synthesis_Transfer_Profile Profile abstraction, transfer, synthesis, and scoped outlook claims.</data></node><node id="P058-C0202"><data key="k0">prior_claim</data><data key="k1">Complete nonclaims ledger and extended tables: This appendix records the scope fence used throughout the paper. The entries below are drafting nonclaims: they do not weaken the theorems, but state what the theorems are not meant to assert. @ &gt; p 0.42 &gt; p 0.50 @ →prule Nonclaim Scope discipline Not a</data></node><node id="RL01"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not redefine P1–P6, closure/objecthood, the F/E/G laws, or the eight existing no-gos as VII novelties.</data></node><node id="RL02"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer shared access from common source, carrier, ancestry, vocabulary, or instrument.</data></node><node id="RL03"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer witnessed contact from interface compatibility.</data></node><node id="RL04"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer a join from contact.</data></node><node id="RL05"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer strict join from product, common refinement, co-presentation, or relabeling.</data></node><node id="RL06"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer objecthood from strictness/nonfactorization.</data></node><node id="RL07"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer directionality from strictness, contact, order sensitivity, or holonomy.</data></node><node id="RL08"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer occurrence from soundness, admissibility, executability, or reachability.</data></node><node id="RL09"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer reachability from rule text or mathematical soundness.</data></node><node id="RL10"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not equate enablement with descent, containment, sufficiency, causation, or endogeny.</data></node><node id="RL11"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not label theorist-triggered or hidden-observer execution endogenous.</data></node><node id="RL12"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not infer top-down creation of lower facts from structural selection or constraint.</data></node><node id="RL13"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not relabel bridged records native or erase source lineage.</data></node><node id="RL14"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not apply total-lens results to partial self-owned domains without a bridge theorem.</data></node><node id="RL15"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not credit prospective commitment to a predicate fixed after observing the target evidence.</data></node><node id="RL16"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim zero-cost interaction while omitting observer/instrument occupancy.</data></node><node id="RL17"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not universalize a finite, pointwise, horizon-limited, or open-family negative.</data></node><node id="RL18"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not call no evidenced contact certified non-interaction without coverage and detector-power witnesses.</data></node><node id="RL19"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim a complete taxonomy of admission, contact, joins, or obstruction without a completeness proof.</data></node><node id="RL20"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim necessary-and-sufficient join conditions unless both directions are proved on the declared carrier.</data></node><node id="RL21"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim categorical reduction before defining the category and universal property.</data></node><node id="RL22"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim a full generators-and-relations algebra before primitive operations and equivalences are fixed.</data></node><node id="RL23"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not posit a conserved contact degree or one scalar interaction currency without an invariance theorem.</data></node><node id="RL24"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not treat Two-Theory World success as a universal proof.</data></node><node id="RL25"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not alter imported Foundations V/VI Lean sources; use VII-owned wrappers and exact bridge records.</data></node><node id="RL26"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not claim fresh Lean kernel verification when Lean 4.28.0 has not been replayed locally.</data></node><node id="RL27"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not reconstruct P039 body theorems from its abstract; eighteen includes and the bibliography are missing.</data></node><node id="RL28"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not count P040 and P058 as independent evidence or choose a canonical version without a later ruling.</data></node><node id="RL29"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not silently erase failed bridges, retractions, refunds, or negative runs from append-only ledgers.</data></node><node id="RL30"><data key="k0">red_line</data><data key="k1">Do not present this readiness dossier as a Foundations VII proof or paper draft.</data></node><node id="SixBirds.AdmissibleClaim"><data key="k0">prior_lean_declaration</data><data key="k1">SixBirds.AdmissibleClaim</data></node>